      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>WiTrt the average playwright it is all work and sio play. A TALE well told is twice told, but a tale twice told becomes monotonous. _ .</p>
        <p>_ OF all sorts of embarrassment, that of riches is the most easily overcome. THE man who always looks before he leaps, never leaps. KEEP your troubles to yourself; when you tell them you are taking up the time of the man who is. waiting to tell you his. IF we could live our lives again, we would no doubt avoid a great many of our mistakes and replace them by an entirely different assortment. IT is a slow baby that does not know which of its relatives is most easily imposed upon.</p>
        <p>GETTING THE BETTER OF ROBERT.—One Of the better order of " bikists " had ventured on the footpath for a short distance-L-this was in Surrey —to avoid a newly-macadamised bit of road. Up popped ,a man in blue. "Your name and address, please.' Cyclist gave card containing his right name and address. Constable (reading) : "'S. J. Smith, 2, Raspberry Crescent, 'Arrow-on-the-U.' Go on with you I want your proper name and address; we've too many Smiths about here, or I'll run you in." Cyclist (desperately) : "Then, if you must have it, it's William Shakespeare, Stratford-on-Avon." constable: "Thank you, sir; sorry to have troublsd you." (Proceeds to -enter the name and address carefully in his book.) Mits. Trott; "Oh, I only went into town on a shopping expedition 1 " Mrs. Streete : "Did you? 'Tell me all about it. Did you have a real good time ? " Mrs. Trott, "Not at all. I found what I wanted in the very first place I went into." Mrs. Streete : "How provoking!" _</p>
        <p>FAIR MAIDEN (at the football game, during an .exciting melee): "Oh, look there—can't Jack hug just beautifully!" SUGGESTED A SOLUTION.—An ostentatious mem- ber of a certain County Council, whose father is well-known as a retired omnibus-driver, was one -day displaying a large seal he usually wears, repre- senting St. George and the Dragon, and while several bystanders were expressing their axlmira tion of it, its owner remarked, in solemn tones— " Aw' one of my ancestors is—aw—supposed to ,have killed the dwagon—aw—don't you know." "Dear me," inquired one of his hearers, who knew something about him, "did he run over it?" ADAM'S NURSE.—A Sunday-school superinten- -dent at the close of an address on the Creation, which he felt sure he had kept within the compre- hension of the least scholars, smilingly invited questions. A tiny boy with a white, eager face and large brow, at once held up his hand. "Please, sir, why was Adam never a baby ?" The superintendent coughed in some doubt as to what answer to give, but a little girl of nine, the eldest of several brothers and sisters; came promptly to his aid. "Please, sir," she said, smartly, there was nobody to nuss him!" CHOLLY Fiveclock ; " Yaas, I twied to learn to play golf last summah, don't y' know, but I gave it up when I got struck on the head with the ball and knocked silly." Miss Quickwit : "How sad! And can the doctors do nothing for you: " NOT LIKE THAT.—Here is a story illustrative .of the prosaic nature on which art makes no im- pression :—ln Westminster Abbey there is a large marble tablet in memory of a famous bishop. It is a basrelief, representing the bishop—a portrait— in the agony of death, sinking into the arms of an allegorical female figure, presumably intended for the Angel of Death. It is said that an aged couple from the country were being shown round the Abbey, and• that, pausing long before the tablet, the old lady remarked to her husband—" That's a _good likeness of the bishop, but" (regarding the angelic personage attentively) "it's a very poor one of Mrs. . I knew her well, and she didn't look like that!"</p>
        <p>SMART.—A north-countryman went to a solicitor, laid before him a case in dispute, and then asked him if he would undertake to win the suit. "Certainly," replied the solicitor, "1 will readily undestake the case. We are sure to win !" "So you really think it is a good case ? " "Un- doubtedly, my dear sir. I am prepared to guarantee you will secure a verdict in your favour." "Very good, sir; I'm much obliged to you, but I don't think I'll go in for law this time; for, you see, the case I have laid before you is my opponent's 1" WILLING TO EXCHANGE.—The pastor bade her proceed.—" Tell me all," he urged, kindly, "I put a button in the contribution box"' she faltered. He smiled. "And did your conscience trouble you ? " he asked. The woman raised her eyes earnestly. "No," she answered. "I put in the wrong button and broke a set, and I would like to exchange it, if you please." - -</p>
        <p>_ AT LAST.—The stout party with the eye-glasses gazed with rapture at the rolling, dark-blue waves. At last," he said, "the dream of my life is realised? I have the glories of the mighty deep before my eyes, the moisture of the salt breeze for the first time on my cheek ! " Here he wept. The careless loungers of the sandy shore, reeognising the celebrated writer of sea stories, respected his emotion, and stood silent, nor jarred upon his joy with their idle babble. DIFFERENT.—They were talking about how they happened to marry. "I married my wife," said one, after the others had all had their say; "be- cause she was different from any woman I had ever met." "How was that ? " chorused the others. "She was the only woman I ever met who would have me." PRECEPT AND EXAMPLE. Scene: A Sunday School. Johnnie: "I say, Miss Jones, I know now why you did'nt want me to rob birds' nests last spring." Miss Jones (with an oriole in her bat) : Why was it Johnnie?" Johnnie (gazing with admiring eyes at the hat) "'Cause you wanted the birds to grow big enough to wear 'em."</p>
        <p>- A CRISIS.—Under the electric lights the whole room was seen to be .in a state of wild disorder. Articles of vertu were strewn over the carpet, mingled with many toilet accessories. Bureau drawers and cupboards were pulled open and their contents hung out. The furniture was dragged out of place and upset, ancrrugs lay in crumpled heaps. In the midst of this terrible disarray two human beings confronted eath other; one a woman, the other a mocking, jeering fiend whom the law called her husband. He eyes had a strange, hunted look; her nervous hands pressed hard on her feverish brow. She strove for speech, but it was denied her, while the man still laughed his cruel, triumph- ant laugh. At last, with a stifled groan, she fell swooning on the floor, irrevocably disarranging her stiff shirt-front. She was a new woman, she had lost 1-cr collar-button, and she did not know how</p>
        <p>TO PERSRRVF, YOUR HAIR m:e common sense: don't riin it by sousing it with cold water or etarve it by neglect: all living things reqnire nourishment. and pnre grease Is nu- •donbtedly the bet nourisher of the hair for more than a century. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL has Lean considsred the best preserver of the hair ever Used it prevents it falling off or turning grey, removes rcurf and promotes a luxuriant growth : for children It is un3urpaessd :also in a golden colour for fair hair Bottles 3s 6d., 75.. lOs 6d. (eqt:o.l to 4 small). itowiand's Macassar Oil.—Nourishes the hair. Rowland's Macassar oil.—Prev'nts baldness. Ask anywhere for Rowland's Macassar Oil. of O. Hatton garden, London, and avoid cheap, worthless imitations.</p>
        <p>To MoTnmtg.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poon sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it producer natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain. and the little cherub awakes "as bright las a button." Of all chemists, Is. 4-d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
