      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"CAPITAL" 011A1.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" 011A1.</p>
        <p>In connection with the reported success of Dr.l Nansen's Polur expedition, it must be remembered that the North mainetic Pole, which differs in position by many degrees from the geographical pole, was discovered by James Ross, nephew of Captain John Ross, on June 1, 1831. Excluding minor ventures, some eighty expeditions have set out, under the control of various nations,to explore the Arctic regions, dat'ng from the days of Sabas- tian Cabot to the present time. The earliest defi- nite information that we possess concerning the northern regions comes, curiously enough, from an Irish monk named Discuil, who writing about A.D. 825, says that he had information from brethren who had been at Thule during several months, and they reported that there was no darkness at the summer solstice.</p>
        <p>Great relief is experienced in many quarters at the indication which Mr. John Morley has given in a speech at Montrose that he is in favour of a scheme of flows Rule all round. There are many leading Liberals who regard this as the beat solu- tion of the Home Rule question, and the fact that Mr. Morley is now regarded as favourable has given immense impetus to this view. The Scotch Home Ru:ers are so satisfied with Mr. Morley's declara- tions that they intend to give him the benefit of their support.</p>
        <p>Arrangements are already in progress for the re- ception of the American Congregationalists who in- tend in the coming summer to make a pilgrimage to ngland. They are to land at Plymouth early in June, and, including a week's visit to Holland, they will be on this side of the Atlantic for fully a month. The cathedrals of the mother country are to be a matter of special study with the pilgrims, and to that end the utmost facilities will be afforded them.</p>
        <p>It is said that great offence has been caused in Court circles in Berlin by anonymous and abusive letters addressed to the Emperor from England. The people of this country have no doubt been united in resenting German interference in the Transvaal; but that is no reason why the vulgar ruffianism which takes refuge in anonymous letters should disgrace our name abroad. 1</p>
        <p>Mr. Rawson Shaw's desire, shown in the House of Commons on Tuesday night, that the Secretary should issue sterner edicts against the reckless cyclists who imperil the lives of inoffensive citizens had a practical interest for those who knew that a member of the House—Mr. Round—has been dis- abled by one of the wild wheelmen. Perhaps if it had been a Minister, or if Mr. Balfour did not im- peril himself as well as the lives of others, the Home Secretary would have given a more sym- pathetic answer. Mr. Shaw's suggestion was cer- tainly se mild as to provide an easy loophole. He only asked the police to be instructed "to use every endeavour to ascertain the correct name and address of those who cause these accidents, so that they may at any rate be obliged to explain their conduct if called upon." This certainly does not advance matters much.</p>
        <p>Sir Wilfrid Lawson and his friends are making very special efforts to secure a good division Pri the second reading of the Scotch Local Veto Bill, which is down for second reading on the 29th inst. It is claimed that the temperance question has a much firmer hold in Scotland than in England, arid that a number of Conservatives will vote in favour of the Scotch Bill. rills remains to be seen.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board have fired Monday the 9th of March as the day for the election of parish councils for the coming year. All the mem- bers of the 6,000 parish councils in England and Wales retire simultaneously, and not a third at a time, as is the case with some other local bodies, and the newly elected members will come into office on the 15th of April. The Board have issued .a code of rules under which the elections will be held, and these are substantially the same as the rules which regulated the first elections in Decem- ber, 1894, but with such modifications as experince has suggested. The rules ought to have been out some months ego, instead of being deferred to within three weeks of the elections. I am informed that they'would probably have been later still but for a member of the House of Commons having put a question on the paper and thus sent a bomb among the officials in Whitehall. Other rules relating to the elections of guardians and rural and urban dis- trict councillors will follow within a day or so. In one respect the new rules differ from those of Sir Henry Fowler. for they provide that where there is an equality of votes and the returning officer is not qualified to give a casting vote, he is to decide by drawing lots which candidate is to be elected.</p>
        <p>Apropos of the sixty-fifth birthday of Lord Salis- bury, and of Her Majesty the Queen attaining her seventy-seventh year this spring, it is curious to note how many personages of advanced age at the present moment rule the destinies of Europe or take part in their councils. In Germany we have Prince Hohenlohe, Premier at the age of seventy- seven, aided by Prince Bismarck, who is now in his eighty-first year.. In Russia, Prince Lobanoff guides the foreign policy of that country, the senior of Lord Sali.43ury by two years. In Austria, Count Kalnoky has just retired at the age of seventy-two, his successor, Count. Goluchowski, being sixty-five. Crispi, the-Italian Premier, is seventy-seven, and so forth.</p>
        <p>The "Kaffir Circus" iq about to lose one of its brightest ornaments. The great" Barney " Barnato has booked a passage to the Cape by the Tan tallon Castle, which sails on Saturday. The announcement will doubtless be received with interest by those associated with his numerous ventures, including the famous " Bank." In connection with this it may be mentioned that the Harlech Castle is due about the end of the week at Plymouth. On board is a contingent ofJameson's followers in his now histori- cal raid.</p>
        <p>The poet Cowper hated sales, and in his " task " he wrote:— Estates are landscapes, ffazed upon a while, Then advertised and auctioneered away. Yet, by a strange irony of fate, this is just what has happened TO his beautiful garden at Olney,in Buck- inghamshire, which contains the summer-house in which the poet wrote many of his works. But the competition for the poet's garden does not appear to have been very keen; and the tenant, who has the place at £l6 per annum, eventually secured the " lot " for £4BO.</p>
        <p>The Princess Yourieff,* v. who was one of the prize-winners in the battle of flowers at. Nice on Sunday. is the morganatic widow of the Tsar Alexander 11., by whom she had several children. One of the main causes of the estrangement which subsisted for several years between the " Tsar- Emancipator" and his elde,t son, who became the " Tsar-Pence keeper," was the introduction of the Princess Yourieff sky into the family circle of the Winter Palace. It was to her that, Alexander 11. finally announced his intention to grant a constitu- tion to the Russian people on the very day that he drove forth to meet his doom at the hands of the Nihilists. During the reign of his successor the Princess spent most of her time abroad, but that she is now viewed with more indulgence by the present Tsar may be inferred from the fart, that she was lately visltA at Nice bv the invalid Tsarevich. She invariably spends the winter on the Riviera, and lives in great style, having been richly pro- vided for by Alexander 11.</p>
        <p>Freedom "broadens down" rather slowly in Germany. According to a letter from Berlin, English residents have found it specially necessary of late to be guarded in their remarks about politi- cal events when in public places. The writer states that to his knowledge one Englishman was haled off to the police-col:art for an alleged indiscreet reference to the Emperor,uttered while about to sit down to dinner, and had to pay a fine of ten marks by way of hors d' euvre. A good story is being told in the English clubs. An Englishman was taxed by a German police-officer with publicly using an uncomplimentary epithet with regard to the Emperor. "You are quite mistaken, I assure you," said the Englishman ; "I was talking of the Emperor of China!" " Nein ! Des geht nicht, !" retorted the official ; " there is only one Emperor who is—well, who does such things !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 22, 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 22, 1896</p>
        <p>a weakness for loading the women of his family with necklets, usually of pearls, and Queen Margherita rarely appears at any function of importance without superb strings,or, as Lord Beaconsfield would have called them, " ropes " of the beautiful gems. Every year the Queen's necklace increases in value,as the King adds a row to it annually. so that the chains now reach to Her Majesty's waist. There is no member in the Royal Family in Italy who can compete for one instant with the regal magnifi- cance of the jewellery possessed by Queen Margherita, who is one of the best dressed women in Europe and,un- like the Princess of Wales, inclined to err on the side of extravagance. The young Queen of the Netherlands is to be married, for dynastic reasons, towards the end of next year, shortly after she has celebrated her seventeenth birth- day. A paragraph has been going round the Papers which stated that troubles are likely to arise in Europe in consequence of the Emperor William's wish that his eldest son should marry Queen Withelmina, but this is pure fiction. The Kaiser really wishes the Queen to marry Princess Frederick Henry of Prussia, who is in his twenty-second year, and the eldest son of Prince Albert, the Prince Regent of Brunswick. It is probable that Queen Wilhelmina would have married Prince Carl of Denmark but for his betrothal to Princess Maud of 1 Wales. By the way, speaking of Princess Carl, reminds ' me that his mether, the Crown Princess of Denmark is the tallest Royal lady in Europe; being over 6ft. 2in. She is very rich, having inherited five millions from her grandfather Prince Frederick of the Netherlands. Although we have so far experienced an exceptionally mild winter, we must remember that we are by no means out of the wood yet, and in spite of bright spring-like days we shall do well to continue to don our warm winter garments until March is numbered among the past months of this particular year. Of course I know how desperately hard it is to do so when new, fascinating, spring modes meet us at every turn, still in the wearing of many of these new spring modes, lurks danger. The coldest March experienced over Eneland during the whole of the past twenty-five years occurred at the close of a winter which was quite as mild, if not milder, than the present season. This was in 1883 when northerly winds and snow showers were experienced throughout nearly the whole month, the thermometer in London sinking at least five degrees below freezing-point on no fewer than ten nights. In three years out of the' last twenty-five, viz.. in 1877, 1883, and 1889, the lowest temperature of the whcle year occurred in London in the month of March. There is no doubt that the mild winter has had a most favourable result on trade. In the Trade Unions there are only a little over half as many out of work as was the case this time last year. All Sociological experts declared that if the winter was comparatively mild, and the influenza kept off, this would be one of prosperity, and for once the experts bave been right. Few of us can remember a season when fur was more attractively placed before us than the present one; and rarely indeed have we needed it less, still, those of us who indulged, not wisely but too well, in paltry adornment have contrived to stick gallantly to our guns, averring the while that damp and fog were all sufficient to demand its adoption. If it were not for Niagara we should scarcely have a wintry illusion left, but an hour or two spent at this icy but cheerful rendezvous helps one to keep an at- mospheric balance. I noticed the other afternoon that on all the smartest gowns a liberal supply of braid was employed, either in plain rows, or in the elaborate scroll designs beloved of the tailor—one particular gown worn by a pretty girl, whose skating left nothing to be desired was in a violet cloth, the seams of cloth coat and skirt being piped with black satin. The short undulating basque most obligingly displayed the white satin coat lining, and a dainty little toque of blac k velvet, violets and lilies-of-the-valley, completed one of the dain- tiest costumes I have seen this year. There is no doubt that all the seams of our much gored skirts are to be out- lined in Some way. They will either be adorned with braid, or piped with satin, while ball and dinner gowns will have the seams outlined with tiny quillings of chiffon, flowers of the small Banksia rose type, or sequin and bead passementerie. The very latest cut of skirt boasts not the smallest vestige of fulness at the waist at the back, the gores there being as smartly sloped off as in front, but at the hem these skirts stand out well,both at the sides and back. lam hearing every day that the tight fitting sleeve will shortly reign supreme, but at the present moment very little change is noticeable. However, I would warn my readers to be wary and wait a little ere they indulge in smart spring frocks, as in all probability another three or four weeks will see a decided alteration in the balloon arrangements now en evid- ence. Rumours are afloat to the effect that the dress-improver (?) is coming back to us! The news hails from America and one described appears to be a formidable monster. It has pleats like an organ, and is twenty-seven inches long. It consists of five hollow tubes of striped English horsehair, and possesses the happy faculty of springing back into share after any amount of crushing. Some time back I mentioned the fact that spinning was the latest up-to-date accomplishment, now the cor- rect thing is to preserve the clippings of our live, or the entire hair of our deceased canine pets, and weave them into something to wear. I hear upon excellent authority that one sentimental yet practical lady of title,has spun the hair of her defunct terrier (it must" have been a Skye surely) into a Tam o' Shanter. A good hairy Pomeran- ian doe, by the way, might yield quite a respectable shearing. Some really remarkable prices have been realised for musical enpyrighte sold quite recently : "In the Gloam- ine." by Lady Arthur Hill. has been disposed of for £94 12e. ; " Elfin Knight." cantata by Gibsone, £53 ; "Sea Maidens." hy J. S. Roeckel. £lO7 16s. ; " West- ward An," £126 54. ; while the copyright of "Fairy Barque," an easy and taking piece by Smallwood, has been Fold at the record price of £l,BlO. Opening oysters is a somewhat dangerons! task to the uninitiated, and many of my readers will doubtless be glad to know that an easy method whereby to accom- plish this particular feat is to place the oysters on a hot stove for a few seconds when they will open, and the knife can then be inserted without the slightest trouble. In these go ahead days premature wrinkles are very prevalent, and these great despoilers of beauty are evi- dently troubling several of my correspondents at the present moment. They arise from a variety of causes. and nervous or sick headaches, worries, excess of emotion,whether of laughter or tears,will produce them. Gentle massage, with an application of thick emulsion will do much toward assisting to remove these Prema- ture wrinkles. Here is the recipe for an excellent emul- sion. Take two ounces of cucumber juice, two ounces of narhnnne honey, one ounce of white wax, and three drachms of rose water. Melt the wax with a gentle heat and add the other ingredients, mixine all well to- gether. This should he rubbed gently along the lines, if across the forehead from side to side of the face, ansl not from nose to forehead. This detail must not be for- gotten, as it is very important. PINEAPPLE PIE is a delicious luncheon or supper dish. Take two cupfuls of finely-chopped pineapple (tinned will do) two tablespoonfuls of spongecake crumbs, half a cup of water, sugar to taste, three eggs and a teaspoon- ful of the pineapple juice. Mix together, the pineapple. crumbs, and a good teaspoonful of sugar, the water and two well-beaten yolks of eggs. Line a pie dish with crust, put in the mixture and cook until the pastry is well dried and browned. Whisk the whites of egg to a etiff froth, add two tablespoonfuls of castor suear. When the pie is cooked, and whilst it is still hot, cover Ithe ten with the froth, and replace it in the oven for the egg just to set.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>The expert of oranges from Jamacia is increasin, ranidly. The trees which yield the bulk of the fruit are self-g,rown seedlinps sprinpinp up in cattle pastures, or in the npiphhourhood of coffee fields ; and they receive little or no, cultivation. The first Scotch newspaper of which we have a record is the " Mercurius Scoticus," which appeared on Aug. 5, 1651. and disappeared apain in November of the fol- lowing year. It was a weekly paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DZAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I am told on excellent authority that the shore- basqued coats are to continue to usurp all out affections. Then an equally excellent authority whispers to me that the short bolero is to have a further run of popularity; while again have I been informed that the picturesque coat of the Louis XVI. period is at last to receive that recognition which its charms demand. lam certain that the spring coats will mostly be sac-backed, and that the sleeves of these will be considerably smaller, but not really tight, still preserving a fulness on the top, this fulness, however, not to droop to the elbow, nor to set up to the ears, but to exhibit a moderation of demeanour pre-eminently praise- worthy. Besides the straight-backed coat, the coat which hangs in pleats from a yoke will also be recognised, and then, again, many will continue to recognise the becoming advantages of the short tailor-made coat, which fits tightly at the back, is semi-fitting in the front, and lends itself so amiably to wai.stcoo.ts of diverse detail.</p>
        <p>An excellent coat and skirt costume is made of Scotch heather homespun, the coat having a smart, straight front, fastened with dyed horn buttons. The square collar is simply stitched round. Six yards of the widest homespun is ample for this, or for a short figure from five to five and a•half will be sufficient, while if a thick homespun is used, neither the skirt nor coat need be lined, the former being simply hemmed up with mackintosh. There is no prettier or more becoming head- gear for a child than a " Liberty " hat which, though expensive to buy, can well be made at home by the exercise of a little patience, and at a comparatively small outlay. " Liberty " hat</p>
        <p>should be ma'ae of a fairly good pongee silk; the commoner qualities are narrow and require more joins in brim , and crown. The silk should be about 22 inches wide, and of this 2,1 yards will be required for the hat, but, of course, the age of child and size of hat preferred will alter this. For quite a young child a pair of strings may be added; these are not necessary for older girls.</p>
        <p>Speaking of hats reminds me that a smart speci- men of what is worn by fashionable women is of highly-glazed coarse straw, shot deep blue and green; the rather low crown is encircled by big rosettes of green gauze, with a cluster of shaded blue-green wings at the side, while underneath the upturned brim at the back are placed three smaller rosettes of blue gauze. This hat is an exception to the rule of floral trimmings, but is decidedly stylish, odd as is the blending of the colours. Another pretty hat has a wide, slightly bell-shaped crown of black and white straw, and a broad brim of plain black, on the front of which a half-wreath of cowslips lies. Large rosettes of kilted black tulle are placed closely round the crown with a cluster of flowers, and a tall black and white aigrette at the aide, and more cowslips are arranged beneath the brim at the back.</p>
        <p>A really excellent tea-gown which I have in my mind's eye has a low square bodice with double short puffed sleeves. It is embroidered with beaded Indian palms. Choux bows secure the kerchief folds on the shoulders, whilst vertical rows of gagings shape them at the back into a deep scallop. Corselet with tapering chatelaine pen- dants in silk gimp, in keeping with the delicate tints of the skirt.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
