      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Thursday last, being the anniversary of the birthday of the Duchess of Fife, doubtless found Her Royal High- ness the recipient of many presents, for although she is one of the most reserved amongst the members of the Royal Family, she is one of the most popular. The Duchess particularly dislikes the official ceremonies of the Court, such as Drawing Rooms and State Concerts, and does not care for great festivities of any kin&amp; She is extremely fond of walking and thoroughly enjoys a shopping expedition without a carriage or attendant,and as she invariably wears a thick veil, her identity is sel- dom revealed. Her Royal Highness is never so happy as when she is in the nursery with her two little girls, and indeed it may be truly said that she inherits all the home instincts of her mother, the Princess of Wales. The Duchess of Fife, like her sisters, Princesses Victoria and Maud, is a good needlewoman. Garments of a useful nature form her favourite work, though her embroidery is very beautiful. She is a first-rate whip and drives tandem splendidly. The Princess Letitia, from whom -a necklace of the value of £4,000, the gift of King Humbert, has been stolen, is the widow of the ill-fated Prince Amadeus,and sister-in-law of the Italian monarch. King Humbert has</p>
