      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY FRAUDS.</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY FRAUDS.</p>
        <p>The ease with which the public will, if unwarned,</p>
        <p>allow Itself to be befrauded, is the stronghold of evil doers.</p>
        <p>A timely warning is—offered to the public and the trade in the " British and Colonial Druvist " last week by the manufacture of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, a remedy which, owing to the many remarkable cures which it has effected,is largely sought by the pub- lic. The extraordinary feature of the case is the fact that the public should occasionally allow itself to be imposed upon by imitations of this remedy, sold under various misleading names. In the recent case, the sellerf—a chemist in Bourne- mouth, was compelled to sign and publish the following apology, a warning to all traders : " BounsEmotmr, 20th January, 1896.</p>
        <p>"I, JOAN VINCENT, of Bournemouth, Chemist, DO HEREBY APOLOGISE for having infringed upon your rights by selling two boxes of Pink Pills which were not of your manufacture, as and for your celebrated Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and in consideration of you foregoing your right to an Injunction, de hereby undertake not to infringe again upon your rights in any manner whatsoever. (Signed) JOHN VINCENT." There need be no danger of being imposed upon if cus- tomers will insist on the full title " Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People." Illegal imitations are sometimes, bearing a portion of the true title. They should be al- ways refused,as they cannot be genuine and may be dangerous, and an act of public usefulness will be per- formed if the facts are communicated to the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.G., from whom the genuine Phis may be ob- tained direct, in case of doubt, at 2s. 9d. per box, or 13s; 9d. for six boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>OR, CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. By MILS. E. D. E. N. SOUTHWORTH.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXV. CAP'S TRICKS AND PEAItS. .` I'll be merry and free, I'll be sad for naebody • Naebody cares for me, I cares for naebody."</p>
        <p>-B URNS.</p>
        <p>The next day, according to agreement, the pastor came and dined at Hurricane Hall. Daring the dinner he had ample opportunity of observing Capitols,. In the afternoon Major Warfield took an occasion of leaving him alone with the contumacious young object of his visit. Cap, with her quick perception, instantly dis- covered the drift and purpose of this action, which Immediately provoked all the mischievous propen- sities of her elfish spirit. "Uncle means that I shall be lectured by the good parson: if he preaches to me, won't I humour him to the top of his bent V—that's all 1" was her secret resolution, as she sat demurely, with pursed- up lips, bending over her needle-work. The honest and well-meaning old country clergy- man hitched his chair a little nearer to the perverse young rebel, and gignerly—f or he was half afraid of his questionable subject—entered into conversa- tion with her.</p>
        <p>_ To his surprise and pleasure, Capitola replied with the decorum of a young nun. Encouraged by her manner, the good minister went on to say how much interested he felt in her welfare; how deeply he compassionated her lot in never having possessed the advantage of a mother's teaching; how anxious he was by his counsels to make up to her as much as possible such a deficiency. Here Capitola put up both her hands and dropped her face upon them. Still further encouraged by this exhibition of feeling, Mr. Goodwin went on. He told her that it behoved her, who was a motherless girl, to be even more circumspect than others, lest through very Ignorance she might err; and in particular he warned her against riding or walking out alone, or indulging in any freedom of manners that might draw upon her the animadversions of their very strict community. "Oh, sir, I know I have been very indiscreet, and lam very miserable said Capitola, in a heart- broken voice.</p>
        <p>"My dear child, your errors have hitherto been those of ignorance only, and I am very much pleased to find how much your good uncle has been mis- taken; and how ready you are to do strictly right when the way is pointed ont I " said the minister, pleased to his honest heart's core that he had made this deep impress.on. A heavy sigh burst from the bosom ef Owitola. "What is the matter, my dear child?" he said, kindly. "Oh, sir, if I had only known you before!" ex- claimed Capitola, bitterly. "Why, my dear ?—I can do you just as much good now." "Oh, no, sir it is too late! it is too late!" "It is never too late to do well."</p>
        <p>" Oh, yes, sir, it is for me! Oh, how I wish I had had your good counsel before! it would have saved me from so much trouble " "My dear child, you make me seriously uneasy ! do explain yourself," said the old pastor, drawing his chair closer to hers, and trying to get a look at the distressed little face that was bowed down upon her hands, and veiled with her hair—"Do tell me, my dear, what is the matter ? " "Oh, sir, I'm afraid to tell you! you'd hate and despise me! you'd never speak to me again!" said Ca.pitola, keeping her face concealed, "My dear child," said the minister, very gravely and sorrowfully, "whatever your offence has been, and you make me fear that it has been a very serious one, I invite you to confide it to me, and having done so I promise however I may mourn the sin, not to 'hate,' or despise,' or forsake the sinner. Come, confide in me."</p>
        <p>_ "Oh, sir, I daren't I indeed I daren't 1" moaned Capitola. "My poor girl I " said the minister, "if lam to do you any good, it is absolutely necessary that yea make me your confidant." "Oh, sir, I have been a very wicked girl! I daren't tell you how wicked I have been!" "Does your good uncle know or suspect this wrong-doing of yours? " "Uncle I Oh, no, sir! He'd turn me out of doors. kill me! Indeed he would, sir. Please don't tell him 1" "You forget, my child, that / do not know the nature of your offence," said the minister, in a state of painful anxiety. "But I'm going to inform you, sir! and, oh, I hope you will take pity on me and tell me what to do; for though I dread to speak, I can't keep it on my conscience any longer, it is such a heavy weight on my breast!" "Sin always is, my poor girl I " said the pastor, with a deep groan. "But, sir, you know I had no mother, as you said yourself." "I know it, my poor girl, and am ready to make every allowance," said the old pastor with a deep sigh, not knowing what next to expect. " And—and—l hope you will forgive me, sir! but—but he was so handsome I couldn't help liking him!"</p>
        <p>"MISS BLACK!" cried the horrified pastor. " There! I knew you'd just go and bite my head off the very first thing! Oh dear, what shall Ido !" sobbed Capitola. The good pastor, who had started to his feet, remained gazing upon her in panic of consternation, murmuring to himself : "Good angels! lam fated to hear more great sins than if I were a Prison chaplain I" Then going up to the sobbing delinquent, he said: "Unhappy girl! who is this person of whom you speak 7" "H —h—h—him that I met when I went walking In the woods I" sobbed Capitola. "Heaven of Heavens! this is worse than my very worst fears I—Wretched girl! tell me instantly the name of this base deceiver !"</p>
        <p>_ "He—he—he's no base deceiver; he—be—he's very amiable and good-looking; and—and—and that's why I liked him so much; it was all my fault, not his, poor, dear fellow 1" "His name 2" sternly demanded the pastor. " Alf—Alf—Alfred," wept Capitola. "Alfred whom l" "Alfred Blen-131en—Blenheim 1" "Miserable girl! how often have you met this miscreant in the forest 7"</p>
        <p>"I—don't—know I" sobbed Capitola. "Where is the wretch to be found now ?" " Oh, please don't hurt him, sir! Please don't! He—he—he's hid in the closet in my room." A groan that seemed to have rent his heart in twain burst from the minister, as he repeated in deepest horror: "In your room (Well! I must prevent murder being done!) Did you not know, you poor child, the danger you ran by giving this young man private interviews; and, above all, admitting him to your apartment? Wretched girl better you'd never been born than ever so to have received a man 1" "Man? man? MAN 7—l'd like to know what you mean by that, Mr. Goodwin 1" exclaimed Capitola, lifting her eyes flashing through their tears. "1 mean the man to whom you have given these</p>
        <p>private interviews." " / I—/ give private interviews to a man! Take care what you say, Mr. Goodwin! I won't be in- sulted! no, not even by you 1" "Then if you are not talking of a man, who or what in the world are you talking about!" exclaimed the amazed minister.</p>
        <p>"Why, Alfred, the Blenheim poodle that strayed away from some of the neighb Iles houses, and that I found in the woods and brought home and hid in my closet, for fear he would be inquired after, or uncle would find it out, and make me give him up I I knew it was wrong, but then he was so pretty-" Before Capitola had finished her speech, Mr. Goodwip had seized his hat, and rushed out of the house in indignation, nearly overturning Old Hurri- cane, whom he met on the lawn, and to whom he said:</p>
        <p>"Thrash that girl as if she were a bad boy—for she richly deserves it!" "There! what did I say! now you see what a time I have with her! she maker; me sweat, I tell you!" said Old Hurricane, in triumph. "Oh, oh, oh," groaned the sorely tried minister. "What is it now?" inquired Old Hurricane.</p>
        <p>The pastor took the major's arm, and while they walked up and down before the house, told how he had been sold' by Capitola, ending by saying: "You will have to take her thinly in hand." "I'll do it," said Old Hurricane. "I'll do it." The pastor then called for his horse, and resisting all his host's entreaties to stay to tea, took his departure. Major Warfield re-entered .the house, resolving to</p>
        <p>Say nothing to Uapitola for the present, but to seize the very first opportunity of punishing her for this fiippancy. The village fair had commenced on Monday. It had been arranged that all Major Warfield's family should go, though not upon the same day. It was proposed that on Thursday, when the festival should be at its height, Major Warfield, Capitola and the house-servants should go. And on Saturday, Mrs. Condiment, Mr. Ezy and the farm-servants should have a holiday for the same purpose. Therefore upon Thursday morning all the house- hold best:rred themselves at an unusually early hour, and appeared before breakfast in their best Sunday's suit, Capitola came down to breakfast in a rich, blue silk carriage dress, looking so fresh, blooming and joyous, that it went to the old man's heart to dis- appoint her; yet Old Hurricane resolved, as the pastor had told him to "be firm," and once for all, by inflicting punishment to bring her to a sense of her errors.</p>
        <p>"There, you need not trouble yourself to get ready, Capltola, you shall not go to the fair with us!" he said, as Cap, took her seat. "Sir!" exclaimed the girl, in surprise. _. _ _</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes you may stare! but I'm in earnest! you have behaved very badly! you have deeply offended our pastor! you have no reverence, no docility, no propriety, and I mean to bring you to a sense of your position by depriving you of some of your indulgences! and in a word, to begin, I say you shall not go to the fair to-day!" "You mean, sir, that I shall not go with you, although you promised that I should," said Cap, coolly. "I mean you shall not go at all, demmy !" "I'd like to know who'll prevent me," said Cap. "I will, Miss Vixen! Demmy, I'll not be set at naught by a beggar I—Mrs. Condiment! leave the room, mum, and don't be sitting there listening to every word I have to say to my ward. Wool, be off with yourself, sir! what do you stand there gap- ing and staring for 7—be off, or—" the old man lcoked around for a missile, but before he found one the room was evacuated except by himself and Capitola. "Now, minion 1" he began as soon as he found himself alone with the little rebel: "I did not choose to mortify you before the ser- vants, but once for all, I will have you to under- stand that I intend to be obeyed 1" And Old Hur- ricane "gathered his brows like a gathering storm." _ _</p>
        <p>"Sir, if you were really my uncle, or my father, or my legal guardian, I should have no choice but to obey you; but the same fate that made rae desolate made me tree! a freedom that I would not exchange for any gilded slavery 1" said Cap, gaily. "Pish 1 tush pshaw I I say I will have no more of this nonsense! I say I will be obeyed," cried Old Hurricane, striking his cane down upon the floor— "and in proof of it I order you immediately to go and take off that gala dress and settle yourself down to your studies for the day." "Uncle, I will obey you as far as taking off this dress goes, for since you won't give me a seat in your carriage I shall have to put on my habit and ride Gyp," said Cap, good humouredly. " WHAT I I do you dare to hint that you have the slightest idea of going to the fair against my will?" " Yes, sir," said Cap, gaily—" sorry it's against your will, but can't help it not used to being ordered about and don't know how to submit, and so I'm going 1 " "Ungrateful girl! actually meditating dis- obedience on the horse I gave her I " "Easy now, uncle—fair and easy! I did not sell my free will for Gyp! I wouldn't for a thousand Gyps He was a free gift 1 " said Capitola, beginning an impatient little dance about the floor.</p>
        <p>"Come here to me! Come—here—to—me I " exclaimed the old man, peremptorily, rapping his cane down upon the floor with every syllable. Capitola danced up to him, and stood, half smiling, and fingering and arranging the lace of her under-sleeves. "Listen to me, you witch! Do you intend to obey me or NOT ! " "NOT !" said Cap, good-humouredly, adjusting her cameo bracelet, and holding up her arm to see its effect. "You will not! Then demmy, Miss, I shall know how to make you 1" thundered Old Hurricane, bringing the point of his stick down with a sharp rap. "Eh I " cried Capitola, looking up in astonish- ment. "Yes, Miss, that's what I said! MATE YOU I" "I should like to know how," said Cap, returning to her cool good-humour. "You would, would you? Demmy, I'll tell you! I have broken haughtier spirits than yours in my life. Would you know how ?" "Ye.," said Capitola, indifferently, still busied with her bracelets. "Stoop, and I will whisper the mystery." Canitola bent her graceful head to hear. " With the sod 1" hissed Old Hurricane, Capitola sprang up as if she had been shot, wave after wave of blood tiding up in burning blushes over neck, face and forehead, then turning abruptly, she walked off to the window. Old Hurricane, terrified at the effect of his rude, rash words, stood excommunicating himself for having been provoked to use them, nor was the next aspect of Capitola one calculated to re-assure his perturbed feelings. She turned around; her face was as white and still as marble, except. her glittering eyes, that, half sheathed under their long lashes, flashed like stilettoes; raising her head and keeping her eyes fixed upon him, with the slow and gliding motion, and the deep and measured voice that scarcely seemed to belong to a denizen of earth, she approached and stood before him, and spoke these words: "Uncle, in all the sorrows, shames and sufferings of my destitute childhood, no one ever dishonoured my person with a blow; and if ever you should have the misfortune to forget your manhood so far as to strike me--" she paused, drew her breath hard between her set teeth, grew a shade whiter, while her dark eyes dilated until a white ring flamed around the iris. • "Oh, you perilous witch, what then?" cried Old Hurricane, in dismay. "Why then," said Capitola, speaking in a low, deep, and measured tone, and keeping her gaze fix, I upon his astonished face, " tue—first—time —l—should—tind—you—asleep—l—WOuld take z or—and-- "Cut my throat! I feel you would, you terrible termagant 1 " shuddered Old Hurricane. "Shave your beard oil smick, smack, emoove 1" said Cap, bounding off and laughing merrily as she ran out cf the room. In an instant she came bounding back, saying: "Uncle I will meet you at the fair lau revoir lau revoir I" arid kissing her hand, she danced away and ran off to her room. "She'll kill me! I know she will! If she don't in one way she will in another. Whew I I'm per- spiring at every pore. Wool! Wool, you scoundrel ?" exclaimed the old man, jerking the bell-rope as if he would have broken the wires.</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir here I am, marse !" exclaimed that worthy, hastenivg in, in a state of perturbation, for he dreaded another storm. " Wool I go down to the stables and tell every man there, that if any of them allows a horse to be brought out for the use of Miss Black to-day, I'll flay them alive, and break every bone in their skins! Away with you l' "Yes, sir!" cried the shocked and terrified Wool, hurrying off to convey his panic to the stables. Old Hurricane's carriage being ready, he entered It and drove off to the fair. Next, the house-servants (with the exception of Pitapat, who was commanded to remain behind and wait upon her mistress) went off in a waggon. When they were all gone, Capitola dressed herself In her riding-habit, and sent Pitapat down to the stables to order one of the grooms to saddle Gyp and bring him up for her. Now when the little maid delivered this message, the unfortunate grooms were filled with dismay— they feared their tyrannical little mistress almost as much as their despotic old master, who in the next change of hi i capricious temper might punch all their heads for crossing the will of his favourite, even though in doing so they had followed his directions. An immediate private consultation was the consequence. and the result was that the head-groom came to Pitapat, told her that he was sorry, but that Miss Black's pony had fallen lame. The little maid went back with this answer. When she was gone the head-groom, calling to his fellows, said: "That young gal ain't a-gwrine to be fooled either by 010 marse or we! She'll be down here herself nex' minute, and have the horse walked out. Now we must have him lame a little. Light a match here, Jem, and I'll burn his foot.' This was immediately done. And sure enough, while poor Gyp was still smarting with his burn, Capitola came, holding up her riding train and hurrying to the scene, and asking indignantly: "Who dares to say that nay horse is lame Bring him out here this instant that I may see him." _ The groom immediately took poor Gyp and led</p>
        <p>1 ,</p>
        <p>him limping to the presence or his mistress. df At the sight capitei . was almost ready tow with grief and indignation. "He was not lame last evening. It mast' been your carelessness, you good-for-nothing loungers! And if he is not well enough to take to the fair to-morrow, at least, I'll have the vojk set of you lamed for life 1" she exclaimed, aegl' as she turned off and went up to the house-43,4 caring so much, after all, for her own personal appointment as for Old Hurrican's triumph. Cap's ill-humour did not last long. She exchanged her riding-habit for a morning wran and took her needle-work and sat down to sevt_n! the side of Mrs. Condiment in the housekeer'' MOM. etW.i</p>
        <p>soo!</p>
        <p>The day passed as usual, only that just after set Mr. Condiment, as a matter of precaution, vie all over the house securing windows and do( before nightfall. Then, after an early tea,. Condiment, Capitola, and the little maid, Pitk gathered around the bright little wood fire, that chilly Spring evening made necessary in the boo° keeper's room. Mrs. Condiment was kni' Capitola stitching a bosom for the Major's and Pitapat winding yarn from a reel. The conversation of the three females left sit in the old house naturally turned upon subjects fear—ghosts, witches and robbers.</p>
        <p>-10:C</p>
        <p>'WO</p>
        <p>;hirtl</p>
        <p>Mrs. Condiment had a formidable collectiofl_.! accredited stories of apparitions, warnings, dreatf, omens, etc., all true as gospel. There was a haunt°, house, she said, in their own neighbourhood--w, Hidden House. It was well authenticated that es! since the mysterious murder of Eugene Le unaccountable sights and sounds had been seen BP heard in and about the dwelling. A traveller, I brother officer of Colonel Le Noir, had slept the, once, and "in the dead waste and middle of t,,u night" had had his curtains drawn by a lady, pm, and passing fair, dressed in white, with floW hair, who, as soon as he attempted to speak to bel, fled. And it was well known that there was no lag, about the premises. Another time old Mr. Ezy himself, when a after coons, and coming through the woods near roof lighted up by a strange blue flame; draW near, he saw within the lighted room a feline.' clothed in white, passing and repa.ssing the dow.</p>
        <p>Another time, when old Major Warfield was cal; with his dogs, the chase led him past the hanntei house, and as he swept by he caught a glimpse a pale, wan, sorrowful female face pressed agalm_; the window-pane of an upper room, which vaniso', in an instant. "Thr, might not that have been some yoleg woman staying at the house 7" asked Capitola. "No, my child, it is well ascertained that OF the murder of Eugene Le Noir and the disappear:, ance of his lovely young widow, no white fewPfh has crossed the threshold of that fatal house," sor Mrs. Condiment. _ _ nf</p>
        <p>"Disappearance, did you say? Can a lady condition disappear from a neighbourhood and rl inquiry be made for her I" " No, my dear, there was inquiry, and it falai answered plausibly that Madame Eugene was ines':, and sent off to a lunatic asylum; but there are th mu' who believe that the lovely lady was privato rrio,le way with." whispered Mrs. Condiment. °How dreadful! I did not think such t. 1: happened in a quiet country neighbourll.ll Something like that occurred, indeed, in New 'Yortgg/I within my own recollection, however," said tola—who straightway commenced and related _A story of Mary Rogers, and all other stories of that memory supplied her with. te As for poor little Pitapat, she did not presume enter into the conversation, but with her ball 21 yarn suspended in her hand, her eyes started they threatened to burst from their sockets, eP", her chin dropped until her mouth gaped wide ope.; she sat and swallowed every word, listening With thousand-audience power. By the time they had frightened themse pretty thoroughly the clock struck eleven, and t7' thought it was time to retire. 004 "Will you be afraid, Mrs. Condiment?" Capitola. "Well, my dear, if I am, I must try to WO a the Lord and overcome it, since it is no use top afraid. I have fastened up the house well 00 have brought in Growler, the bull-dog, to sleeP_A the mat outside of my bedroom door, so I shall 0:i my prayers and try to go to sleep. I dare ird there is no danger, only it seems lonesome like us three women to be left in this big house bY • "b tiler</p>
        <p>selves.'' tt</p>
        <p>Yes," said Capitola , Jut as you say no danger; and as for me, if it will give yoe? comfort or courage to hear me say it, I am nOt,it. least afraid, ahhough I sleep in such a remote ro'oti, and have no one but Patty, who, having n° Trfil heart than a hare, is not near such a P°"''' ,gbp</p>
        <p>protector as Growler." And, bidding her little maid to take up the 144 lamp, Capitola wished Mrs. Condiment good-lug' and left the housekeeper's room.</p>
        <p>( To be continued.)</p>
