      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>WHAT IS THAT ONE THING?</p>
        <p>It seems like an absurdity, yet it is true all the same. I mean that you might have a cellar full of wood and coal and still shiver with cold; and you tcou/d if it were</p>
        <p>not for one thing. " Oh, that is so obvious," you may say. "It was hardly worth while to hint at it. Any- body can see it with his eyes shut." All the better for me then; I shan't have to explain. And by the same sharpness you will be able to pick out the important point in two short letters I am about to copy for you. The first runs thus : "In December, 1.890. my daugh- ter (Mrs. M. J. Muther) got into a low, weak, nervous condition. Do what she would she could not get up her strength. Gradually she wasted away until everyone thought she was in a decline, and had not long to live. In fact, shellwas so low and dejected she did Itot care whether she lived or not. She was under a doctor for six</p>
        <p>months, but his medicines did her no good. My husband</p>
        <p>then said, My daughter, I will Dow see what I can do for you.' What he meant was that he would have her take a medicine called Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup.' He had used it himself when he was ill, and thought it might prove as beneficial to her as it had to him.</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Mother said s he was willin' to try the Syrup, although she had:little or no .faith in its helping one as 113 a das she was. For if she really had consumption we know there:is no cure for that. My husband, however, got a bottle from Mr. Hulme, the chemist, in Rochdale- road, and my daughter began taking it. After the first bottle we saw a great improvement. Sha could eat, and the food caused her no pain. She continued with this remedy, and gradually gained strength, but it took some time to bring her round, she was so very low and weak. After a time she was able to get about, and never looked behind her. Since then she has been strong and well. We have told many persons how Mother Seigel's Syrup restored her to sound health, and are willing you should publish this statement of, the facts. (Signed) (Mrs.) Margaret viatson,.E.ll, Ruby-st., Bury, Oct. Bth, 1895."</p>
        <p>" In March,9.893," says the second letter, "I began to fail in health. I could not say exactly what ailed me. I felt, low, weak, and tired, and had no strenah for anything. My appetite fell away, and what little food I ate gave me great pain at the chest and side. My hands and feet were cold, and nearly all the colour left me. I was often in so great pain I could hardly do any work. I was frequently sick, and could keep no kind of food down.</p>
        <p>"I got weaker and weaker, in spite of all that was done, and had to he off my work for seventeen weeks. In this way I went on until November of the same year- -1293. Then I happened to read about Mother Seigel's Syrup and what it had done for others suffering like me. I got a bottle of this medicine from Mr. W. Heywood, grocer, in Oldham-road, and after taking only the half of it I felt much better. I could eat without pain, and was stronger and brighter every way. When I had finished the bottle I was quite cured, and have bad no return of the complaint since. I have told many others about what the Syrup did for me, and out of thankful- ness I am willing my letter should be made public.. (Signed) Miss Lydia E. Morton, 1, Greaves-st., Middle- ton Junction, near Manchester, October 10th,1395."</p>
        <p>Both these ladies say that they were very weak, and that their food—of which they could take but little— did„ them no good. In the midst of plenty they were actually starving. So much wasted was one of them that it was believed she had consumption. The event showed that they both suffered from dysepsia, and no- thing else. But that was quite encugh ; and besides it often runs into consumption and other fatal maladies. By setting the stomach right Mother Seigel's syrup fully cured them both.</p>
        <p>Coals and wood are useless without means to light a fire; and bread and meat are as nothing unless we can digest them and make them part of our flesh and bone. That is easy to see and important to remember. And it its power to help nature to work this transformation that is makes Mother Seigel's Syrup so wonderful a remedy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>CONCERT IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>At the opening of Parr Hall Miss Clara Butt made a reputation that will never die in Warring- ton, and the expressions of satisfaction with her singing from those who beard her were only equalled by the tokens of regret shown by the people who were absent on that occasion. Since then a very lively recollection of her has existed in Warrington, and when it becameTkinwn that she was engaged for the Warrington Musical Society's concert the predictions of success were numerous. Happily these prophecies have come to pass, for the second concert of the season under the aus- pices of the Society, which was held in Parr Hall on ',Thursday week, was a signal success. The attendance was large, and the concert generally of the highest order. An excellent and somewhat , classical programme had been arranged, and it was done full justice to by both artistes, chorus, and orchestra. An orchestral triumphal march (Prout) I followed by "Lord Ulna's Daughter," which the chorus 200 strong sang well, were the first items. A Beethoven concerto for piano and orchestra in C Major came next, Mr. F. W. Reynolds performing on the piano with his accustomed ability. Miss Butt then made her first appearance, and was very warmly received on stepping on the platform. She sang "Che Faro" (Gluck),from "Orpheus,"with ex- quisite finish and feeling, and the audience would fain have had a repetition, but the singer pre- ferred to bow her acknowledgments in that instance, no doubt with a view to husbanding her vocal resources. Her next song was in the second half of the programme when she rendered "A Song of Thanksgiving" with so much power and expres- sion that the audience would not this time be denied, and Miss Butt complied with "Kathleen Mavour- neen." Near the end of the concert she again appeared and sang with great sweetness,"The Silver Ring." The pretty air, the artiste's finished inter- pretation, and her graceful style,again aroused the enthusiasm of her hesrers but notwithstanding repeated demands Miss Butt declined good-humour- edly to respond. Of Miss Ethel Barns it can be said without exaggeration that her skill as violinist was as pronounced as that of Miss Batt as a vocalist. Commencing with a concerto by Max Bruch, for violin and orchestra, she won golden opinions from all; but for her sole "Airs Russes " (Wieniawski) she was vociferously encored, and in response shet played a " Berceuse " by Faure. Though only making two appearances it was patent to everyone with any knowledge of the violin that Miss Barns had a complete command of the instru- ment, and her precision and finished execution were thought by many to be unexcelled by that of any other living lady violinist. Whether or no, there were no two opinions about the fact that she has a great future before her in the musical world. Miss Barns' youthfulness rendered her skill all the more remarkable. During the concert Mr. Reynolds played a pianoforte melody of his own composition, which though simple was graceful and pretty and therefore welcomed by the audience. As a soloist he also gave a finished interpretation of the" Air de ballet," (Chaminade),and me l; with hearty approval. The chorus contributed largely to the success of the concert by their splendid rendering of the " Spring's Message," for chorus and orchestra (Gade), and the fantasia on national airs by G. F. Vincent. But the greatest choral sucoess was won by the male voices in the Pilgrim'snhorus froni,"Tannhauser"( Wagner) which commencing very softly swells out into a grand volume of round and as go adually dies away again. A special appropriateness was attached to this chorus as Thursday the 13th was the anniver- sary of Wagner's death. An encore was demanded and accorded. Mr. F. H. Crossley conducted and the organist was Mr. J. B. Caldwell. Both gentle- men acquitted themselves with conspicuous ability. The copcert was one of the most successful that has been given by the Musical Society and will rank high among the important musical events of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>WARRINGTON AMATEUR. PHOTO- GRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above society was held on Tuesday evening at the committee romm of the Museum, the chair being occupied by Mr. J. Fairhurst, the presi- dent.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. P. T. Frith, E. A. Wallington, and W. Win- stanley• were unanimously elected members of the society.</p>
        <p>SECRETARY'S AND TREASURER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. S. STONE read the ninth aunual report, which stated that the number of members at the beginning of the year was 63. During the year four new members had been elected,and 15. resigned or their names had been crossed off the list owing to removal, &amp;0., leaving at the end of the year a membership of 49. Owing to various circumstances the meetings during the past year had not been held very regularly, and the attendance of the members had not been very good. The following de- monstrations had been given far the benefit of the society :—Mr. F. C. Bynoe on " The Frena Hand Camera " ; Mr.A. C. Baldwin of the Eastman Company, on nikko. solio, and platino bromide printing papers; Mr. H. Bond, hand camera and enlarging camera; Mr. T. Hesketh, "Enlarging by artificial light " ; Mr. H. N. Houghton, " Developers" ; and Mr. 13. S. Stone, "Lan- terns." The Committee of the Warrington Museum having offered to allow the society the free use of the committee room for the meetings during the year on the condition that the members of the society will from time to time exhibit photographs in a frame to be pro- vided, and would undertake to form an album of photo- graphic views of the various old houses, So., in the dis- trict, it was decided to accept those terms, and one ' frame containing photographs supplied by various mem- bers was now on exhibition in the Museum. The pho- tographic survey had so far not been successful. A list of places was supplied by Mr. Madeley and a copy sent to each member with a request to send in prints, but there being no response,the Council decided to offer prizes with the object of stimulating the work, but after further consideration, it was decided that there I were so many points to consider in connection with the I scheme that it could not be made into a competition,and therefore it would perhaps be unwise to give any prizes therewith. It was decided, however, that during the summer mouths (or rather the autumn, as the time had got on) outings should be arranged to visit as many of the places named as possible, and get photos for the Museum. Owing to unfavourable weather on Saturday afternoons, it was only possible to arrange one such visit which was made to Marphany Hall, Dutton Hall, and New Manor, Preston-o'th-Hill, and although a good number of plates bad been exposed, but few prints had been received. Permission was obtained to photograph Arley Hall, but a visit there could not be arranged. It was hoped in the coming spring the project would receive • more attention from the members,as the fact'of having something definite to work at would give some additional interest to the work of the members who would undertake it, as the photographic survey seemed likely to develop into a national one, it was well worth the careful and thoughtful attention of the members. In the early part of the year,Mr.H. Pritchard having left the district, Mr. D. S. Stone was elected hon. secret- ary in Isis place, and Mr. H. Milling was elected librarian. The council had, during the year, gone carefully into the question of revision of rules, and new rules would be brought forward at that meeting for approval or alteration. The advantages of the society did not seem to be fully recognised by the members. There was a library containing works of reference to most of the photograph matters and lantern and other subjects. The various photograph periodi- cals were also placed weekly in the reading room of the Museum and there were belonging to the society for the free use of members, a lantern for oil or limelight with all the various jets and fittings (except cylinders), a sheet (for which a charge of is. was made.), two hand cameras and a lantern slide reducing camera. It was hoped that that year, which had opened well, would be the most prosperous and interesting one in the history of the society, but to do that would require the hearty co-operation of each member which was what they looked for.—The report was adopted on the motion of Mr,H. N. Houorrrox, seconded by Mr. H. BOND.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. DALTON then read the balance sheet for the past year which stated that the total liabilities of the society were £54 41. 91 The assts were books and papers in library. £4; apparatus, £34; cash in building society, £l2 155.; balance from the profit and loss account, being loss, £2 14s. 1011.; cash in treasurer's hands. 14s. 100.—The balance sheet was adopted on the motion of Mr. BOND, seconded by Mr. HESKETIL</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen were elected on the Council upon a ballot being thken. Messrs. Dalton and G. Roberts acting as tellers:—Messrs. C. B. Aylward, H. Bond, J. Critchley, F. Dalton, T. J. Down, J. Fairhurst, R. Graham, J. Harding, T. Hesketh, H. N. Houghton,G. Kirby, H. Milling, D. S. Stone, T. Welsby, and J. Lyon Whittle.</p>
        <p>Subsequently, a new code of rules drawn up by the Council was submitted to the meeting and passed unani- mously, and the meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MILK IN THE COCOANITT.</head>
        <p>THE MILK IN THE COCOANITT.</p>
        <p>PERSONS who suffer with throat affections and inflam- mation of the lungs are too apt to consider only the local affections whatever they may be, and forget the condi- tion of the system which encourages them. The man who takes an ordinary specific for bronchitis, for instance, thinks only of relieving bronchitis, without any thought that he will probably have a recurrence of the disease, the next time his system is exposed to con- ditions similar to those which caused his present attack. This was exactly the case of a gentleman who wrote the following letter "I can hardly expresa my gratitude for the extreme benefit I have received from Scott's Emulsion. I have been a sufferer for many winters from bronchitis in its worst form, and I have tried all sorts of remedies (so- called), when a friend happened to mention that he had been very bad and tried Scott's Emulsion, seeming to improve rapidly, and who, by the way, has completely recovered. I thought I would also give it a trial, and I find it was the best thing ever I did, as after several bottles I can defy the weather, even fog, and suffer no inconvenience, and can go about my business in comfort. I was in a very low state in- deed when I started it, but I can go about like other people now. Yours gratefully, (signed), JOHN ROGERS, 98, Newcastle Street, Burslem, S:affordshire, January 12th, 1896."</p>
        <p>There is the "milk in the cocoanut " for you. Mr. Rogers had had experience arid knew of what he wrote. Scott's Emulsion gives relief that is permanent. It is not merely a specific for temporary benefit. It may almost be called bottled flesh and blood ,for it contains the most nourishing properties of food. Cod liver oil is the basis of Scott's Emulsion, and this standard remedy is com- bined with the valuable Hypophosphites of Lime and Soda in perfect proportions and easy for aszimilation. Scott's Emulsion 13 perfectly palatable. It is easy on the most sensitive stomach, and has augmeated the scope and effectiveness of cod liver oil a hundred fold.</p>
        <p>By its high standard of excellence and peculiar mani- pulation Scott's Emulsion stands alone. It cannot be duplicated. The genuine has trade mark of a man carrying a big fish on his back, on the wrapper. (9)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PROPHECIESTOF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE PROPHECIESTOF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>An electrical journal, in commenting on the incredu- lity of some avowedly scientific people as to eventual developments in electrical application now deemed im- possible,says the many instances in which we are enjoy- ing the benefits of inventions which our forefathers only hoped for should lead us to infer that what we have will be superseded by our descendants. For instance,in 1845, a writer in a New York paper suggested a plan for bringing England within speaking distance.He proposed to run a copper wire. well covered, and as large as a pipe stem, from Nova Scotia to the coast of Ireland. " This," he says," as is thought, may be accomplished by winding the wire upon reels, and arranging it on board a steamer, so as to be reeled off as fast as the boat goes, and dropped the whole width of the Atlantic. Another paragraph shows that the hail of bullets, as from a Maxim or a Nordenfelt, was also achieved by an electric method fifty years age. In 1845 there appeared an account of experiments in London with an electric gun, " the invention of Mr. Benningfield, of Jersey." Tha gun, a barrel for discharging bullets or balls five- eighths of an inch in diameter, was placed over the body of the machine by which the propelling power was generated, and the whole was run on a carriage with a pair of wheels weighing half a ton, and calculated to be drawn by one horse at the r ate of eight or ten miles an hour. " The barrel is supplied with balls from two chambers, the one fixed and the other movable. The last may be made large enough to contain an immense number of balls. It is calculated that ~rxe thousand balls or more, can be discharged in a minute, the volleys be- ing shot in almost continuous or constant succession,thc stationary chamber supplying the barrel."lt was avowett at the time—for the process was kept a secret—however, that the propelling Dower was produced, not by steam, but by the application of gases exploded by galvanic electricity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
