      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SCEPTIC CONVINCED.</head>
        <p>A SCEPTIC CONVINCED.</p>
        <p>I have 'very often (writes a correspondent) beard doubts expressed as to the accuracy of the statements made in newspaper: respecting certain wonderful cures effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale Peuple,but, after an experience lihave just had, it is impossible to doubt the authenticity of these statements any longer. I was deputed to ascertain the facts connected with the marvellous recovery of Janet Grey, aged 15, of 5, Bank- terrace, Basford, Stoke-on-Trent. I called upon Mr. Grey with this object in view. He informed me that his daughter had suffered severely from anaemia and general weakness. She commenced to suffer at the age of 12, and gradually became weaker and weaker. Her appetitejcompletely failed her, and she got so weak that she was compelled to take to her bed. She had natural cravings for vinegar, 4k3., and was in a thoroughly des- pondent state of mind. Once a bright and happy child, she lost interest in everything, and life , seemed a bur- den. An able doctor prescribed for her and did his utmost for her, but she sank low er and lower. However a friend of the family recommended a trial of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and as a last re- source a box was obtained and tried with astonishing results. After the first box had been taken the colour began to return to the child's face. Her appetite came back. She was able to run about ence again, and be- came cheerful and light hearted. The change was marvellous.</p>
        <p>She was completely cured, and is now stronger than over she hi.s been, and looks, rosy and well. Dr. Wil- liams' 2ink Pills for Pale People have had unrivalled suc- cess as a tonic and strengthening medicine. They cure anemia, and the many disorders which arise from an impoverished state of the blood, such as pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness,loss of appetite, palpitations, shortness of breath, pains in the back, ner- vous headache, early decay, all forms of female weak- ness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica. These Pills are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Com- pany, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d. They invigorate the system after overwork, worry, and indiscretions of living.</p>
        <p>THE SITURDAY EVENING CONCERT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday ovening the Parr Hall was crowded, upstairs and below, by an audience larger than any previous one since the opening of the building. All the seats were occupied and practically all the standing room. This proved the wisdom of the committee in re-engaging Mr. Mel B. Spurr and the Mdlles. Tostea, who were such a success earlier in the year. There was also Mrs. J. W. Wilson (soprano of the Leeds and Newcastle Festivals). Mr. H. E. Brenton acted as accompanist. Mdlle. Elsa Tostea,the juvenile pianist was the first artiste to appear and she commenced the programme with an efficient performance of " Rhapsodie liongroise," (Liszt). Her only after appearance was in accom- panying Mdlle. Marienne Tostea, and in this she displayed considerable ability. Mr. Mel B. Spurr occupied a large part of the programme and sang one of his humorous songs "Two Dollies" with good effect. Singularly his greatest success was in the musical sketch of his own composition, "Fancy Fair," which abounds in laughable songs, and mimicry of the usual and necessary people at such functions. Mr. Spurr came out in a new role on Saturday and one moreover in which he had attained ectual proficiency, viz: that of reciter and performer on the zither. On this instrument he gave a solo and followed with a recital of "The Game of Life," with zither accompaniment. Both were clever performances and later on in the even- ing Mr. Spurr gave a most dramatic recital of" The DEath Bridge of the Tay" (W. Carleton). He was encored at different points and his talent was thoroughly appreciated. Mdlle. Tostea for her mandoline solo, "Scene de Ballet," was encored, and gave an expressive and skilful inter- pretation of " Ave Maria." which was even better liked. Thom6s " Andante " was her next piece and the pretty air, and skilful execution of Mdlle Tostea won her another encore, when she complied with a selection from " Trlvatore." Mrs. J. W. Wilson sang" Should he Upbraid " in a pleas- ing manner and later on gave a" rendition of "When the heart is young. The main item of the programme was reserved for the last,and consisted of an original operetta entitled "The Ladies' Chance: A Leap Year Idyl." The characters were Harry Howard ( a bachelor), Mr. Spurt; and Lucy Loftus (a spinster), Mrs. Wilson. In the piece Miss Loftus, for a wager, visits Mr. Howard in his chambers, and eventually after some amusing conversation, ex- ercises her leap-year prerogative and proposes to Mr. Howard who accepts with joy. Several attrac- tive duets were sung and the whole piece was of a lively character and did credit to its composer, Mr. Spurr. Mrs. Wilson and Mr.Spurr acted their parts thoroughly well.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>A great many records were either made or broken at Hunslet on Saturday, on the occasion of Warrington's visit there. The Warringtonians took tour points out of Hunslet,a thing they have not yet done this season with any other club, and Hunslet have not lost four points to any other Club. Th 3 match in question was P•also the first Warrington have gained in Yorkshire, and the goal they scored was the first placed goal they have ever scored in that county. Warrington were also the only Lancashire club engaged with a Yorkshire club who came off victorious on Saturday. Therefore, taking the match as a whole, Warrington have good reason to be satisfied with themselves.</p>
        <p>Humlet undoubtedly had the best of the game as far as the general play went, but they found a sterling de- fence which they could only penetrate once, and this was where they lost the game. The Warringtonians played a marvellous defensive game, and, as they have shown of late with increasing clearness, they can tackle with a vengeance, and this they did on Saturday. The forwards also proved the superior of Hunslet in combin- ation. They have very few superiors for this, and only lack in weight.</p>
        <p>Bate and Foden bottled up their opponents, and it was a smart bit of work of" Smack" which led up to the score which Lawless was enabled to make under the goal posts. All the threequartera and He3som played well, Barber in particular, and it is gratifying that Heesom was able to show his old-time ability for kick- ing goals.</p>
        <p>Commencing to-lay, Warrington have five successive home matches, viz., Liversedge, St. Helens, Tyldesley, Manningham and Batley. Four of these matches when played away were lost by small majorities, Liversedge being lost by 8 points to 3 ; St. Helens by 3 points to nil; Manningham by a like number; and Batley by 4 points to nil ; while Tyldesley were beaten on their own ground. All these five should, therefore, be won by Warrington with comparative ease, and if this be the case, they will occupy a respectable position on the League table. Mannit.gham will be about the stiffest match, for they are going extremely strong at present.</p>
        <p>To meet Liversedge to-day, the following team is chosen : Heesom,full back; Lawless, Barber, Cross, and Carey, threequarters ; Bate and Foden, halves ; Nevins, Taylor, Hill, Saunders, Swift, Andrews, Thornton, and Burrows, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Widnes are having a rare race for positions in the League table. Widnes came a cropper at Oldham on Saturday, being beaten by 23 points to nil, which came as a matter of course after their vic- tory of the previous week over Tyldesley, who have beaten Oldham twice this season. This defeat gave Warrington an opportunity of passing them,but on Tues- day, Widnes again got ahead by beating Leigh. The Widnes team continue their erratic course and it is never safe to'prophesy what they will d 9 next. To-day, they meet Huddersfield, and it will be interesting to see how they will fare against the Tykes, for at the first meeting, Huddersfield routed them by 20 points to nil. It would not be surprising to see Huddersfield win, as Widnes have never won twice in succestion this season, and as they won the last match they played it is now their turn to lose.</p>
        <p>Unlike Widnes, Runcorn keep steadily on winning match after match, and one thing is certain—they will not be far from the top of the League table at the end of the season. It is simply wonderful how they continue to do so well despite all the casualties they have had, and the small stock of reserves they have to play upon. It seems incredible, but they have only lost four matches this season,having lost fewer than any other team in the Northern Union. Singular to state, three out of the four clubs which have beaten them are the three clubs above them on the table,Manning,ham, Halifax,and Old- ham, and the only other club which has bested them is St. Helens,this match being lost the second week in the season.</p>
        <p>The following jotting from the "Athletic News " [will be read with interest by Runcornians. "Frequently statements have been made of the great expense in- curred by Northern Union clubs in fulfilling engage- ments, which extend from the far end of Yorkshire at Hull, to the little Cheshire town of Runcorn. Several clubs have indeed got themselves semewhat deeply into debt. It is this fact, as much as any other, that has brought forth the proposal to divide the competition into Lancashire and Yorkshire sections, the champions of each to play for the premier place of honour at the end of the season. Under these circumstances it will sur- prise most people to learn on the authority of a Runcorn official, who is also a member of the Union Committee, that the finances of his club are in a better condition than they have been for some time, and that the forma- tion of the Union has been a blessing to them. The secret undoubtedly is that the players are all native born, and their demands for " broken time" most moderate— indeed; they may truthfully be described as amateurs— and the committee of the club incur no expense in poaching expeditions."</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers are totally unable to beat a Lancashire club.and they got a good beating from Leigh on Saturday. They have now played 15 matches with teams in their own county and never a one have they won.</p>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets have already lost two home matches by their suspension for referee baiting. They should have received Hull as their visitors on Saturday, and Tyldesley on Tuesday,but neither match could be played. They have been very severely punished owing to the ruffianism of some persons who probably have nothing to do with the club, and their case is harder still as they are now at the very bottom of the League,and it is likely that they will stick there in consequence of the suspension. Anyhow, the severe penalty for being suspended for so long a time should be a warning to the spectators who attend clubs not very far from Warrington. The Northern League is as follows :</p>
        <p>Score. Score.</p>
        <p>Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag st. Pts. 1%! anningham 29 ... 23 ... 6 ... 0 ...221 ...104 ... 46 Halifax Oldham Runcorn 27 ... 15 ... 4 ... 8 ...210 ... 83 ... 38 Br'ghouse Ran 29 ... 14 ... • 8 ... 7 ...140 ... 84 35 Leigh Bradford Hull Hunslet Wigan 2B ... 11 ... 10 ... 7 ...124 ...102 29 Leeds Tyldesley Liversedge 27 ... 12 ... 12 ... 6 ...197 ...157 27 • St.Helens 26 ... 10 ... 10 ... 6 ... 99 ...117 ... 24 Widnes Warrington Wakefield T 29 ... 8 ~. 17 ... 4 ... 76 .. 217 ... 20 Stock pott Batley 27 ... 6 ... 15 ... 6 ... 72 ...202 ... 18 Beglit'n Ran 27 ... 5 2... 16 ... 6 ... 94 ...145 ... 16 Huddersfield 2B ... 6 ... 19 ... 3 ... 91 ...155 ... 15 Rochdale H 27 ... 3 ... 16 ... 8 ... 56 ...179 ... 14</p>
        <p>The Lancashire section is as follows : Score Score Pts. Pld. Won Lost Drn For. Ag'st. Leigh Oldham Runcorn Wigan Tyldesley Widnes *St. Helens Warrington Rochdale H l2 ... 2 ... 5 ... 5 ... 32 ... 66 ... 9 Stock port B 'hen Ran l5 ... 0 ... 10 ... 5 ... 22 ... 82 ... 5 *St. Helens have had two points deducted for playing an ineligible man.</p>
        <p>The Yorkshire section runs as follows : Score Score Pts. PM. Won. Lost. Drn For. Ag'st. Manningham 15 ... 13 ... 2 ... 0 ...111 ... 38 ... 28 Leeds Hull l4 ... 7 ... 6 ... 1 ... 83 ... 42 ... 15 B'glionse Ran l4 ... 6 ... 5 ... 3 ... 61 ... 36 ... 15 Halifax Liversedge l5 6 ... 7 ... 2 ... 93 ... 75 14 Hunslat l2 ... 6 ... 5 ... I ... 70 ... 50 13 Bralford l5 ... 4 ... 6 ... 5 ... 45 ... 65 ... 13 Batley l5 ... 4 ... 9 ... 2 ... 37 ... 97 ... 10 Huddersfield— 14 ... 4 ... 9 ... 1 ... 35 ... F 4 ... 9 Wakefi'd Tri'tv 15 ... 4 ... 10 ... 1 ... 41 ...137 ... 9</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TERRIBLE FIRE IN LONDON.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE FIRE IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>LOSS OF EIGHT LIVES.</p>
        <p>A terrible fire broke out in a house in Chutch-street, Shaftesbury Avenue, London, on Sunday morning, and eight lives were lost. Two constables saw an unusual light in one of the upper rooms and tried to rouse the Inmates. Several escaped, but the smoke appears to bave overpowered some of the others before the alarm was given. A man named Moore sprang from the second storey window, but fell on to the railings below, and after escaping death from fire will probably die from the effects of his injuries. His wife and three children perished in the flames. The fire spread with such great rapidity and the heat was so intense that the firemen were utterly unable to give the people in the house any assistance.</p>
        <p>PURE RICH RED BLOOD BY TAKING HOOD'S SARSAPARILLA.</p>
        <p>" I venture to say that Hood's Sar- saparilla is the best blood purifier I have ever known. I have suffered very much from impure blood. The symptoms were pimples on the hands. I tried many bottles of lotions, which only kept them back, • but did not cure them. In the spring I bled at the nose quantities of blood which was very dark.and which when cold had the appearance of a lump of black liver. I believe if that had not come out it would have killed me. and so said the doctor. and I think it was nothing but blood poison. I could not sleep, for pains. in my legs, arms and all my joints, and my body was very much swollen. Every little cold gave me bronchitis and asthma. I was a little better in the summer, but during last winter I was as bad as ever. Early in the spring I received one of the papers advertising Hood's Sarsaparilla. I at once sent for a bottle. I have now had two large ones and two small ones, and I am a better man to-day than I have been for years. My hands burn a little at night, but I think they will soon be all right. I have recommended Hood's Sarsaparilla all around and have told many what a blessing I have found. You are at liberty to print this letter for the good of others." jOSEPH CAPPLEHAN, Ebenezer Cottage. Flamborongh. N.B. If you decide to take Hood's Sarsaparilla do not be induced to buy -any other. Insist upon Hood's and only</p>
