      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LEST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The traffic upon the canal last week showed a slight falling off from the previous week's returns, the arrivals and departures of seagoing vessels being less by 58, and the net registered tonnage less by 5,438 tons. Coastwise traffic increased by 1,442 net tons, although there was a decrease of 43 in the number of ships ; whilst the foreign exhibited a diminution of five ships and 6,880 net tons. Amongst the arrivals were 20 vessels with general cargoes, and six with stone and slate; two full cargoes of pig iron were received from Bowling, and one from Barrow; steel bars and billets from Barrow and Harrington, and large consignments of green fruit by the steamers Grange and Heimdahl from Valencia and Borghese from Sicily and Spain. Twenty- three vessels sailed with general cargoes, seven with coal two with salt, and two with purple ore. Full cargoes of pitch were exported to Cette and La Rochelle, and a schooner sailed with fire-clay for Belfast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>JANUARY TRAFFIC RETURNS.</head>
        <p>THE JANUARY TRAFFIC RETURNS.</p>
        <p>It is noteworthy that the Ship Canal traffic and revenue for last month, are the largest recorded for any month since the opening of the waterway, and, what is still more important, that the sea-going traffic is responsible for the whole of the in- crease, the barge traffic, which yields a revenue of about 4d. per ton only, being below the average. Appended is a comparison of last month's sea-going traffic and receipts therefrom with the best previous months, which shows that 6•8 per cent more cargo paid toll in January than in any similar period hitherto :</p>
        <p>Tons. £ July. 1895 105,994 August, 1895 103,428 October, 1895 t,a January, 1896 113,006 This record is the more satisfactory when it is remem- bered that last month the timber trade, which is one of the most important connected with the canal, was prac- tically at a standstill, and many of the Baltic ports were closed or closing,whereas in Ju1y,1895 (the best previous month in regard to the volume of traffic), the timber trade with both Canada and the Baltic was at its height. The improvement is largely due to the development ot the cotton trade, 37.000 bales of the American and Egyp- tian staple having been imported here during the month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SHIP CANAL BILL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council, on Tuesday, Mr. Brandreth stated that the committee ap- pointed to consider the above matter had had an inter- view with Messrs. Beloe and Priest. They thought after a conversation with them that they were fit and proper persons to take charge of their case, and they gave them instructions to draw up a report on the matter. Messrs. Beloe and Priest, engineers, Liverpool, sub- mitted their report with regard to the Ship Canal Bill. They alluded to the proposals of the company, and stated with regard to the closing of the tidal openings that it was manifest that a much smaller quantity of tidal water would enter the Canal than did at present, as the only access fcr tidal water into the Canal would be through the locks at Eastham, unless the company chose to raise the sluices, but they were not aware of any clause in any Act compelling them to do so. The contaminated water in the canal between Latchford and Easthana would not receive the same amount of delution by the addition of fresh tidal water as it did at present, and the consequence might be disagreeable, if not serious to the Runcorn dis- trict. A more serious matter in their opinion was that the Ship Canal Company would have it in their power to raise the water in the canal to a higher level than they could at present. Under existing conditions it was absolutely impossible for the water to stand at a higher level than the sill of the tidal openings, when the water in the river had fallen below that level, but when the tidal openings were closed, it would be at the option of the Canal Company to what extent they would lower the water by means of the sluices, and how rapidly they would de so. Consequently, all the sewers which now discharged into the canal, and into the river Weaver be- low a certain point on the latter river, would be water- logged to a greater extent and for a longer period than they were at present, and they thought it the Council's duty to oppose the closing of the tidal openings on those grounds, as experience had shown that it was not ad- visable to leave the control of the flow of water in the hands of the Canal Company, and mechanical means must be provided to prevent the water level being raised, or the period of high water in the canal being lengthened. With regard to the quantity of water which could flow into the canal through the Ea.sthana lochs, it would be necessary to make observations of the velocity of the current, and also to ascertain the quantity which now flowed through the exciting tidal openings on ordinary tides. They had asked the Canal Company for authority to ebtain this information. Any flew coming down the river Weaver. during flood tide, could under existing circumstances flow into the Mersey through the tidal openings, thus rendering it impossible for the Canal Company to raise the low water level of the canal more than a few inches, whether they used the sluices or not, but by the proposals now before Parlia- ment, the sluices were only to be raised during ebb tide and the whole of the flood waters which come down the river Weaver and the canal could be stored in the lower reaches of the canal until the ebb tide. This should he stoutly resisted. The clause 31, giving power to the Canal Company and the Warrington Corporation, to enter into agreements for settling any question pending between them, should also be opposed. The construction of the embankment on Norton Marsh was objectionable, and no doubt would be opposed by the Mersey Conservancy. The engineers recommended the presentation of a petition against the Bill.—The Clerk stated that he had written for a pass, and the Ship Canal Company had forwarded him one. He had sent it on to Messrs. Beloe and Priest. He hal also written to their Parliamentary agents at London who had stated that the last day for lodging petitions against the bill was likely to be the 29th inst. Mr. Heine had seen the report drawn up by Mr. Diggle their surveyor, and he had highly complimented him upon the way he had pre- pared it.—On the motion of Mr. Heywood. seconded by Mr. Williams it was decided that the Clerk should, if 11(1 found it necessary, call a special meeting of the Council to affix their seal to a petition to be lodged against the bill.—This was agreed to. PROPOSED COMPETITION WITH THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting in Liverpool on Wednesday Mr. A. Holt submitted his revived scheme for the construction of a " plateway " between—in the first instance—Liverpool and Manchester, en which much of the vast amount of geods traffic could, he thinks, be economically conveyed. The scheme was in existence when the Ship Canal move- ment began, and put it, for a time, out of criurt. Mr. Holt now thinks that the canal—" Manchester's trump card "—has not succeeded, and that the railways are in consequence left practically masters of the situation. A plateway, or" a highway up to date," would, he thinks, if once started, spread rapidly to the main centres of traffic. A short discussion followed Mr. Bolt's address. The scheme does not appear to have been received with much enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-ViTARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A</p>
        <p>The quarterly open m—ee-t-i-ne—of the Wycliffe P.S.A. was held on Sunday afternoon, when there was an excellent attPndance of both sexes.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, the Rev. John Yonge, occupied the chair and expressed the pleasure it gave him to see so many of them at another quarterly meeting. He welcomed the ladies especially and be thought he was expressing the feelings of many of those present when he said that they felt that the success of the P.S.A. Society had been largely helped by the sympathy and co-opera, tion of the women. It had struck him that there were two things which a great number of their sisters could do to help ha furthering the work. On Sunday next they would come to the end of the third year and that day fortnight they would commence a new year in the history of the P.S.A. and would then begin a fresh register of members. He found as a matter of fact that something like two or three hundred of the gross number who had been enrolled during the past 12 months failed to re-enter themselves on a new register. It was to coun- teract that,that he exhorted the women to endeavour to get their friends and relatives to re-enrol themgelves. Mr. Yong(' thought the P.S.A. movement in Warrington had not attained the succesa it might he expected to realise.They had a membership of 1,158 men hut he nor- sonally would not be satisfied until it reached 1,500, and he therefore appealed to the women to he recruitin&amp; officers for Wycliffe P.S.A. (Applause.) Mr. BALMER, of Manchester, then gave a humorous, but earnest address on " The Home," and the duty of the occupants of it towards one another and the whole world. He was listened to with great attention, and judging by the frequent applause, the speaker's remarks were appreciated.</p>
        <p>Miss Birchall gave a violin solo, the skilful perform- ance of which was well liked. Mr. Pemberton sang "A dream of Paradise," and was well received. Mr. J. A. Yonge, of Wakefield, read the lesson, and Mr. Healy offered up prayer.</p>
      </div>
