        <p>e THE whole medical profession the world over is engaged in an earnest search for some remedy which will cure Indigestion, Constipation, and Dyspepsia. Statistics show that nearly half the deaths in civilized countries are caused by diseases of .the stomach and kidneys. Hitherto the remedies prescribed by the medical profession for Indigestion have been of little value, but the C. A. Vogcier Curative Compound does cure Indigestion_ It is the most wonderful medicine in existence, a few doses gives relief—a few bottles cure the most chronic cases. A trial bottle will convince the sufferer that we make none but true statements. Price 113 and 2;6! of all Chemists; or sent free by Parcels Post on receipt of 14 or 30 stamps by the Sole Proprietors, The Charles A. Vogeter co., 45, Farringdon Road, London.+ *"</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN HOME MIS</p>
        <p>ItY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual public meeting in support of the Home Missionary Fund was held at the Bold-street Wesleyan School on Wednesday evening. Mr. R. G. Henshall pre-. sided, and there were also present, the Revs. Charles Garrett (Liverpool), Owen Watkins- G. D. Mason, IV. G. Triggs and J. Gunnell.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN, in his opening speech, said that he felt it a great honour to preside at a meeting at which the principal speaker was the Rev. Charles Garrett. (Applause.) He thought that they were fortunate in having secured Mr. Garrett's presence at their Home Missionary Anniversary. Mr. Garrett had for many years been conducting one of the most successful home missionary enterprises that Methodism could boast of, and he had no doubt that he would be able to say many things of interest of the work in which he was engaged. He dared say that many saw in the papers a short time ago that at Bolton, the trouble was taken of seeing how many people attended places of worship on Sundays. It was found that 14,314 were present at the chapels and churches out of a population ot 125,000. Leaving the aged, sick and young children out, it seemed to him that practically five out of every six people at Bolton wore living entirely outside all religiotis denominational influences. Re thought that was a very sad state of things, and he was very glad that Mr. Thomas Walker had nobly offered £5,000 towards the building of a Cen- tral mission. Si'nat obtained in B3lton was only a sample of whit might be found in all the large manufac- turing towns. Methodists had done nobly for the foreign missions, and he thought that what they wanted was to try to get more earnestness and more:enthusiasm for the home missionary work, and LH trusted they wou!d strive to give more liberally and to support more heartily, so that the organisations would be able to push forward the work more energetically.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. D. MAs'ort then gave the annual report, and observed that the income from the foreign missions had beeia the largest in the history of the circuit. They did not yet know what was the result of the home mis- sionary collections this year, bat be hoped that that would also be the largest ever known. They in War- rington were entering upon a new departure, by having a deaconess who would enter upon her work shortly in the Sankey Bridges district of the circuit. The collections for the past year in the Warrington circuit for home missions had been £67 194. 5d., which included among other items, yearly collections, £l5 ; congregational col- lections,£23 O. 5i.; public meeting, £7 4s. 81.; juvenile associations, £l7 63. 4d.</p>
        <p>The Rev. CR4.RLE3 GARRETT said that he was glad to come to Warrington for several reasons. The last time he came he was not worth his carriage, and he owed a debt of gratitude to Dr. Smith for the care he had taken of him at that time,and he had never looked back since. (Applause.) Mr. Watkins was also his right hand man, and did many little things for him, so that he could not say "No " to him when he asked him to come to speak that night. Warrington had always had a place in his affections. From time he first came there when Dr. Smith's noble father was alive, and other friends, War- rington had always been associated in his mind wits hearty Methodism and vigorous work. He was always glad to be at Warrington. Mr. Garrett then went on to speak upon various questions connected with home mis- sions. He said that one great hindrance to the work was drink. It was thought a short time ago that they were getting the upper hand of it, but it was again asserting its power and dictating in almost thundering tones to the legislature of the country. This was a serious mat- ter, for when the drink traffic prospered nothing else could prosper. (Hear,hear.) Then there was prieitism, which was coming to the front. If our country ever got under the dominion of the priesthood, be it Protestant priesthood or Roman Catholic priesthood, it would sink amongst the nations. Then there was the awful state under which the poor lived. Mr. Garrett gave some par- ticulars which showed the miserable conditions under which the poor of Liverpool worked, and then gave some interesting details of his work at the Liverpool mission. He appealed in conclusion to his audience to contribute generously towards home missions which were so important to the people of this country. At the close a collection was taken for the home mis- sionary fund, and the meeting terminated with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
