      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EX A_Al IN ER.</head>
        <p>THE EX A_Al IN ER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, February 220 d, 1896.</p>
        <p>FURTHER reports upon the Armen- The Armenian • lan atrocities made by the British Horrors. AMBASSADOR at Constantinople and by the Consuls in various districts in Turkey have been published this week and they again confirm all the worst accounts of the horrors that have ap-</p>
        <p>..... _ . _ peared in the newspapers. Sir PHILIP CURRIE estimates the total loss of life respecting which accurate information could be obtained at 25,000</p>
        <p>persons, but he adds that the figure would have to be increased to a much higher number if the massacres of which no detailed information could be had were taken into account. Even in Con..</p>
        <p>stantinople very many Armenians were clubbed to</p>
        <p>death in the streets or made away with by other and worse methods. There can be no doubt about the complicity of the Turkish authorities in these murders. The Softas and Other fanatical Turks were armed with clubs and other weapons and were allowed by the police to chase the un- fortunate Armenians through the streets, and in several cases the police actually held the victims while they were being battered to death. The Armenians who were taken prisoners by the authorities were treated with the greatest bruta- lity, and one official of the British Consulate saw with his own eyes foar of them brought into the courtyard of the Ministry of Police and bayonetted in cold blood. An incident of this kind throws a flood of light upon the constant complaints that were received by the foreign ambassadors that inoffensive Armenians, mostly servants or work-</p>
        <p>ingmen, had mysteriously disappeared. In one hospital in Constantinople no fewer than eighty dead bodies were received, and each body showed by its condition that death had been inflicted with frightful violence. When outrages of this sort could happen in the capital under the very eyes of the indignant representatives of foreign countries, it is not difficult to imagine what took place in the provinces. At Ak-Hissar, a place within half-a-day's journey of Constantinople, the Armenians, who had gathered in the town from the surrounding villages on a market day, were suddenly attagked by the Mussulmans and many of them were killed ; and, according to an eye- witness, some of the wounded were thrown into wells by their pitiless assailants and filth and rubbish cast on the top of them. In the remoter provinces the massacres were on a more extensive scale. While in Constantinople and its neighbourhood the victims might be counted by hundreds, away in Anatolia and Armenia they fcould be numbered by thousands.</p>
        <p>_ At Erzereum the Mussulman mob, which had been prepared for several days beforehand, made an attack on the Armenian shops, massacred all the Armenians they could find, and then commenced a general pillage and the result was hundreds of dead and wounded Armenians. On the very day that Lord SALISBURY delivered his famous Guild- hall speech he received the following message by wire from the British Charge d'Affaires at Constantinople :--"‘ Yesterday one of the foreign Consuls arrived from Erzeroura on leave. He describes the scene during his journey as heart- rending. The whole country between Trebizond and Erzeroum is :devastated. On the outskirts of Baiburt he counted 100 dead bodies lying together near the road. Nearly all the villages ar3 burned, and in many cascs the male population entirely wiped out." At Trebizond, it is officially reported, therelwas "a planned attack on the defenceless male Armenians indoors and out of doors." A trumpet was sounded from the minaret of a mosque near the Government House,and almost simultaneously a massacre began at five different parts of the town. The streets were flooded with armed and infuriated Turks, and these, aided by soldiers and by Greeks and Persians, slew every Armenian they could lay their hands upon. It is known that five or six hundred 'men were killed in this fearful outbreak of social and religious hatred and even women were not spared. As it was in Trebizond and Erzeroum, so it was in numerous smaller towns and villages. The devastations spread over a territory larger in extent than the whole of Great Britain, and in a despatch on the 13th of December the British Ambassador de- clares that it was a moderate estimate to place the loss of life at 30,000. The latest Blue-book proves very clearly that the" Concert of Europe" which Lord SALISBURY so highly eulogised in one of his speeches has been little more than a sham and a delusion. It is to the credit of the English Government that it endeavoured to persuade the other powers to act vigorously in their dealings with the Porte. Russia has undoubtedly been the stumbling block in the way. When that country effectively protect to join with the other powers to ertee protect the Armenians the much- vaunted"Concert " practically went to pieces, and the persecuted Christians had little further to hope from the protection of the Christian Governments of Europe. The whole course of these negotiations is a miserable record of the mutual jealousies of the great powers of Europe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22. 1896 RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22. 1896 RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE Rev. W. B. HA.LB, a Massa- American Church Finance. chusett's clergyman, gives an account in the " Forum " of the extraordinary methods of raising money that are resorted to in connection with some of the Ameri- can churches. American preachers of the gospel are, it appears, wonderfully original, and it must be admitted, just as candid as they are original. The case of one Church Fair was described by its promoters as" a programme of ingenious devices for obtaining money under false pretences." In anether case a clergyman told the people who had assembled at the opening of his bazaar that " they came to be cheated," and the reason he gave for holding a bazaar at all was this—" The Baptists had just had a War Concert; the Uuiversalists, a Lawn Fete ; the Free-Will Baptists, a Chocolate Drill; the Congregationalists,a Mrs.Jarley's Wax- ifforkiShow;the Unitarians,a Faney Dress Ball;the Swedenborgians, a May-Pole Frolic ; the Epis- copalians, a Cafe Chantant ; and the Zion Church, a Cake-Walk. What was there left but a bazaar." The advertisement of a performance at the "People's Church, Boston, promised, we are told, a "distinctly livelier show" than that announced for a notorious variety hall. The Baptist Church of Avon gave a Living Picture Show with "Over the Garden Wall "and "Rock of Ages" as inter- ludes; and in Stoughton " the Universalist Church, with an eye single to the glory of God," gave a Female Negro Minstrel Show. In fact, it is said that female minstrel shows have this year been the most popular of Church entertainments in Massachusetts, but at Middlesboro' the Unitar- ians "enlivened their minstrel performance with a song-and-dance turn; an impersonation of the Bowery Girl and a skirt dance." The Church of the Epiphany at Winchester, Massachusetts, seems to have given quite a clever presentation of the "Mikado,,' the local Press commenting upon the performance in the following terms :—"Pish Tush, by the leader of the Church of the Epip- hany choir, was a most happy impersona- tion. Nanki Poo, in his cream tights, made life indescribably sunny for !Funs Yum. The Mikado himself, a Calumet Club end-man, delighted all with his happy local hits. In short, the Church of the Epiphany, as the producer of light operatic diversion, has crowned itself with glory." But, it seems that in spite of all these incongruous accom- paniments of divine worship, there is an improve- ment to be noted. According to Mr. HALB the lottery has almost disappeared from American church entertainments. There has not been any Congresses of Beauty lately, and the "selling of kisses" which used to be a recognised mode of rehabilitating church finance, has been "discoun- tenanced by a growing discernment of the proprie- ties." There is undoubtedly still plenty of room for further improvement. In this respect at all events the ways of the "old country" have not been bettered on the other side of the Atlantic.</p>
        <p>The various District Councils of Lancashire and Cheshire have been decidedly snubbed by the County Councils of the two counties in connection with their pa titions in favour of agitating Parlia- ment to tax cyclists. The District Councils are to a large extent constituted of farmers and landed gentry, and they do not like to have to pay for the maintenance of the roads in their districts. They, therefore, wish to have their rates lightened by mulcting the cyclists to the extent of five shillings , each or thereabouts each year, the new tax to be used for the maintenance of highroads. The County -Councils of Lancashire and Cheshire have each decided that it is unjust and undesirable that such a tax should be levied, and it is probable that most other County Councils will come to the same con- clusion. We shall probably hear very little more about this tax. Even if it were right to tax cycles —about which there are two opinions—the tax should be used for the benefit of the whole country and not for one class only, as it would be if the wishes of the Warrington and Runcorn District Councili were followed.</p>
        <p>Ou Friday, at the Warrington Police Court, th e magistrates granted the first judicial separation of man and wife under the new Act of 1895. The parties in this case had only been married two years and during that time the defendant .had given his wife the magnificent sum of 16s. for maintenance, struck her, kicked her,and abused her in all manner of ways in addition to having squandered her sav- ings, amounting to £BO. Mr. P. Davies, who prose- cuted, stigmatised the defendant's conduct, as that of a beast and not of a man, while witnesses used similarly descriptive phrases with regard to him. The case was probably one of the worst ever brought into the Warrington Court and the magistrates:were by no means over severe in sending defendant for three months' hard labour, and granting his wife a separation and a maintenance order.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL MUSICIAN. Mr. R. Birkett Musgrove (organist and choirmaster Frodsham Parish Church, and conductor of Frodsham Musical Society) in the recent examination held at the Royal College of Organists, London, successfully passed in the paper word section. The examination in ques- tion is a very severe one, and Mr. Musgrove is to be heartily congratulated on his success. CONGREGATIONAL HOME MISSION WORK. On Sunday two excellent sermons were preached in the Bethesda Church by the Rev. W. J. S. Davies, of Nantwich. The rev. gentleman earnestly pleaded for liberal offertories,which were to be devoted to the funds of the Congregational Home Mission and Church Aid Society, which was accomplishing an important work. The collections realised a satisfactory sum. There were good congregations. RUNCORN CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR SOCIETIES. Tbe second of the united gatherings of the Christian Endeavour Societies of Runcorn was held in Bethesda schoolroom on Wednesday evening. Members of each of the societies were present. The proceedings were pre- sided over by the Rev. J. S. Simpson, who spoke a few words of welcome. Papers on various topics connected with the societies were read by Miss Eleatherington, NUBS Miles, Miss Booth, Mr. Wilson, and Mr. Barker. Mr. Gowanlock sang with much taste "Nazareth." During the evening light refreshments were served by members of the Bethesda Endeavour Society. RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. An interesting gathering held under the auspices of the above association took place on Saturday evening in the Brunner Guild Hall. There was a good attendance. The Rev. T. Bramall presided. The following pro- gramme was submitted :—Song, "Silly Tommy," Mr. A. Outhwaite (encored) ; song, "Riding on a load of hay," Miss Leathwood ; song, "Old Folks at Home," Miss Carter; song, "The cows arc in the clover," Miss F. Lunt (encored) ; song, "Could I," Miss Wilson ; song, " Tit for tat," Mrs. Temperley (encored) ; recita- tion, "Christmas Day in the Workhouse," Miss Davies ; and recitation, "Beautiful Snow," Mr. Hulse (encored). RUNCORN FRES LIBRARY COMMITTEE. Mr. William Handly presided at a meeting of this committee on Tuesday evening. There were also pre- sent Messrs. H. J. Grice, T. Riley, W. J. Cunningham, and J. D. Jones (librarian). It was decided that the thanks of the committee should be tendered Dr. Bowman for his lecture on "Books—their use and abuse," and also to Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P. for presiding on that occasion. It was further agreed that the Technical In- struction Committee should be thanked for allowing the use of the Institute for the course of lectures. The com- mittee recommended that three copies of Niekson's "History of Runcorn" be purchased. The librarian re- ported that he had received the following among other gifts: From Sir John T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., annual donation of £5; also facsimile ef the first issue of the "Daily News ; " Agent General of New Zealand, "New Zealand Official Year Book for 1894," and various pamphlets and papers descriptive of the colony. Accounts to the amount of £6 Os. 93. were passed for payment. The librarian's report for January showed that during the month 2,166 books had been issued, being a decrease of 4 in the reference department, and 117 in the lending department. Classified, the books distributed were as follows: Theology 37, philosophy 7, science 115, history. &amp;c., 149, poetry 26, fiction 1,597, miscellaneous 202, French literature 1, music 32. ALLEGED THEFT OF ROPE FROM • BARQUE. At Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning before Mr. E. Imison, a man of no fixed abode, named John Hedgececk, was charged with stealing 90 fathoms of six inch manilla hawser. value £s.from the barque "M. and C. Cann," lying at Weston Point, the property of Patrick Fitzgerald, master, on the previous night.—Captain Fitzgerald stated that the barque was lying in Weston Point basin. On Wednesday evening about seven o'clock, he caught the prisoner io the act of stealing the rope. Whilst he was securing Hedgececk, another man made his escape. On Tuesday the rope was coiled on the fore- castle, but the prisoner had placed it in a wheelbarrow on the dock side. The rope produced was only part of it, as it had been cut in two, and spoiled. Witness kept his hold on prisoner until assistance arrived, and he sub- sequently placed him in the custody of the police officer. Prisoner acknowledged the theft, stating that he had been employed by a man named Rowles. The case was remanded until Saturday in order that efforts might be made to arrest the accomplice.—A man named James Royse surrendered himself at the Police Station on Thurs- day morning. admitting having been with the prisoner. It was therefore decided to try the case this (Friday) morning instead of Saturday as previously decided upon. For NAME BROOCHES, soli silverd name brooches 100. (any name) Government stamped, Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DESERT'S, 10, CHURCH-STREET. LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Electro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- grart Watch, 6s. 11d.. etc.), on page 7 f this Paper. -Advt CONCERT AT THE BRUNNER GUILD hALL. There was but a small attendance at the Brunner Guild Hall, Runcorn, on Monday evening, when a con- cert for the benefit of workmen at present out of work, was given. The chair was occupied by Mr. F.. Imison, J.P., and he was supported on the platform by several members of the Runcarn Trades Council who had pro- moted the concert.—The Chairman, who was well re- ceived,said it was one of the saddest features of indus- trial life that men who were willing and anxious for work,could not find any. The causes of this were many and varied,gbut they all regretted that where a man was willing to earn broad for himself and family he could not have the opportunity of doing so. Referring to the state of trade he said he believed the prospects were brighter than they had been. They had passed through a distressing time, but it was a long lane that had no turning and he (the speaker) believed they could now see the end of this period of trade depression. (Ap- plause) The programme was then proceeded with. Songs were given by Miss flarrop. Miss Johnson, Miss Wilson, Messrs. Atherton. John Leathwood, Birkett, Shingler.and Worrall. Selections were well rendered by members of tne Volunteer Band, and Mr. F. Smith con- tributed mandoline solos. Miss Baker and Miss Geddes recited. and Miss Simkin and Mr. Morris, and Messrs. Worrall and Shingler sang duets. Mr. Grounds was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOATING CLUE.</p>
        <p>The second asnual general meeting-Oi the above club was held in the Guild Hall. Mr. Millinger took the chair. and submitted a financial statement for the year which was very satisfactory. The committee and officers re- tired in due course, and offered themselves again for re- election, with the exception of Mr. Millinger for the office of secretary. Mr. J. A. Smith, jun., thought that was the most fitting time for proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Millinger for the most able and effective manner in which he had performed the business of the club dur- ing his term of office. He thought he was only express- ling the wishes of the members in saying they would be pleased to convey in some more tangible form the ap- preciation of his services. Mr. A. Palin supported the vote of thanks which was carried unanimously. Sir R. M. Brooke, Bart., J.P., was re-elected president, and Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., F. J, Norman. J.P., E. Imison, J.P., J. Verdin, J.P., J. F. Hazlehurst, W. Collier, W. Rowland, C. Dut- ton. and H. Fitton, and Mrs. J. Hazlehurst were re- elected vice-presidents. Mr. A. Abel was elected secre- tary Mr. H. Johnson, treasurer ; Mr. F. Abel, captain; Mr. W. West, sub-captain ; Mr. S. Hughes, captain Wednesday members ; committee, Messrs. Millinger, Kniyeton, McNorton, Speakman, Buxton, T. Robinson and D. Coventry. It was decided that the member's subscriptions for 1898 should be increased to meet the extra expenditure which would be involved in the erec- tion of a more commodious boat-house, as the present housing accommodation for the boats was altogether in- adequate,and it was considered necessary that the house should be complete for the commencement of the season. —Mr. Sherratt said that he had just been informed by the secretary (outgoing) that April 15th was the anniver- sary day of the birth of the club, and he proposed that a social gathering be held on some approximate date to celebrate the formation of the club.—This was seconded, and it was decides to leave the matter in the hands of , the committee.—i vote of thanks was tendered the , Chairman.</p>
        <p>SABRED CONCARTS AT TIIB PUBLIC HALL. Both on Sunday afternoon and evening there were large audiences in the Public Hall, Rnncorn, on the occasion of two sacred concerts which had been pro- moted with the object of rendering assistance to Mr. George Lawton, of Runcorn, who met with an accident six years ago at Huskissan Dock, Liverpool, and who is still confined to bed through paralysis. As a result of this praiseworthy effort promoted by Messrs. G. W. Yates, W. Bylield, T. Outhwaite, C. C. Le Couteur, and W. Shaw (missionary), a good sum will be handed to the family of Mr. Lawton. The afternoon concert con- sisted of miscellaneous items. Under the conductorship of Bandmaster T. Outhwaite, the Runcorn Volunteer Band performed three selections. The solo part in " The Holy City" selection was creditably played by Private T. Knight. Two anthems entitled " Let every heart rejoice," and "Wake the song of Jubilee," were sung by the choir connected with the Runcorn branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen. Mr. Price conducted. Mrs. Temperley sang "The Better Land," and "My Bud in Heaven," and the other soloists Messrs. A. Shinaler, Wesley C. Cooke, and C. Atherton. The singing of each vocalist was greatly appreciated. Mr. C. C. Le Couteur played the pianeforte accompaniments, and Mr. W. Cooke presided at the organ. At niaht, the scripture cantata entitled "Faith Triumphant, or the healing of Naaman, which has previously been given at Bethesda Congregational Church, was repeated. Mr. Le Couteur conducted, and the cnaracters were impersonated as fol. lows : Queen of Syria, Miss Heatherington ; wife of Naaman," Miss Pope; Hebrew maid, Miss M. Heather- ington ; Iran (the prophet). Mr. E. Worrall ; Naaman (champion of Syria), Mr. W. Gowanlock ;Syrian priests, Messrs. W. Lunt, E. Lunt, D. Crossley, and F. Shingler. There was also an interpreting quartett consisting of INitises Hayes and Harrop, Messrs. J. Leathwood and Joseph Littler, and a grand chorus of Hebrews. Mr. W. Shaw (missionary) presided. The cantata was exceed- ingly well rendered, and reflected great credit on all who took cart. Mr. W. Cooke was again the organist, and Mr. W. Grounds presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>SWINE FEVER AT RUNCORN. Swine fever is reported to have broken out on the premises of four separate pig-keepers in Runcorn. In each case it has been found necessary to destroy a num- ber of pigs. THE ELECTRIC LIGHTING QUESTION. The Electric Lighting Committee of the Runcorn Urban Council met on Thursday morning at the Town Hall. Mr. G. H. Kirkham occupied the chair, After considering the statistics and information which had been forwarded for perusal, the committee were of the opinion that it was not feasible to embark on the project of lighting the streets of Runcorn by electricity at the present time, and adjourned the censideration of the matter sine dei. BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY. At a meeting ot this society on Wednesday evening, Miss Hallding read a very instructive paper on" Long- fellow." She gave a short sketch of Longfellow's life. and read a number of quotations from his works. A number of members expressed their appreciation of Miss Hallding's paper. Amongst those whe spoke were Mr. Simister (chairman), Mr. T. Sanders, Mr. J. Bisbrown, I and Mr. William Douglas. MARINERS' CHURCH MISSION, RUNCORN. Mr. Jones occupied the chair at the Monday evening entertainment in this mission. Songs were rendered by Misses A. Hill, C.Williamson, and M.Hill, Messrs.Bazley, T. Hazlehurst, and J. Rutter. The Misses A.and M. Hill and Masters R. A. Banner and A. G. Breadman contri- buted duets, and Messrs. J. Breadman, L. Johnson, R.A. Banner,and S.Johnson formed a quartette party. Read- ings were given by Mr. T. Sweetman and Miss M. Hill, and a sketch by Mr. J. Williamson. The accompanist was Mr. Gardiner. A pleasant evening was spent. OT. LUKE'S SUNDAY SCHOOL. The annual tea and meeting in connection with this Sunday school was held on Tuesday. A good number sat down to tea. Afterwards a large gathering listened to songs and recitations given by scholars and friends. The Rev. J. S. Simpson presided, and delivered a brief address in which he urged those present to take advan- tage of the Sunday school. At the close of an enjoyable evening's entertainment, Mr. Slade, superintendent of the school, proposed a vote of thanks to all who had con- tributed to the success of the evening. Mr. J. Corfe seconded, and it was passed unanimously. INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES. The annual meeting of the Runcorn and Frodsham District No. 9 was held on Wednesday in the Cocoa Rooms, Runcorn. The annual balance sheet showed an increase on the year of £1 58.5 d. The total funds avail- able for funeral claims was now £414 12s. 3d., and the total membership 474. The election of officers resulted as follows :—Mr. William Lockett, D.C.R. (Frodsham) ; Mr. James Sayle, D.D.R. (Runcorn) ; Mr. Joseph Booth, P.D.C.R. (Runcorn) ; Mr. James Lea, D.T. (Runcorn) ; and Mr. J. E. Warburton, D.S. (Runcorn). Mr. W. E. Hough (Runcern), and Mr. J. R. Jones (Runcorn), were appointed auditors. CAMDEN AND NELSON-STRERT SUNDAY SCHOOLS. The Rev. T. Brackenbury presided at the annual meet- ing of the officials and teachers at Camden and Nelson- street Sunday schools, held in Camden schoolroom on Monday evening. The :reports for tee year were read, from which it appeared that there was a marked im- provement in the attendance at Nelson-street schools. The election of officers for the ensuing year was then proceeded with. Messrs. P. Speakman, Beckett, and Harrison were re-elected superintendents of Camden Sunday school, Messrs. S. Littler and .1. Naylor (secre- taries). and Messrs. Ditchfield and J. Naylor (auditors). For Nelson-street school, Messrs. J. Bayley, W. Lambert, and T. Millinger were appointed superintendents, and Messrs. J. Littler and J. Hughes, secretaries. THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. Agentertainment was given to the students attending this institute on Saturday evening. The proceedings were presided over by Mr. E. Marshall, the secretary, and there was a fair attendance of pup,ls. The pro- gramme was of a miscellaneous description. In addi- tion to a number of songs being sungia lantern exhibition of slides illustrative of scenes in Switzerland and the Mediterranean was given. Mr. Robinson manipulated the lantern, and Mr. Wilding explained the views. The vocalists were Miss Stevens, Mr. Branch, and Mr. Crosby. Instrumental duets (violin and piano) were contributed by Messrs. Arthur and Charles Timmins, jun. Mr. William Cooke fulfilled the duties of accompanist. MISHAP TO A RUNCORN BOUND VESSEL. Early on Wednesday morning a collision occurred off Crosby lightship between the Welsh coasting steamer Eilian, inward bound for Runcorn, and the Leyland steamer Lisbon,outward bound for Oporto. The weather at the time was somewhat hazy,and a strong breeze was blowing. The impact happened at a spot almost oppo- site the lightship, and both vessels, after colliding, re- mained fastened together for a few minutes, during which a boy and three of the crew of the Eilian clam- bered on board the Lisbon. The captain of the Welsh coaster, seeing that the damage to his vessel, she having had her bowsprit carried away and her bows smashed, was not of such a severe nature to warrant herlabandon- ment, ordered the men to return,which two of them did. The Eilian was then taken towards Formby and beached. The Lisbon also sustained damage to her boxes, and she was put back and docked. The Eilian was taken to Rock Ferry and beached. No loss of life resulted from the mishap. HALTON-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH TEA MEETING. There were about 150 persons present at the annual congregational tea meeting in connection with the Wes- leyan Church, flalton-road, on Monday evening, held in Mill Brow scheelroom. The tea tables were presided over by Mesdames Frith, W. George, Handley, Brimelow, Garner, Miss Corns and Miss Pope. The succeeding entertainment was of a very successful character. The choir capitally rendered two glees, and Miss Clara Tay- lor sang a solc., with violin obligato by Mr. George Frith. Songs were also rendered by Miss Pope,Miss K. Edwards, Messrs. H. Johnson, E.lgar Johnson and William Coven- try. Duets were sung by Messrs. Robert Yates and W. Coventry, and Messrs. William and David Coventry .Miss Davies recited, and Miss Austin contributed a reading. The chair was occupied by the ftev. W. George, who made a statement with reference to the financial position of the church. MARRIAGE OP M. HENRY COWARD. At the Parish Church, Runcorn, on Monday, Mr. Henry Coward, son of the late Mr. Henry Coward of the Derby Arms Hotel, builder and contractor, and Miss Annie Shawidaughter of the late Mr. James Shaw.house furnisher, Bridge-st., were united in the bonds of holy matrimony. Owing to a midden bereavement, the event was observed very quietly. Miss Coward, sister of the bridegroom acted as bridesmaid, and Mr. T. Shaw was best man. The bride was given away by her brother. The Rev. H. B. Firth was the officiating clergyman. Numerous presents were received including the follow- ing :—Mrs. Shaw,house furniture; Mrs.Coward,cheque ; Mr.and Mrs.F.Coward,tea service; Miss Coward,reading lamp ; Mr. W. E. Darlington, pair of pictures; Miss Lewis, copper kettle; Mr. Crosby, timepiece; Mr. T. Shaw, kitchen utensils; Mr. and Mrs. Dykes, vases; Mrs. and Miss Bowyer, glass dishes and cake stand ; Mr. A. Shaw, dinner service and coal; Mr. and Mrs. Abel, toilet set ; Mr. and Mrs. Eccleston. picture; Mr. and Mrs. Mort, knives and forks; Mr. J. West and Mr. A. Gleave, marble timepiece; Mr. T. Watson, wash tub: Mrs. A. Riley, glasses; Mrs. Hughes, set of jugs ; and Miss Wright, glasses and jugs. ST. Josses PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. RUNCORN. The annual meeting of the St. John's congregation took place on Monday. Tea was served at 6 p.m. after which the usual meeting was held and presided over by Mr. James Pritchard, The Rev. 'I. G. Owen M.A. (Liverpool) and Mr. E. J. Hughes (Birkenhead) were present as delegates from the Presbytery and having ex- amined the books reported favourably as to the general condition of the church and its financies. Short ad- dresses were also delivered by Mr. Pritchard, Mr. F. Hewitt, Mr. Baxter and Mr. Warburton church officers. An interesting feature of the proceedings, was the pre- sentation of an illuminated address (the work of Mr. rovah,Runcorn,and which was remarkably well executed and much admired). The presentation was made by Mr. Pritchard on!behalf of the church and congregation to Mr. James Kirkham who has been connected with the church and echool for 35 years,and has occupied for many years important positions, and the friends thought the occa- sion of his removal to Rhyl a suitable opportunity of showing their appreciation of services rendered.to the cause by Mr. Kirkham and his family. Mr.i.Kirkham suitably acknowledged the gift after which the meet- ing was closed by singing the doxology. OPENING OP A GUILD PARLOUR AT RUNCORN. The formation of the Wesley Guild in connection with Brunswick-street Sunday, school has been such a success that the membership now numbers about 200, and the want has been felt of a guild parlour. A committee was appointed to consider the matter, and as the result of their labours, a comfortably furnished parlour has been provided, and was opened for the first time on Thursday evening. There was a large attendance to witness the opening ceremony. The Rev. T. Brackenbury opened the proceedings. He supposed the Methodist Church had had premises in Runcorn for same four or five generations, but never until that night Mad Methodism possessed a church par- lour. It had been provided without incurring any debt, and without the trustees being called upon to pay any expenses. It was for the purpose of being used for read- ing and recreation, and he should be equally correct in saying for social enjoyment. He roust not forget to mention that it was going to be used as a girls' parlour. He hoped it would promote the welfare of the church and congregation with which they were connected. (Applause.) He then called upon Mrs. F. J. Norman to declare the parlour open.—Mrs. F. J. Norman in a short speech thanked the ladies and gentlemen who had helped the committee to furnish the rcom with gifts of articles of furniture. She had very great pleasure in declaring the guild parlour open, and wished it every success.— A vote of thanks was passed to the committee, and those who had contributed towards the furnishing of the room, on the motion of Mr. Edward Taylor. seconded by Mr. A. R. Norman,and supported by Mr. Rawlinson. Mrs. Simkin responded. An enjoyable evening was afterwards spent, the proceedings being enlivened by several musical items.</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES Hanct £2 189.6d.' Treadle £44s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durrox 16 and 18. Sankey-et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCILS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Mclntyre,J.P., occupied the chair,and there were also present Admiral Parry,Messrs. Joseph Knowles, J. Weir, T. Lewis, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, G. Pilling, C. W. Davenport, W. Turner, W. Roylance, W. Pickering, S. Woodward, J. M. Williams, J. Brandreth, H. E. Heywood, T. Darlington, C. Moon- field, T. Baxter, C.C., J. Baguley, H. Talbot, J.P., and Mr. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), and Mr. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>ROAD IMPROVEMENT AT WESTON. The Local Government Board have made an order in- vesting he Rural Council with certain powers regarding South-road, Weston, declaring the expenses incurred by the Council in the execution of those powers to be special expenses.—Mr. Chadwick prc posed that the Council advertise for tmders for the construction of a sewer be- fore making the road.—Mr. Wright seconded,and it was agreed to.</p>
        <p>A CLERK'S COMPENSATION.</p>
        <p>The Lords' Commissioners of Her Majesty's treasury wrote with reference to the claims of Mr. Roscoe for compensation for loss of office as Clerk of the late High- way Board of East Bucklow. Their lordships under- stood that Mr. Roscoe held his late office for more than ten years at an annual salary of £lO5 inclusive of a sum of £3O paid to him through the late Board's surveyor. Subject to any observations which the Council might have to offer their lordships accordingly proposal:to award Mr. Roscoe a compensation allowance payable by the Council of 13s. &amp;l. per annum. Their lordships de- clined to admit the bill of costs included by Mr. Roscoe in his statement of claim—ln answer to a question by Mr. BranJreth, the Clerk said he did not think they could do other than pay an annual sum, under the Act of 1894.—1 t was decided to pay the ameunt as fixed by the Treasury.</p>
        <p>MAIN ROADS. The Clerk to the Macclesfield Rural District Council forwarded a copy of a resolution passed by them, and asked the RuNcorn Council if they approved of it, to make a similar representation to the County Council. The resolution alluded to, considered the present time appropriate for approaching the County Council, desir- ing them to hand over the repair of the main roads to the Rural District Councils, stating that it was impossi- ble that any county surveyor coula keep a proper check on the expenditure going on daily on the main roads throughout the county seeinc, that there were 466 mileo of rural main roads, and 156 miles of urban main roads, and that the most suitable bodies to control the expendi- ture were the Rural District Councils ; also that the ex- penditure of the rural main roads had advanced frorr ' £24,755, or £53 per mile unaer the management of the highway boards, to £44,287 or £B7 per mile per annum under the County Council, or an increase of £19,532 per annum.—On the motion of Mr. Woodward, seconded by Mr. Williams, it was decided that the communication should lie on the table.</p>
        <p>RELSBY WATER SUPPLY. Mr. Heywood submitted the report of the committee appointed to consider the question of a water supply to Helsby by the He!shy and District Water Company. It stated that it be a recommendation to the District Coun- cil that the Clerk be instructed to write and inform the Company that the Council did not think that the Local Government Board would allow the scale of charges as submitted by them,as supplying by meter would natur- ally tend to restrict the free use of water ; that the Council were, however, quite willing to request the Local Government Board to hold an inquiry on the un- derstanding that the Company stood by their offer to contribute the sum of £5 therefor, and the Company undertaking that such plans and specifications of the proposed scheme as would be required at a Local Government Board inquiry, should be prepared ; also that it be a recommendation to the District Council that they accept plans showing the pipe lines of the proposed scheme on a scale of 50" to the mile.—Mr. George C. Taylor wrote as'aing whether he was to understand that the District Council were going to grant wayleaves to the Water Company, or failing that, how did the Coun- cil intend to find him a water supply ? Be thought it would have only been fair if the district councillor for Helsby had spoken up in favour of a ratepayer who de- sired to supply water to his house at his own cost, and without prejudice to his neighbours.—Messrs. Potts, Potts, and Gardner wrote stating that they would be glad to receive a reply to their last letter, and to hear that the information which had been supplied by the Water Co.to the District Council and their offer to refer the charges to be made for supplying cottage property to arbitration was considered satisfactory, and that no further difficulty would be placed in the way of the company extending their mains, so as to provide the whole of Helsby with a proper supply of water.—Mr. Heywood stated that the secretary of the Water Com- pany called upon him the previous day, but as he (Mr. Heywood) was out, he left a message stating that there had been some misunderstanding between the commit- tee of the company. The committee thought that if the Local Government Board held an inquiry it would do away with the necessity of a plan. He suggested that their Clerk write to the secretary of the company inform- ing him that it would be quite necessary to have a plan at the Local Government Board inquiry.—Mr. Brandreth in referring to the letter of Mr. Taylor, said if a gentle- man built a house at the top of the hill, he could not expest the ratepayers to carry water to him at their own expense.—Mr. Heywocd thought the Parish Council of Helsby might call a meeting of ratepayers to express an opinion on the matter,as to whether they desired to have a water supply of their own or to be contented to have this half supply proposed, and at an exhorbitant price. —Mr. Brandreth said the inhabitants had expressed an opinion, and were against taking the matter up at all. With respect to the scheme of the Water Company, the gentlemen who were promoting it intended primarily to supply their own houses. It was but for the benefit of 'a fE vr, and not for the ratepayers generally. Mr. 'Tay- lor wanted water at his own house, but the other ratepayers objected to paying for water going up to his house. There were only one or two places that required a water slimly. He knew one gentleman who had expended £6O or £7O in order to supply his house with water, and had now got a good supply. He (Mr. Bran- dreth) and friends, had gone to the expense of about £5OO, and when people went to an expense of that sort, they did not want to pay for a ratepayer's scheme. He added that he did not think the Council should grant the way leaves until it had been deeided what charges were to be made for the water. He understood that the Company wished to pay 15 per cent, on the capital invested, and he (Mr. Brandreth) thought the Company would have to charge the public too much for the water to pay that dividend. If they would put down a scheme to satisfy the Local Government Board he was perfectly satisfied that they should have the way-leaves.—The Chairman was certain the matter had been before them quite long enough, and he thought the better course t pursue would be for a meeting of the ratepayers to decide either for or against the scheme of the company. —Mr. Roylance moved that the question be referred to the Helsby Parish Council, and that the Chairman of it call a meeting of ratepayers to consider the matter.— Mr. Bradford seconded the proposition.—Mr. Heywood said he would be prepared to support in that Council ' what the ratepayers decided upon.—Mr. Brandreth said he should protest against the wayleaves being granted until the company's charges for water were decided upon.—Mr. Wright said they were in possession of the scale of charged, and he was prepared to support Mr. Heywood. Mr. Heywood, Mr. Brandreth, and Mr. Woodward having again spoken. the proposition was pot to the meeting, and was carried, only Mr. Brandreth voting against.</p>
        <p>EPIDEMIC OF MEASLES. Dr. Adams reported that measles had broken out at Manley in epidemic form, and as the number of known cases amounted to 40 and only two had presented them- selves at school he suggested that the schools be closed for a period of three weeks. This was agreed to. SCARLET FEVER. Dr. Adams in his report stated that three cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Halton; one at Helsby ; two at Moore; one at Keckwick ; one at Preston o'th Hill; and three he'll cases at Frodsham. This concluded the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SERIOUS FALL DOWN A QUARRY AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS FALL DOWN A QUARRY AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>A NIGHT OF SUFFERING. George Bills, a cooper at Weston Chemical works, and residing in Bankes-row, Weston, received seri- ous injuries by falling into the quarry of the Run- corn Hill Red and White Sandstone Company on Thursday night. From inquiries made. it appears that at 7.30 p.m. he lett home with the intention of going to Runcorn to post a letter. He took the way through the fields, and it seems from the state- men he has made, on reaching the quarry and opposite the shooting range of the Runcorn volun- teers, he pulled off his boot, and placed it on the wall which fences the quarry from the path. His boot evidently fell over, and in trying to secure it he must have slipped and fallen to the bottom of the delph a depth of between 30 and 40 feet. He then seems to have crawled along a circuitous path, and reached the top, where he was found still alive on Friday forenoon. The poor fellow must have suffered intense agony throughout the night from the injuries he received, and it is almost miraculous that he was not killed outright. His wife and friends were searching for him on Thurs- day, but it was not until 10.30 on Friday morning that the efforts of a search party which had been organised at the works, were rewarded with suc- cess. He was found on the edge of the quarry man exhausted condition, and hilden from view by a hedge. A number of workmen had been engaged in the quarry from early morning within a stone's throw from where the man was found, but he ap- pears to have been unable to shout to them. It was found that his loft foot was scattered, the bones protruding through the skin; that he had lost a considerable quantity of blood,and his legs and face were badly bruised. P.C. Morris, a member of the St. John Ambulance Association, rendered first aid, and messengers were despatched for medical assist- ance. Drs. Ormsby, McLennan and Delany,of Run- corn, were soon in attendance, and they considered it advisable that the injured limb should be ampu- tated, which operation was successfully performed the same afternoon. The man is 28 years of age, and hopes are entertained of his recovery.</p>
        <p>ViIFROM THE MOMENT OF BIRTH USE CUTICURA SOAP.—It is not only the purest, sweetest, and most re- freshing of nursery soaps, but it contains delicate emol- lient properties obtained from Cuticura (ointment), the great skin cure, which purify and beautify the skin,and remove skin blemishes, occasioned by imperfect cleans- ing and the continued use of impure soaps. Guaranteed absolutely pure by analytical chemists of the highest standing whose certificates of analysis accompany each 1 tablet.</p>
        <p>ONE MONTH ONLY.—In order to popularize the Royal Club Stationery. a smooth-finished vellum Paper, with Envelopes to match, I shall offer during the month of February only,ls. Boxes for 10d.—Jourt S. GREE.r. Stationer. ac., 34. Bridge-st., and at 23, Buttermarket-Bt.—Adv;. HOUSEHOLD LINENS, hemmed and marked: free of charge. and. W. Dutton, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Adut.</p>
