      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT</p>
        <p>(Continued from page 6.) THURSDAY. Mr. G. BALFOUR, the Irish Chief Secretary, stated in the House of Commons on Thursday that the Govern- ment intended to bring in a bill this session to reduce the judicial establishment in Ireland. Mr. A. J.. BALFOUR. in reply to Sir W. H. HOULDS- WORTH, said it was not true that the Government had refused to co-operate with Germany in furthering an international monetary agreement by reopening the Indian mints. In answer to Sir E. A. BARTLETT, Mr. G. CURZON stated that the pledge given by the Russian Government in 1886 not to occupy Corean territory under any cir- cumstances whatsoever was still binding. Mr. A. J. I3ALFoun, in moving the resolution dealing with the discussion of the Estimates, said t'le view of the Government was to improve discussion in Supply, which they wished to regard as an important Parliamen- tary function. If there was a strong feeling against the proposal to limit the discussion of the Estimates to 20 Parliamentary days it would be abandoned, but the Government could not consent to half of the proposals being adopted. The resolution having been read from the Cnair, the debate was adjourned. Mr. RITCHIE, in asking leave to introduce a bill to facliltate the construction of light railways in Great Britain, explained that it was now the view ot the Government that if these railways were to be made in great numbers and in the districts in which they were most needed aid must be given both by the locality and by Parliament. The Government proposed that grants should be made to the extent of £1,000,000 for the con- struction of these railways. Mr.Rrrcrox afterwards brought in a bill to amend the Dis Pases of Cattle Act, 1894. The object of the bill is to make the present restrictions on the importation of foreign cattle permanent The bill was read a first time.</p>
      </div>
