        <p>FIRE AT COCKHEDGE MILLS, -- HEAVY DAMAGE. A very serious tire broke out at Messrs. Rigby and Armitage's Mills at Warrington, on Thursday morning about 5.40, and although it was sub- dued in little over two hours, it is estimated that upwards ef £15,000 .damage has been sustained. Fortunately at the time the fire broke out, there were very few employes in the mill, and thus there was no panic with all its serious consequences. The fire was caused by an employe who was lighting the gasses,prior to the commencement of the work, accidentally igniting some of the inflammable material in the top storey. In a moment the whole room was ablaze. The firebell was at once rung, and the steam fire engine " Major" was quickly on the spot under the charge of Superintendent Turner and members of the fire Brigade. The larger fire engine "Captain "was later on also brought to the scene of the conflagration. Abundance of water was at hand, and in the reservpirs near the Cheshire Lines Railway, and the firemen by about six o'clock were playing on the burning building. The floor of the room which was on the top storey of the building was fireproof, and. this, and the efforts of the firemen prevented the fire from. spreading to the rooms below. It was, however, found to be impossible to save the top storey. At half-past six, the roof fell in, and immediately afterwards, the flames shot out to a great height, and could be seen all over Warrington and far into the country. The heat from the flames was intense. The enormous amount of water, however,which was poured on the fire gradually took effect, and about eight o'clock, the fire was completely extinguished, though the fire engine was kept in the works for some hours afterwards. The room in which the fire had occurred was completely wrecked, and nothing but the bare walls were lett. The outer walls of the room are 20 inches thick, and the root was sup- ported by iron girders, but these were twisted into various shapes by the enormous heat. In the room which was destroyed, there were five pairs of mules and some very fine ring frames. Great assistance was afforded by a large number of the workmen under the charge of Mr. Hibbert, who used the fire appliances belonging to the mill with good effect. A large body of police were also present, under command of Mr. L. Talbot, the Chief Constable,and they were able to keep the crowd who assem- bled, at a respectable distance from the burning building, and thus prevented them from hindering the work of the firemen. The damage is covered by insurance in several fire insurance companies, including the Queen's and the Royal. A consider- able amount of damage has, of course, been done to the machinery and stock in the lower rooms by the volumes of water that were poured into the mill, but it was, fortunately, not serious enough to make a prolonged stoppage of work neces- sary, and during Thursday a notice was is- sued to the employes to the effect that work would be recommenced yesterday (Friday) morning. As about 1,500 persons are employed at these mills a prolonged stoppage would, of course, have been seriously felt in Warrington. After the fire, the building presented a very forlorn apbearance, and during the day the mills were visited by a large number of spectators. There was unfortunately one person injured during the efforts to put on!, the flames, as an overlooker named Eckersley had the misfortune to fall on some of the machinery and cut his knee so severely that he had to be taken tc the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>- 7 4;</p>
        <p>4ik 'll</p>
