        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), and Alder-</p>
        <p>man Wright.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.— John Quinn was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-st. on Thursday afternoon.—P.O. Gibbs proved the case and mention -d that defendant had tried to obtain drink at the "Lower Angel" and had been refused.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs or in default of payment to go to gaol for 14 days. AN ADJOURNED NEGLECT CASE.--EllPri Evans, 8, Jolley's- yard,appeared to answer the cnarge of child neglect which was preferred against her and her huibana on the 17th January and adjourned to give them time to reform.—lnspector McAteer. N.S.P C.C., stated that since the adjournment he had visited the house several times and the condition of the children was greatly improved. They were now comfortable. He wished to withdraw the summons.—The magistrates agreed to this course and expressed satisfaction at the result of the proceedings. MoxDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman). Aldcrm an Harrison,</p>
        <p>and Mr. F. Monk's..</p>
        <p>THROWING HALF A BRICK THROUGH A WlNDOW.—Thomas Flannery, a man with 27 convictions against him, was charged with committing wilful damage to a window of the "Lower Angel," on Saturday, to the extent of £2 158.—John Howard, manager of the' Lower Angel" sail on Saturday night defen4ant was twice ejected and ten minutes after the last occasion something came flying through the glass win- dow. Defendant hid thrown half a brick and had had drink but was not drunk.—P.C. Lewis proved arresting de- fendant who admitted the offence.—The Chief Constable stated that the defendant might easily have been charged with manslaughter as the half brick which he threw struck a woman ma the head. She hal in consequence to be medi- cally attended.—Defendant was sent to gaol for two months hard labour. BEGGING.—Henry Holden, aged 63, was charged with beg- ging in Sankey-qtreet on Sunday night.—The case was proved and defendant discharged on promising to leave the town. LICENSE TRANSPERS.—The following transfer, of public- house licenses were granted :—" The Pelican." from Fair- field Turner to William Chalmers ; "The Coach and Horses," Atherton's Quay to Fairfield Turner ; " The Three Pigeons," from William Chalmers to:Richard Baker; "The Roan Cow,' from John Hollingworth to John Henry Hackney. AN UNSANITARY BAKEEIOUSE.—Thomas Dewitt, was charged with having on the 11th February, his bakehouse in Oliver at.. in an unsanitiry conditiou.—lnspector Nor- man said that he visit ,d the premises in question and found the floor a quarter of an inch deep in filth created by the manufacture of confectionery. The benches wers also very dirty. He had cautioned the defendant about it and he bad since cleaned the place.—The maqistratea said that they thought that dirt in premises where the preparation of food was going on. was very dangerous, and imposed a penalty of 20s. and costs (P.l 7s. in all). TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in ths chair), and Dr _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Smith.</head>
        <p>Smith.</p>
        <p>THEFT BY MEANS OF A. TRICK.--Arthur Lee, labourer, of Howley, was charged with having stolen by means of a trick, on the 15th February, a wire cage. v.due 45., the property of Isaac saves. gas stoker, of Howley-lane.—The Chief Con- stable stated that on the day in question complainant had a number of rats in the cage, and after taking them out, he washed and cleaned it and then put it away in his kitchen. His son John, aged four years, gained possession of t'le cage and took it into the street to play with. Whilst outside prisoner aecosted the child and said " Here, I will have this cage, and here is a penny." He thereupon gave the child one halfpenny, and impudently walked away with the cage. The police were then informed of the affair, and P.C. Delahay vislted prisoner's house. On making inquiries the constable received the following impertinent reply from the pri oner, "I bought the thing fairly, and it is going to remain where It is." As far as he (the Chief Constable) knew, prisoner had borne a good character, and it wis rattler a trivial case for which to send him to goal. He was of opinion that a nominal penalty would meet the case.—Prisoner, who pleaded guilty, was fined ss. including costs. THURSDAY.—Before Mr. S. Chandley (chairman), Alderman Sistton, and Lieutenant Colonel --igway.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL CAREs.—The following persons were fined for not sending their children regularly to school .—William Thomp- son, 63, Pierpoint-street, fined 2s. Fd. ; Martin Care!, Pump Yard, School-street. 2s. 6d. ; Joseph Newris. 135, Orford- lane, 2s. 61 ; Joseph Ellis, 19, Howard-street, ss.—Mr. W. Jones, school attendance offic.r. prosecuted. WHDNF.SDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairlough (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. H. Fleming and J. rGeddes.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CASE OF INTIMIDA:TION—.LMichaeI Glynn WM charged with being drunk and disorderly in Sankey-st. at 7.30 on Tuesday night.—P.O. Berry stated that at the time in question defendant was in Mr. Booth's shop, Market Gate. and at first refused to go out. Eventually he went but entered the shop of Mr. Toft, tobacconist. where he asked for an ounce of "nail-rod." The girl in the shop, aged 12 years, served and he refused to pay saving it was not full weight. He reached over the counter and took two ounces. Wit ess then went in and arrested defendant. The girl was I frightened.—The Chief Constable staed that this was a bad ! case, and they had had many complaints of men going to shops and terrotising wom.n.—lt was defendant's second offence, and he was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. G. Comber (chairman) Mr. S. _ Armitage, Mr. W. Tinsley and Mr. W. Mort imer. _</p>
        <p>INTEMPERANCE —The following persons were fined for being drunk at varies times and places : Richard Hines, Sankey, 2s. 6d.; William Moody, Sankey, 2s. 6d.; John Cain Burtonwood 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>A WOMEN'S QUARREL.—Sarah Williams. Fairzlough-street, Burtonwood. was charged with assaulting and heating Mary Ellen Wood on the 10th February in Burtonwood—Mr. Davies pleaded guilty on behalf of the defendant.— Mrs. Wood said that on the day in question she was coming up the street where defendant lived and saw her combing -her hair. As complainant reached her the defendant struck her in the face and then twined her hand in complainant's hair and pulled out a handful.--Mrs. Houghton, William Bannister. and Ellis Edwards, corroborated.—Mr. Davies stated that he thought evidence for the defence was true but all the wit- nesses only spoke of a quarrel at 10 o'clock while he was in- structed to defend in a quarrel which took place at 9.15. At the time complainant went to defendants house an ent, red the yard where she swore at defendant. This caused the row which took place.—Ali e Tickle, said that she saw complain- ant at 9.15 enter Mr. Williams' yard and after some words pull her dress off. They then had a fiqht in the street.— Alice Gerrard. a girl of 14. supported Mrs, Tickle's statement. —Mrs. Williams was also summoned for refusing to go in her house when requested by the police and to this charge Mr. Davies pleaded guilty.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs and bound over to keep the peace for three months. CYCLISTS FINED,--John Highton,Croft,and Leonard Miller, were fined is. and costs for riding a bicycle on the f )otpath at Croft. DOG AT LAIIOE —George Jones, was fined Is. and costs for allowing a dog to be at large in Croft on the 16th February.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDlY.—Before Mr. F. J. Norman (chairman), and Mr. IL Imison.</p>
        <p>DOG AT LARGE.—Frederick Sadler. 107, Mersey-road, Widnes, was summoned for fallowing his dog to be at large without collar or muzzle in the Holloway, Runcorn, and was fined 2s. 6d. and c)sts. FAILING TO REPORT SFIEEP SCAB—HEAVY FIN. George Walton, shepherd, Rattan, was summoned for har- ing on the' 3rd Feb.. at Norton, been in possession of one hundred sheep which were suspected of sheep scab, and 'ln-</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION AND COUGII.—SOreneSS and 'Dryness tickling and irritation, inducing cough and affecting the voice. For these symptoms use Epps's Glycq.ine Jujubes. In contact with the glands at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, the Glycerine in these agreeable con- fections becomes actively healing. Sold only in boxes, 7,14., tins is. lid., labelled "JAMES EPPi 3z; CO., Ltd., Homce.)- pathic Chemists, London." Dr. Moore, in his work on "Nose and Throat Diseases," Bays: "The Glycerine Jujubes prepared by James Epps and Co., are of undoubted service as a Curative or palliative agent," while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat and Ear Infirmary, writes : "After an extended trial, I have found your Glycerine Jujubes of considerable benefit in almost all forms of throat disease."—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22, 1896.</p>
        <p>lawfully neglected to give notice of it to the police. The de fendmt admitted not having reported the matter, but stated that he was not aware that the animals were suffering from trie disease—P.S. Benfield stated that on the 3rd inst he saw a flock of sheep in Norton Park. He noticed mat they were rubbing themselves very much, and went and inspected them. He found that the animals had scratched a great deal of wool from their backs and were in bad condition. The sheep were examined on the 6th and 14. h inst. by veterinary surgeons who certified them as suffering from sheep scab. Many sheep on the adjoining farm of Kr. Houghton's had got the same disease,and this was attributed to coming in contact with the flock in charge of the defendant.—Mr. Ferguson, veterinary surv,eon. Warrington, and inspector under the Contagious Diseases (Anima's) Act, also gave evidence.— For the defence, Mr. Bate, veterinary surgeon, Frodsham, said that on the 4th February he was called in to examine the sheep at Norton Park. He saw symptoms indicating sheep scab, but failed to find insects. Defendant asked him whether it was sheep scab, b it he deferred expressing an opinion until the following day wire.a he again examined them. He then found that it WAS sheep scab, but it was not fully dev,loded.—ln answer to Superintendent Okell, wit- ness admitted that the defendant should have reported the matter to the police.—Defendant stated th he never saw a case of sheep scab in his life. He was ignot ant of what had been the matter with them.—The Chairman said when he told the defendant that he was liable to a penalty or £5OO he would see the position he Irld out himself in. He had de- cided to impose a line of £5 and costs, and they hoped for the future the defeniant would be very careful. The fine which amounted to £7 17s. 6•1. wis paid. AN UNLICENSED Do—Charles Lever, 52, Heath-road, • Runcorn, was fined Es. and costs for having on the 4th Feb. kept a certain,dog without a license.—P.C. Kendrick proved the case. _ _ _</p>
        <p>PROFANE LANGUAGE. —John Price, grocer, Halton-read, was summoned for using obs2ene and profane language on the 11th February.-51r. Burton on behalf of the defendant pleaded guilty.—P.C. Wylie stated th 3 cue, and a fine of ss. and costs was imposPd. 4 DRIVING WITHOUT RElNS—Harry Yates, a youth, residing with his parentr, in Lightburn-stree% was summoned for having on the 29th Janu try drivea a lurry without reins P.C. Juett gave evidence as to stopping the defendant who gave him a wrong name and address,—Defendant was fined ss. and costs. . . .</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S THREAT.—CIara Worthington, 26. English Row, was summoned by Annie Elizabeth Hillidge for using threats to her on the 13th February.— rho complainant stated that on the date 'named the defendant came out to her house and said she wo id 'do" for her before she had finished with her.—Corroborative evidence was given by Annie May. After the defendant and Mrs. Bazley had given evidenr.:e, the magistrates bound the defendant over to keep the peace for six months, and pay the costs. A BOATMAN AND Tits EMPLOYER. —James Ashcroft, boat- man on the " Bertie," belonging to qr. R. Abel, of Runcorn, was summoned for using threats towards Richard Abel.—Mr. Burton, solicitor, Runcorn, appeared on behalf of the com- plainant, and stated that since the summons was issued the defendant had seen Mr. Abel, and expressed his sorrow for what had occurred. Mr. Abel therefore wished him (Mr. Burton) to withdraw the summons, as he dTd not wish to press the case agahast the defendant.—The case was tberefore wi hdrawn.</p>
        <p>PLAYING IN THE STREETS.—Richard Buckley (13), William Cawley (14), Witham Jones (12), John Stanway (13), William- Brock (15), and Pr,derick Rowlands (14), of Shaw-et., were sumrnoned for having on the 6th Pel4ruary played football in Lowlands-raid—P.C. Wylie and P.C. Wright spoke to seeing the defendants playing in the streets. They had received complaints of the defendants conduct.—The lads denied that they were playing football but admitted playing "kick-cap." Each defendant was fined the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS should scqune the "Pocket Diary and Companion," issued by the well-known Sports Manu- facturers, GEORGE G. BussEr &amp; Co„ Peckham, London. All</p>
        <p>the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges, 6d.; postage, fd. If you are not satisfied, G. G. B. Co. will return the price.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. HUNSLET. There was considerable confidence amongst Warrington players and partisam that their favourites would be able to get the better of Hunslet in the return, as they did in the first match. When a few minutes before starting time Barber put in an appearance at Bank Quay,confid- once was increased, and the team,as picked, accordingly made the journey co Hunslet, viz :—Heesom ; Carey, Barber, Cross, Lawless; James T. Bate, Foden ; Nevins. Taylor, Thornton, A.ndrews, Swift, Saunders, Hill, Fair- hurst. On the otner hand Hunslet team had undergone</p>
        <p>some changes, the most notable being the substitution of Connor for the injured Hannah, and the inclusion of Bestow of Leeds, in place of Fletcher. The following were the Hunslet players : W. Goldthorpe ; Connor, A. Goldthorpe, Robinson, Wright; Lapping, Bestow; Kaye, Walsh, Deacon, Mawson, Leach, Ruby, Mosley, Eno. There was only a moderate attendance to begin witb but as the game proceeded the gate improved. Hill having got the ball in motion, the Warrington for- wards were too smart to allow an adequate return and the defence were put on their metal at the very outset. Passing by the home three-quarters relieved the tension and fetched play to the other end. They were beginning to peg away rather intently at Heesom's charge, but before that custodian was called on Barber surprised the " natives " with racing grandly away from within the Warrington 25 to a similar position in Hunslet ground, all efforts to intercept him having been futile. When being hemmed in on touch he cleverly kicked the ball over the heads of them in front of hint, but the kick was rather strong and W. Goldthorpe was on it before him and returned it. The spectators were very unselfish in applauding Barber for this fine performance. Again in close quarters Hunslet were making things dubious for Warrington, and when matters were about at their worst for Robin- son had worked his way close to the try line, Bar- ber again came to the fore, this time in effecting a fine tackle of the would be scorer. A. Goldthorpe during the pressure was another element of danger, for he kept dedging about for a good position, and was being well fed. Twice he essayed drops at goal, and on each occa- sion narrowly missed lowering Warrington colours. In a sense, these attempts saved Warrington, for had tries been tried for they would probably come off,for Hunslet I were doing all the pressure. So much for having a good ' kicking reputation. The defence shown by Warrington was all that could be desired, Cross in especial bringing off some splendid tackling. Seeing the strong defence against them Hunslet went in for large and sometimes random shots for goal, but neither the penalty given them nor their mark kicks brought them any result. Consequently there was a good deal of exchange kicking going on,and at length an opening was found, for Carey failed to hold one and Deacon easily snatched up and ran in without opposition. It was not a very difficult position. but A. Goldthorpe was decidedly out of it on Saturday with his place kick, for much to the disap- paintment of the spectators,he proved himself an unsafe pilot with the ball. A prettily combined movement by the Warrington forwards brought the play from half- way where it had rested for sometime after the drop out to Hunslet 25, and as it proved they made much better use of their oppertunity. Scrimmaging the packs to Bate's advantage, that player made a pass to Lawless, and before the homesters were aware where the ball really had passed to, Lawless grounded it behind the pests. Heesom took good care of the place and . had little difficulty in putting Warrington ahead. The effect on both teams was very apparent for whilst it nerved Warrington to yet greater efforts it unhinged Hunslet whose play became of a more erractic and spasmodic nature. At any rate Warrington took up the running and from now to the interval sub- jected elunslet to much uneasiness. When the interim whistle did sound, which it did soon afterwards, War- rington were pressing. Half-time, Warrington 1 goal; Hunslet 1 try. The second half openid in a surprising fashion. Hunslet had all the best of it and sharp passes right across were well taken and completely noopluseed the Warringtonians for a moment. Wright had the ball but alas for him but well for Warrington,Barber cut across and rushed him into touch and thus saved his side.. The Hunslet men were not easily shaken off and that same thing occurred directly after wards but it met with the same fate. A. Goldthorpe was again unfortunate with a penalty kick. It fell short and Cross cut it out of danger's way. Throughout most of the:half Warrington had to play on the defence, never was their defence more surely tried and nevsr did it show up better. It must be added that combination amongst the Hunslet threequarters fell away sadly, [or their passes were either net accepted at all or faulty. While pressing in this half they did not adopt the long kick practice but went in for strong and forcing manoeuvres. In this they were equally checkmated for Warrington to a man tackled unflincningly. Carey on one occasion prevented a certain try by rushing his man in touch. Another occasion Warrington almost lost their leal. It arose this way. Wnen play was in neutral ground Bate appealed for having made his mark, but the referee disallowed it. During the temporary discus- sion however a Hunslet man snapped the ball and was setting up for goal, but Cross too wily for him stuck to him and when he found the mark was not allowed promptly collared him. Though played out Hunslet continued to make strenuous efforts to draw ahead, but nothing would pierce the defence when it was called on. Heesoin was in fine form, keeping his head cool and being very judicious in finding touch. Towards the finish things eased off for Warrington and they attacked, though not to such an extent as they had been attacked. Huuslet attributed their defeat mostly to the superior play of the Warrington forwards. Those who witnessed that match will admit that there was much truth in what they said, but without detracting from this credit to the forwards it was quite apparent that for speed and tackling Warrington tureequarters quite overplayed their opponents and Bate and Foden did the game with the Hunslet halves. There was not much to choose be- tween the rival full backs for both played well. An in- teresting keenly contested game ended in Warrington securing the double honour of taking four points out of Hunslet and of being the only club that have done so. Result: Warrington 1 goal (five points) ; Hunslet 1 try (three points). WIDNES V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>After the shock of going down before Batley, the Old- ham Club's followers could not tell what to expect from the match with Widnes on the Watersheddings ground, on Saturday. The sequel proved that the Oldham backs were in one of their best moods, and before half time they had settled the Widnes record by a score of four goals and a try, which also proved to be the closing score. Something like 10,000 spectators were present when the teams turned out as selected. Oldham were quickly attacking, both Varley and Arthur Lees being held short of the line. An effective movement between Rispan and Peters brought the ball away, and Nevitt tried his luck at goal dropping from the centre mark,but failed. With play in the visitors' 25,Harry Eagland had a penalty kick.and despatched the ball over the bar. For the next 20 minutes the Oldham backs set the tune at a merry pace, their running and passing being really bril- liant,flurst and then Martin being only rolled over close to the flag. For a moment Peters gave Widnes a better I position, but Burst soon galloped over behind the posts, land a minute later M'Cutcheon ran clean through, and gave Hurst a chance to walk over the line and score his second try. Sam Lees again converted the point. Martin next got possession, and, smartly dodging his opposers, completed a try at the corner. The Widnes troubles were still not over, as Arthur Lees dashed through and scored another try, and Sam Lees put the ball over the bar, so that at the interval Oldham bad the comfortable lead of four goals and a try to nothing. The second half did not furnish any great amount of good football, the Oldham players seeming satisfied with the results of their pre- vious energy. Towards tbe end the Widnes men made an effort,Barber, Drummond, Rispan,and Nevitt turning the ball over to Peters, but he could not get clear, and a couple of shots at goal failed in their purpose,so that the closing score was :—Oldham, four goals (one penalty), one try (21 points) ; Widnes, nothing.—The play in the first 40 minutes was contested in the most spirited fashion, the Oldham backs giving one of their best dis- plays, all the points being credited to the back depart. I merit. Martin gave further evidence of both speed and • dodging abilities, Varley and Arthur Lees were always clever at initiating back movements,and Harry Eagland kicked with great judgment. Brennan and Peters were of most service among the Widnes backs' and of the for- wards Hanley, Foster, Whelan, and Donelly were the most effective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN v. LIVERSEDGE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. LIVERSEDGE.</p>
        <p>Liversedge paid a visit to Runcorn on Saturday, when both teams were strong, although Runcorn were without Warder, who was hurt in the county match,aod Walker, causing W. Faulkner (the captain) to go centre three- quarter. There were 3,000 spectators. At the outset toe Yorkshiremen carried scrimmage after scrimmage, and heeling out, Wood and Barker well served those behind, who indulged in some pretty short passing ; but the tackling of the home side was quick and sure,and the visitors did not become dangerous. W. Faulkner, by a drop into touch, caused Runcorn's first incursion, but J. Faulkner lost a good chance of scoring. The home backs now gave several exhibitions of smart passing, which, however, led to nothing tangible. At length W. Faulkner futively dropping at goal, Butterworth raced up and scored. Myers failed at goal. The Liversedge forwards now made several effective rushes, and Fleet- wood and oharpe each made efforts to score, but Hough- ton relieved in his best style, and half-time play was in the centre. Dull and scrambling were the opening stages of the second half,the ball hovering principally about the centre, until Jenkinson forced Houghton to touch-down</p>
        <p>by kicking over the line. Intercepting a pass from Wood, Butterworth had hard lines, being tackled just short of the line. Fearnley was hurt, and had to retire— a great loss to the visitors—and a few minutes later, after brilliant passing between W. Faulkner, Gregory, Fatmer and Butterworth, the latter scored in a good position, and Myers added the goal. From this point the home team held the upper hand, and some capital tac- tics by Butterworth and Myers enabled the latter to score, but his attempt at goal was a failure. Runcorn won in the end by 11 points to nothing.—Forward, the teams seemed equally matcned, but the pushing power of the visitors was not fully utilised. One remarkatle feature of the gam a was the speed disclosed by Butter- worth. He proved himself a smart, dashing three- quarter, running, kicking and tackling splendidly. W. Faulkner and Myers also played well, but Gregory proved an inefficient substitute for Warder. Robinson and J. Faulkner did some smart work, but the former sometimes exhibited reluctance to part with the ball. Houghton was in form,a statement that needs no enlarg- ing upon. Until he was hurt Fearnley also played a magnificent back game. The Liversedge three-quarters passed well, and it was surprising they did not make more progress, while a word of praise is also due to Wood.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. RUNCORN "A."—The above teams met for the third time this season and the second time on the Warrington ground on Saturday. On the two previcus occasions the Warringtonians had suffered defeat at the hands of the Runcornians, therefore it was no wonder a large crowd assembled to witness what proved to be a most stubbornly contested game. Play had not been in progress many minutes before the ball got moving among the Runcorn backs and Lightfoot dropped what proved to be the winning goal. Pressure was still maintained an 3 a free kick afforded Warrington little relief. Br.-,ady kicked well but it was left to Spruce ' to give the homesters a footing in neutral territory. Smart individual play was tho means of a strict pressure being put on the visitors defence, but owing to poor combination and bad passing by the backs several good opeuings were missed. Relief was brought to the visi- tors by O'Brien passing to Lightfoot in mistake and play rested at centre. Broady got nicely away but found the defence too strong and Jolley cleared. Savage dribbled I well and Hughes charging down an opponent's kick matters looked ominous for Runcorn. Higgin- bottom gave to O'Brien hut he threw badly to Broady when the latter had a good chance of scoring. Subse- quent play rested at the half way and from a free kick Broady had an ineffectual drop at goal the ball falling slightly short. Half time score, Runcorn 1 goal ; War- rington, nil. On resuming play Higginbottom stole away from a pack and ran to the visitors 25 but the chance was lost by wild passing. Strong forward play by Runcorn penned Warrington in their quarters, but Spruce got through a good amount of work with con- siderable credit. Hughes, Savage, and Royle kicked well and further play by Higginbottom and Wilcock once more gave Warrington the upper hand. Isherwood and Scott passed well, and Broady was pushed in touch near the line. Warrington still had the best of matters but the grand defence of the Runcorn backs was found without fia,w,and despite several good attempts at goal by Broady nothing further was scored. Result, Runcorn A 1 goal: Warrington A, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ANOTHER REM A.RKA_BLE LON DON ROBBERY. TIME, NOT MONEY, DEMANDED.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER REM A.RKA_BLE LON DON ROBBERY. TIME, NOT MONEY, DEMANDED.</p>
        <p>"The good old rule, the simple plan. That they shall take who have the power. And they shall keep who can," appears to still exist in London, otherwise the parents of Emily Milman, of 19, Little Goodge Street,l Tottenham Court Road, could not even think of claim- ing that their child had been robbed of forty-three years of time, as she died at six months, and as they might haveexpected her to live to forty-three and a half years the present average life, many will agree that a charge of robbery of forty-three years might be preferred. Against the charge the accused might argue that such theft would be ridiculous and impossible, as it would be as foolish as the stealing of a good name. However, the fact remains that blame rests somewhere for the loss of these forty-three years of life to little Emily, and lest others should suffer from similar losses through the same course, is, vaccination, it should be briefly stated that neither suffering nor death occur during any of these periods of vaccination if Vaccination Yirus Van- quisher, or V.V.V., were given to the child, infant, or adult while the vaccination sore is open, beginning two or three days, if possible, before vaccination is performed. Tbe antisepic actiorrof V V.V. is such that if so administered these germs ot whatever kind are quickly killed on entering the blood from the vaccina- tor's lancet and the whole of the germs that afterwards enter through the sore, whether it be scabbed or not,are similarly destroyed and quickly removed by the kidneys or bowels. Old cases due to previous vaccinations are also cured, as it is plain that the same kind of germs continue in the blood and tissues, and produce the mil- lions of chronic skin, blood and internal cases that have been pronounced incurable. V.V.V. will not only pre- vent, as already stated, the contraction of these diseases but cure the oldest of them if taken properly and regu- larly. The antiseptic in it is death to germ life, theit,gh harmless to adults,or even infants,in large doses. V.V.V. costs 21. 91, and is sold by all chemists ; by Barclay and Sons, Limited, 95, Farringdon Street ; and by the V.V.V. Company, Limited, 31, Slow London, who will send a free pamphlet to any who apply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MESSRS. CROSFIELD AND SONS GRAND SOIREE.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELD AND SONS GRAND SOIREE.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual ball given by the office staff of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield :and Sons was held in the gymnasium on Monday evening. The proceedings were very successful and a most enjoyable evening was spent The re were about 130 ladies and gentlemen present. The secretarial dutias undertaken by Mr. Parkinson with complete success, and he received every assistance from the treasurer (Mr. Brown) and the other members of the committee. The decoration of the ball room was en- trusted to Mr. Ralph Johnson, who did the work in a most tasteful manner. Tne walls were draped in muslin and Japanese fans were hung in different parts of the room, while at the end of the room two [large mirrors were fixed. In front of the mirrors there was a beauti- ful collection of choice plants, kindly lent for the occa- sion by Mr. Crosfield,and elegant plants were also placed in other parts of the building. Suspended from the ceil- were three large baskets of artificial flowers, and fairy lamps were arranged here and there on the floor, adding greatly to the general effect. The passage, entrance, and staircase leading to the refreshment room and retir- ing rooms were covered with red baize,while the refresh- ment room itself was set out with perfect taste. The decorations throughout looked exceedingly pretty, and were much admirsd by all who attended the ball. The guests began to arrive shortly after half-past seven o'clock, and were received by the members of the com- mittee, who were most anxious that those who honoured the gathering with their presence should enjoy the pro- ceedings to the full. Dancing commenced punctually at , eight o'clock, to the strains of Mr. Pearson's excellent string band. The M.C. was Mr. W. H. Robinson, to whose exertions much of the enjoyment afforded to the guests was certainly attributable. Mr, Robinson was ably assisted by the stewards,Messrs. Hamilton, Clayton Houghton, and Gleave. The refreshment buffet was in charge of Mrs. Mottram, of Bridge-street, and her cater- ing gave unqualified satisfaction. The number present, in all, was about 150,and or, leaving each guest was pre- sented with a fancy box, containing a number of tablets of toilet soap and a bottle of elycsrhae, as a souvenir of the interesting event. Mr. A. H Crosfield also for- warded a quantity of flowers from Prance which were distributed amongst the ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS HIP AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS HIP AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the Admiral Nel- son Lodge of Oddfellows which was held on Mon- day at the registered offices, 11, Academy-st., a pleasing feature of the evening's proceedings de- volved upon the Prov. G.M., Frank James Whittle, namely, the making of a presentation of a framed past officer's certificate to Bros. William Holbrook, P. 0., and John 11. Sherlock, P. 0., for services ren- dered to the lodge. Bro. Holbrook thanked the members, and Bro. Emson, C.S., in Bro. Sherlock's absence owing to indisposition, accepted the other on his behalf. These certificates were the result of a prize of 50 guineas given by the Board of Direc- tors of the Unity for the best design. The prize was won by D. Marples and Co., of Liverpool. After the presentations Bro. J. Forrester, P. 0., Trafford Lodge, proposed the toast of "The Manchester Unity," to which Bro. J. Emson, Prov. C.S., suitably responded. Bro. Emson also gave the members a short report of the proceedings of the County Con- ference, which was held at Saddieworth on Satur- day. Bro. F. Taylor, P. 0., Constitution Lodge, proposed "The Warrington District," highly eulo- gizing the officers and committee for securing such excellent rooms for their meetings, &amp;c. Other toasts and songs followed, and a very pleasant evening was brought to a close by the lodge being closed in due form. From the secretary's report, which was issued in January, it appears that the lodge is making good •financial progress. The in- crease in funds in 1894 is £123 and in 1895 ..£lOO. The present capital of the lodge is £1,302, of which ..£1,200 is well invested. The number of members is 162, with an average age of 33 years. The follow- ing gentlemen represented Warrington at the County Conference :—Bros. John Emson. Prov. C.S.; J. H. Norbury, P.P.G. M.; James Boyle, P. 0., and H. Lloyd, P.O.</p>
        <p>FURS.—SEALSKIN JACKETS and FURS of every des- cription Cleaned or Re-Dyed and Altered. Fur Skins Dressed and Made-up int.) Rugs, Muffs, Collars, &amp;c.—B. OOKE, Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper k Newington, and 21, lie nshaw-street,Liverpool, Established .over 21 years ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL V. EIIILMR GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—This match was played on Saturday on the ground of the former, and resulted in a win for the homesters by two goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MURDER OF A FORMER WARRINGTON LADY.</head>
        <p>MURDER OF A FORMER WAR- RINGTON LADY.</p>
        <p>The Nottinghamshire police are investigating a serious case of outrage and robbery. For some little time an old lady named Ann Kave,about 68 years of age a native of Warrington,and who formerly lived in Bewsey-street, has been staying with the family of Samuel Powley, a market gardener, at Arnold, near Nottingham. Powley was away from home on Friday night, and at about ten o'clock a man called at the house and informed Mrs. Powley that her husband had been knocked down by a bicycle and had been badly hurt, and that he wanted to see his wife and children. The ruse was successful,and Mrs. Powley, accompanied by her two elder children, set out in the direction indicated. On her return she found Mrs. Kaye lying on the floor in a pool of blood, with her head badly injured, apparently by a blow with a poker. There were evidences of a struggle have taken place, and the house had been ramsacked and some money and jewellery taken away. The condition of Mrs. Kaye was serious, and she was conveyed to the Notting- ham General Hospital. A younger child of the Powleys, who was asleep in bed when the outrage and robbery were committed, was undisturbed. It appears that the stranger accompanied Mrs. Powley and her two children for a distance towards the inn, where he told them Mr. Powley,was lying,and then left them, saying he was go- ing to Nottingham. As a matter of fact Mr. Powley, who is a candidate for the District Council,was engaged on business connected with the election. Mrs. Kaye was known in Warrington, where she had resideWwith'a*married daughter for some years. The surprise of the relatives may be imagined when they received information of the terrible maltreatment the old lady had received. The details of the outrage were not communicated to them, but it appears that in October last she went to Nottingham to stay with a married son, who is an engineer on the Great Northern Railway, in- tending to return to Warrington in March. During her stay in Nottingham, however, Mrs. Kaye accepted an invitation to spend a few days'at the house of Mr. Samuel Powley, a market gardener, living at Arnold. Mr. Powley, who lives in Gedling-lane,is one of the eight candidates seeking election on the Arnold Urban. District Council, and on Friday night was busily engaged can- ' vassing in the district, and in the course of his business happened to go in the Ram Inn. During his stay there a stranger entered, and seeing Mr. Powley, asked a man sitting near who he was. His neighbour answered that It was Mr. Powley, a market gardener. Shortly after- wards Mr. Powley left the house, being followed by the stranger, who eventually found his way to Mr. Powley's house. Mrs. Powley and the children, together with Mrs. Kaye, were in the house when a knock came to the door. Before they could get there the stranger who is described as a thin, dark man, rather tall, and attired in a hard felt hat and black suit, entered. After asking if Mr. 1-owley lived there, and being answered in the affirmative, the stranger volun- teered the information that: Mr. Powley had met with a very serious accident by being run over by a bicycle, and was lying badly hurt at the Ram Inn, whore he wished to see his wife and children. Mrs. Powley was naturally greatly alarmed, and went out with the stranger, one of her sons going ahead, leaving Mrs. Kaye in the house. The stranger, after accom- panying Mrs. Powley some distance into the town, left her after wishing" Good night," and proceeded on to the Ram Inn by herself. She had not gone far when she was met by her son, who informed her that his father</p>
        <p>was all riiht. Surpecting that there was something wrong, she told the lad to gm for a policeman and pro- ceeded home. On arrival at Mr. Parley's house, which is quite isolated,being:at least three hundred yards from any other dwelling-house, a dreadful discovery met their eyes. Mrs. Kaye was found lying in the back kitchen in; pool of blood. Her head had been terribly battered with a kitcken poker which was found after- wards ,'bent and intended. The unfmrtunate woman lay on her back, moaning slightly. The ceiling and three of the walls were covered with blood, being evidence of the brave resistance made by the old lady to the mis- creant's attack. About the room were traces of a struggle having taken place, and from what cam be gathered it would appear that the old woman was suddenly surprised by the thief, but with coolness and presence of mind remarkable in a woman of such advanced years, seized him and endeavoured to eject him. The man, according to those investigating the matter, made an attempt to escape. but Mrs. Kaye held on to him and endeavoured to attract attention by screaming. Possibly had it not been for this brave attempt to detain the man, Mrs. Kaye would not have been injured. The thief evidently apprehending that outside attentiou would soon be attached became vio- lent with the woman, dragged her into the room, and as could be seen from what was found there afterwards, made the most brutal and murderous attack upon her, using the poker with such force as to cause her death. It is supposed that he then carried out his object of robbing the house. Mrs. Kaye was examined by a medical man, and afterwards was removed to the Not- tingham General Hospital. The man, who is supposed to be a tramp, was between 35 and 40 years of age, and the police are actively endeavouring to find him. Up to the present, however, they have not been able to effect his arrest.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Kaye, who had been gradually sinking during the night, died on Monday morning shortly after 11 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EDUCATION SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The " Times "says :—There is reason to believe that one of the features of the Government scheme for re- dressing the grievances of the Voluntary schools will be a special grant to depressed schools in poor districts,this grant being made to Board schools and denominational schools alike. It is an open secret that Ministers also coctemplate the abolition of the 17s. 81 limit and the exemption of elementary schools from rates ; though, in regard to the latter reform, it is reported that unlooked for technical difficulties have arisen which it will require some ingenuity to surmount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CONCERT AT MOORE.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>A successful concert was given in the Church Institute last evening week,before a good audience. The programme, which included violin solos and other instrumental items, was commenced by a glee, well sung by the choir. The remaining por • tions were ably contributed by the following : Mrs. Atkin, Misses Wakefield, Jeans, White ; and Messrs. Dodd, Carter, Melville, Atkin, Warbur- ton, Gough, and the Rev. J. W. Gilbertson. Messrs. Dodd and Carter contributed the humorous element.</p>
        <p>NEW BOOK, MAGAZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>MACMILLAN AND Co.—The thirteenth of the Illustrated Standard Novels, which has just been issued is" Headlong Hall, and Nightmare Abbey," by Thomas Love Peacock. Mr. George Saintsbury contributes an introduction to the work and apologises for the rudimentary condition and style of writing of the stories compared with " Crotchet Castle," which latter was written 14 years later when the author's familiarity with society and the world was as complete as he desired it to be. It must be obvious from the book,says Mr.Saintsbury, " that the writer possessed in the first place vary unusual and unhackneyed satirical powers, in the second, still less usual or hackneyed fashion of exerting them, and thirdly, one of the least com- monplace and most promising bundles of preju- dices and preferences." "Nightmare Abbey," he continues, "is a distinct advance on Headlong Hall.'—The scheme may be called farcical if any- body pleases; but it is farce of the altogether good kind,where the reality ot the characters more than atones for the improbability of the incidents." The illustrations by Mr. H. R. Millar, are characteristic of the high standard of work which has adhered to pictorial representation in all the novels, and do full justice to both readers'and author's conceptions of the scenes and personalities.—" The Earl ot Dun- donald," is subject matter for one of the English Men of Action Series and his biography is thoroughly and graphically written by the Hon. J. W. Fortescue. A glance through the book 80011 convinces one that Dundonald is fully entitled to 'make one of the heroes whose doings are so interest- ingly described in the volumes of this series.—The Tennysonian People's Edition is assuming quite respectable proportions, the seventh and eighth numbers being "The Princess" (2) and "Enoch Arden," the latter also including " Aylmer's Field" and " Lucretius." We could scarcely imagine a prettier or more serviceable edition of Tennyson than this promises to be.—A most welcome little book is "Heroes," or "Greek Fairy Tales," by Charles Kingsley, and it fonns one of the Kingsley Pocket Edition. The binding and general appear- ance of the book tallys, of course, with the others of the series, but the type is slightly enlarged and will no doubt be thus more acceptable to the eyes of juvenile readers.—" The Century" for this month abounds in interesting articles, stories, &amp;c., including "The Story of the Development of Africa," by Henry M. Stanley. "Pope Leo and His Household," by F. Marion Crawford," and "Certain Worthies, and Dames of Old Maryland," with por- traits by well known artists is contributed by John Williamson Palmer.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Macmillan and Co., have also published a new edition of" The Use of Life by Sir Johy Lub- bock Bart , at the low price of is. The first edi- tion won the work an excellent name and the ad- vantage afforded of acquiring it at so cheap a rate will not be lost by hundreds of thoughtful readers.</p>
        <p>CASSELL AND Co.—From these publishers we have received the bound volume of "Lost on Du-Corrig,' by Standish O'Grady, a story which ran originally through the second volume of "Chums." Here it formed one of the most interesting items of that number and was very well liked. The story deals with the sudden and mysterious disappearance of John Freeman, the eldest of four brothers. He had it turns out,slipped into a subterranean cave, where he remains for a long period of time. His adven- tures, devices for comfort in his new home, and his brother's inadvertent landing at the same pace when in search of him, form a budget of exciting and enthralling events the persual of which would delight any and every boy. For a birthday or presentation book we scarcely know of anything more suitable. In book form the story lakes a conveniently sized volume. There are numerous illustrations and the binding is attractive.while the largeness of the type is also a good feature. In Cassell's Magazine for the current month there is an article enbodying the opinions of several emi- nent travellers, on 'The Race for the Pole" between Nansen and Andriee. As the former is said to have reached his goal the article is rendered exception- ally interesting. It is always nice to be able to read the prophecies of success or otherwise, which have been made in connection with some coming or ex- pected event. The same number contains complete stories by Mrs. L. T. Meade, W. L. Alden, and Mrs. W. K. Clifford. Frederick Dolman contributes an illustrated article on " Hatfield House," the resid- ence of the Marquis of Salisbury, and another of great interest on " Recent Escapes from gaol" by Major Arthur Griffiths, the best man for the sub- ject. This month's " Quiver " contains a very capable paper from the Rev. H. S. Lunn, M.D., on "The Power of the Press "in which he says after referr- ing to the dangers of a degraded Press "This brief review of the power for evil which the press can exert ought only to increase our thankfulness that, as a whole, this great intellectual and moral force has been so largely exerted on the side of justice, truth and righteousness." A number of other articles and stories are contributed by well- known penmen,which,adde,d to the excellent serials, make the " Quiver " the best of papers for Sunday and general reading. GEORGE NEWNES AND Co.—A special number of "The Navy and Army" has been issued contain- ing plates of all the ships commissioned in the famous" Flying Squadron and a number of the "Channel Squadron." The dimensions, armament, draught, and speed of " each are given. All illustrations are remarkable for clearness and detail. We must congratulate Messrs' Newnes and Co. in having so rapidly put before the British public, illustrations, etc. of the vessels com- posing a squadron which has been the talk of Europe during the past month. Another view book has been issued, this time picturing "The Way of the Cross," and scenes in the pilgrimage from Bethlehem to Calvary. The photographic views are so arranged as to illustrate consecutively the incidents of the Life of Christ. In part. we are permitted to peep into the Church of the Annunciation, and gaze upon views of Nazareth, Bethlehem, the Field of the Shepherds, the Church of the Nativity (inside and out), Joseph's workshop, the site of Joseph's House and a number of others. The edition is to be complete in 10 parts of 24 pictures each. All the illustrations are examples of the finest work and are printed on thick glazed paper. Messrs. Newnes and Co. have also commenced to issue a penny library of famous books, which com- mences with the "Vicar of Wakefield." All are una- bridged, and the type though small is distinct. In" Tit Bits" a number of stories of the English coast in war-time entitled " Beacon Fires" have been commenced. Mr. Ileadon Hill is the author, and the narratives are exciting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>VALUABLE WITNESSES.</head>
        <p>VALUABLE WITNESSES.</p>
        <p>are daily coming forward and giving evidence in favour of Warner's Safe Cure as a highl7 efficacious remedy for the treatment of disorders of the 'kidneys and liver. They are rescued sufferers and desire to prevail upon others similarly afflicted to lose no time in resorting to this valuable remedy. Mr. Jas. A. Joy, 63, Corporation- street,Manchester,says : "I have pleasure in testifying to the beneficial effects I received from the use of Warner's Safe Cure. About four years ago I had an attack of Influ- enza which kept me in bed four weeks. When I got up and about again I was very nervous; could get"no sleep: could not eat, and suffered with pains in the head and ' face, which almost drove me mad. I got worse and worse week after week. Of course I had medical advice and various kinds of medicine, from none of which I re• ceived any benefit. When things got to the worst, and everybody, including myself, had given up all hope, I met a friend whom I had not !Peen for some time; and seeing the condition I was in (I had lost 4 stone in weight), he said, 'Try Warner's Safe Cure.' I laughed atithe idea. No quack medicine for me, thank you. But he insisted, saying, Don't get one bottle and use it and then say it is no good. Your case is too serious for that.lt has taken years to bring you to your present condition, and you won't be cured in a week, but if you will get bottle after bottle for a ntonth, and take the medicine as per instructions, and if you do not find yourself well on the road to recovery at the end of that time, I will pay for all you have taken and stand a guinea silk hat into the bargain.' I yielded and got a large bottle, and religiously took eight doses a day. When that was done I got another and followed suit. At the end of two weeks I felt no better, though everyone else congratu- lated me upon the improvement that had taken place. This encouraged me to go on, which I did for about four months. At tho end of that time I was as well as ever I have been in my life, gaining weight all the time. Since thou I have never had a day's illness: but I am never without a small bottle of Warner's Safe Cure in the house." Mrs. Ball, Utkinton Lane, Tarporley. Cheshire, gives striking testimony of her recovery from Indigestion and Dyspepsia. She writes: " After suffering for many years from Indigestion, dyspepsia, chronic weakness of the throat, severe headaches, palpitation of the heart, nervous depression, with an occasional attack of bron- chitis, Warner's Safe Cure has been the means of restor- ing me to perfect health. I shall at all times extol the medicine which has done so much for me."</p>
        <p>At Sheerness on Wednesday Frederick, Williams, a stoker on H.M.S. Wildfire, was sentenced to six months' hard labour for striking a ship's corporal.</p>
      </div>
