        <p>If you get it, you will have the true remedy; but if you accept any preparation made by some one else, under a similar name, you will be disappointed. Remember, the original SYRUP OF FIGS is manu- factured by the</p>
        <p>California j Fig Co. Of all Chemists, is. 114 &amp; 2s. ed., or post free on receipt of price from the Depot for Great Britain and Colonies: 32 SNOW HILL, LONDON. E.C.</p>
        <p>IMPE RIAL PA RLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Mr. CHANNINGE asked whether Russia had been informed that in the event of her taking steps to occupy the Armenian pro- vinces no objection would be offered by England under the Cyprus Convention or any other treaty. Mr. Cunzoil replied that the occupation of the Armenian provinceS by any European Power without the consent of the) Sultan would be a violation of the Treaties of Paris and Berlin, and' no such intimation as that suggested in the question had been addressed to Russia by the PowerS who signed those treaties: .</p>
        <p>Mr. 71. J. BALFOUR declined to appoint a committee to inquire into the practice and procedure of the House of Commons, but stated that the Committee on distreaS arising from want of employment would be re-ap- pointed. The debate on Mr. HARRINGTON'S amendment to thl3 Address, asking for a reconsideration of the cases of the prisoners convicted under the Trea.sen-Felony Act who are still in prison for offences arising out of insurrectiofi. ary movements connected with Ireland, was resumed bY Mr. J. Ilnumotqn. He urged the Gtovernment to be sads. fled with the thirteen years these men had already bees in prison and to release the nine" sad survivors of a 0 old and dead movement of passion and of madness!' The amendment was supported by Mr. ll.PLumarrT,Mr. HALDANE' and Mr. LEM!' and opposed by Mr. A. J. Bale FOUR and Mr. ASQUITH. The closure was moved by Mr. BaLFous:and carried, and on a division the amendment was rejected by 279 votes to 117. • Mr. ATHERLEY JONES moved an amendment in favour of referring the dispute with Venezuela in relation the delimitation of the boundary between the State ana British Guiana to arbitration "in accordance with tbe suggestion of the Government of the United States!' Before the amendment was seconded Mr. A. J. BALFO4O assured Mr. JONES that it would not serve the object he had at heart, and urged him not .to press it. Mr. Pi; ALLAN seconded the amendment, and Mr. DILLON urgeo the mover to persevere with it. Sir W. HARCOURT Or conded Mr. BALFOUR'S appeal, and Mr. Arnaar.EY Jonls ~ ev.entually withdrew his amendment. Sir W .WEDDERBITILN then proposed an amendment er pressing regret that the Government had not adhered tø th. 3 policy of their prenecessors and retired from CD"- ral. Mr. C. E. SCHWANN seconded the amendrnent.whiat was defeated by 193 votes to 79:_</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. The Irish fChieffSecretary iinformednMr. Engle3e97 in the House of Commons on Tuesday that since 1883 the Royal Irish Constabulary had been reduced by 2,20 mm, and the number of resident magistrates was alSe considerably s-naller than some years ago. Asked whether, in view of a recent case, he would take steps for the abolition of the system of proprietarl asylums kept for profit and the substitution of self. supporting State or chartered asylums. Sir M. White Ridley replied that he had communicated with the Lord Chancellor on the subject, and he understood that Lord Halsbury had in preparation a bill which would aim at improving and extending the precautions against the abuse of the lunacy laws. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN stated that according to the lateSt information 21 of Dr. Jameson's men were killed ill , action or died, and 46 others were admitted to hospital. The debate on the Address was resumed on llttr• II Weir's amendment expressing regret that no refer. ence was made in the Queen's Speech to questions/ specially affecting the interests of the fishing popir lations in the Highlands and inlands of Scotland. The amendment was rejected by 276 votes to 143. Major Jameson moved an amendment representing that the refusal of the Government to concede the demands made on behalf of the Christian Brothers' and other denomina. tional schools in Ireland had inflicted great injustice oil those schools. Mr. S. Mac Neil seconded the amend. ment. Mr. G. Balfour said that if the schools were situated in England they would not receive even that limited assistance from public funds which Voluntali schools received. He was prepared to embody the sur gestions he had to make in a bill if there was a chance 0 the measure being accepted as a settlement oX the quoit. tion, and if he had reason to believe that it would be re' garded as a non-contentious measure he thought there was some prospect of being able to pass it this session. Mr. Healy asked that a special day might be fixed fot the discussion of the measure. Mr. A. J. Balfour thought that if great hostility was shown to the measure by the Irish members a day would not be of much use. but if they thought it contained the elements of ail arrangement the Government might grant a day for its/ consideration. The amendment was than withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Con;;;Ons—oii—liednesday, Dr. KENO moved the first reading of the Coroners (Ireland) Bill, the object of which is to provide for the appointment of deputies, to enable coroners to be paid by salary instead of by fees, and in other respects to assimilate the law of Ireland to that of England. Mr. G. BALFOUR suggested that the bill should be read a second time on the distineo understanding that the Committee stage sheuld not be taken for a month. Dr. KENNY agreed to this, and tbe bill was then read a second time. Sir A. Itou.rr moved the second reading of the Boarde of Conciliation (No. 2) Bill. Sir J. LUBBOCK seconded tbe motion. Mr. MUNDELL&amp; suggested that the bill should be read a second time and then referred to the Grand Committee. Mr. RITCEUE, on behalf of the Governmente accepted the suggestion, and the bill was subsequentli read a second time, and then referred to the Grand Committee on Trade. ' Sir Joux LUBBOCK moved the second reading of the Shop Hours (Early Closing) Bill. The bill was read e second time, and the Committee stage was provisionalli fixed for Friday.</p>
        <p>ST. BARNADAS' CHURCH TEA PARTY. The annual tea party and conversazione, in coil: section with the above place of worship, was bele" in St. Barnabas' schools, Bostock-street, on Mondai evening. There were about 300 present, and an er cellent tea was provided. A large number of ladieg presided over the tables, amongst whom were Mesdames Allton, Evans, Trevett, Looker, Roscoe, and Towers, and the Misses Wilson, Harter, Helsbn Lansdale, and Weatherburn. At the conclusion 0..1' the tea, which was thoroughly enjoyed, the tab?, were removed from the room, and dancing was 111" dulged in to the strains of an efficient band. B!: tween the various dances several musical it 1.3131: were rendered in first-class style by the choir, duet by the Misses B. Wilson and M. Helsby, øoiu by the Misses May Allton and Gertrude Harter, an_N songs by Messrs. N. P. James and F. ScarisbrickM: of whom acquitted themselves in an admirablv_ manner. The Rev. G. Allton also addressed tb meeting upon affairs connected with the pave': The room was tastefully decorated with evergreeol flags, and Japanese lanterns, by Mrs. Allton 014 the Misses Allton and Lansdale. Among othe..,'"; present were the Rev. A. H. Duncan, and Wheeldon.</p>
        <p>COCOA is more than a mere stimulating and rerilres drink, it is also a nutritious food. and one of the °tile precious gifts of nature—sustaining and inviaorati°g ,rl3/ system probably more than any other beverage. 01 ' Lancet ' refers to Cadbury's CocoA as "the standaP'o,l highest purity at present attainable in regard to cocolL. Advt.</p>
