        <p>VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>CORONER'S INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse' county coroner, held an inquest at the Varade Room,lrlam-street,on Wednesday week, relative to the death of Frank Burgess,aged two months,whe was; found dead in bed on Saturday. and who had been vac- Cinated three weeks previously. Mr. Brighouse stated that the Government Commission On Vaccination had issued a circular to all coroners ask- ing that where any death was reported closely following Vaccination,they should be communicated with and they Vould be represented at the inquiry if necessary.He (the coroner) had informed the secretary of the Commission Of the circumstances of this death and had received a communication in reply,stating that the commission did ot think it necessary to be represented at the inquest. Be had ordered a post-mortem examination to be made and Dr.Naden was present withiparticulars of the result. John Charles Burgess, 1, Thistle-street, Warrington, father of the child, stated that it was vaccinated on the 10th January at his house,when it appeared to be pretty ▪ t.bDr. Naden performed the operation which was (witness's consent). From that time the child ~as cross and apparently ailing, until its death. On mridaY night, the 7th inst., he and his wife and child Zent to bed between 11 and half-past. The child lay eat Mrs• Burgess and near the side of the bed. She aWoke about 3.15 fn the morning and found the child Still on her arm but dead. V Dr. Naden. surgeon and physician, deposed that he accinated •the child on the 10th January. There seemed to ethno rea operat7i. " for his not _doing so as it appeared a —e saw the baby three or four tim.s after the son,the last occasion being four days before deatth, be had eemed to be in perfect health. Since the dea h nd it - Made and he fo a post-mortem examination of the body a,,n in a und it woll-nourished and with all the organs infia_ mrn°Maltio condition. There were signs of a slight accou in the stomach and he thought tat by z.nteu for the child's crossness during the time stated tacchlat* ‘a• Burgess. There was nothing to show that asealltehde ion had in any way been connected with death, la wounds were perfectly healthy and nearly In a I nswer to tile Coroner's query as to whetber there_ in ii"ea,PY,annearance of the child having been suffocated absen-; ur• Naden said all the signs of suff.cation were rutural --; It was his opinion that the child had died from eauSes or a fit. vaeci. ag.9,rgess added that he strongly disbelieved in 41 wonf -06911 himself. H. would never be vaccinated and vo„sullci b-ele to be an old man in his dotage before he Lhe co tegular rsreUer said that he believed in vaccination at cinated rner:2ds and would have all his family re-vac- ing out Xle event of an epidemic of smallpox break- he vicinity of his home. • Zrnbssio4 `4! were then left to themselves and after a Death flasting some time they returned a verdict of rom natural causes."</p>
