      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>VIOLENT TREATMENT OF A. WIFE.</head>
        <p>VIOLENT TREATMENT OF A. WIFE.</p>
        <p>"TWO YEARS OF MARTYRDOM" "NOT &amp; MAN BUT A BEAST." JUDICIAL SEPARATION GRANTED. On Friday morning at the Warrington Borough Police ‘Jou rt before Aldermen Harrison, and Wright, Joseph 14Achp n, 13 Foundry-street, dry treet, was charged with assaulting 2, beating his wifes Mary Birchall on the Ist, Feb. Mr' Percy Davies prosecuted, and said he was quite sure that, when the bench heard the evidence he had o i lririg before them they would agree with him :that al- though - the woman' had been married only two years to the defendant she had suffered two years of martyrdom, arid the cruelty of not a man but a beast. Two years z.ag° complainant was well-to-do having saved about £BO xinfortunately she married defendant and he had robbed tier of the money and:bragged about spending it in drink to the extent of £3 in a week. Be had added insult to insult, blow to blow, and kick to kick culminating on the lit of February with the assault he was charged With. Complainant had been to the police and com- plained and they had advised her to claim the nrotection Of the Married Women's Maintenance Act of 1895 About 11 o'clock on the night of February Ist. the complainant Wag entering her house from the back yard when the 'defendant without any provocation whatever struck her in the eye felling her to the floor with his fist and causing the discolouration of which traces were yet to be seen, though it was nearly a fortnight ago. When she was on he floor he kick her on the chest and various parts of he body. There was nobody present to protect her,but espectable witnesses who could prove defendant's con- duct would be called. Mrs. Birchall had left the house On the Monday following and went to her sister with Whom she stayed, and she had been ill ever since. Mrs. Birchall, the complainant, a middle-aged woman of Illetlioate appearance and whose face still bore traces usage, supported Mr. Davies' statement, and added B e e nt to her sister's house on the Monday be- t ha ao ..01, e she was locked out. Whether Callon' step-sister of complainant, said that lea Mrs. Birchall arrived at her house she was in a r condition, and she dare not comb her hair for rthree or four days, and had to remain in bed for three ays. Witness went to defendant who used disgusting eguage and threats. The Chief Constable said that he saw complainant on Monday, the 3rd of February and her face was in a de- Plorable condition and a fall could not possibly have caused such injuries. She made a complaint involving defendant. The magistrates committed defendant to goal for three months' hard labour. Mr. Davies then applied on behalf of complainant for lodical separation, defendant having been convicted of an aggravated assault upon her. He added that the case was one of daily abuse in so many different cruel Ways that no one without the mind of a beast could have acted as defendant had done. There was hardly a claimed by Policeman in Warrington that had not had his protection complainant one time or 6nother. The defendant when he married her never brought a penny. She had £BO which she had put away in the house'. This he had stolen from time to time, and put shillings in the place of sovereigns. This was only one of his mean acts. He had spent and bragged about spending £2 a week in think alone. He hal said that he had lived with 14 Women, and that he would finish her (complainant) up. Be had certainly finished her money up, and it was only that morning while he was in court that he (Mr. Davies) had received intimation that while complainant had been living with her sister, defendant had taken all the furniture away, although it was all his s. ' During the whole time defendant was raiarrliedwiheehad onIV given her 16s. He had even threatened to roast her upon the fire, and he had kiekbd her from time to time. P.C. Corcoran on one occasion found complainant at 6 a.m. in the street under the window begging and praying to be let ie. He submitted that this was a 'proper case for the new Act. Mrs. Hannah Bamford, living next door to the Bir- chall's, testified to the frequent cruelty of defendant. Witness had taken complainant in at a.m. She had heard defendant using language not fit for a beast never mind a Christian and threats which made her blood run cold." Mr. Arthur Brownementioned that the defendant had 'eold a horse and cart to a client of his and they now discovered that they belonged to Mrs. Birchall, and he thought they would have to proceed against him for ob- taining money on false pretences. The magistrates granted a judicial separation and Made an order of 7s. 61 a week against defendant. Mr. Davies said he was desired by the relatives of the Znoplainant to thank the Chief Constable and Inspector Pinks for the assistance and help they had given her in the ease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RAT-HAUNTED ISLAND</head>
        <p>A RAT-HAUNTED ISLAND</p>
        <p>ii &amp;elarresnondent writes to the "Yorkshire Post ": parl...ead'sl4land," the property of the Humber Conser- ;Bo4l, situated in the Upper Humber and adjoining Øtb Perribv, Lincolnshire, consisting of some six hun- „led acres of " warp," or reclaimed land, has become '''uroPletely overrun by rats or voles. It appears that B°, Me three or four years ago these rodents took up their gabitation on the island,first in the drains,then in " out- .) and embankments,and finally all over the island Until it has become perfectly honey-combed. Prior to the arrival of these pests no finer piece of pasture was to be found in England ,there having been often from two 'to three thousand head of sheep, cattle, and horses on it. Now the entire area presents the appearance of a ploughed field,or more correctly that of a rabbit warren, the burrowing:and earth-throwing propensities of the rat 'being quite equal to that of the mole. From examina- tion it nas been found that the food or these intruders has been chiefly, if not entirely, of grass and thistle Ito)te.with the result that the grass crop has been alto- gether destroyed. Curious, too, is the quantity of roots to be found in the " runs," stored possibly by way of Provision against the rigours of winter. That matters were getting rapidly from bad to worse had for some time past been apparent to all,but how to grapple with or check the invon became a matter of serious rrOment. Finally, however, the flooding of the island 'being determined upon by the Conservancy,openings on 'the south tide were cut through the embankments, thereby admitting the tidal waters. On examination co, the island it was found that although a perfect hecal tonab of rats, and especially the young, had been des' trtbved,the embankment—which can be little short of. five miles round the island—were perfectly riddled, and otherwise in a deplorable condition, so that even should the next spring tides inundate the island better than last the extermination or the pest will be by no means complete. What., therefore,may be the final outcome of the efforts now being made by the Conservancy to get rid of their unwelcorm visitors remains to be seen. Possibly the sudden dearth of food from flooding m. y assist in reducing the numbers,but as it was reported by eye-witnesses that many rats had been seen swimming across the channel, a distance of three to four hundred yards, to the mainland, the" cold water cure " adopted .may have its disadvantages in more ways than one.</p>
      </div>
