      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>DISCOVERY OF THE NORTH POLE.</p>
        <p>ADVENTUROUS VOYAGE OF THE FRAM. SUCCESS OF DR. NANSEN'S EXPEDITION.</p>
        <p>- - - The reported success of Dr. Nansen's Expedition has been flashed all over the world, and if the North Pole has really this time been discovered, it will cause intense excitement in all the geographical societies. Dr. Nansen will have achieved for Norway a distinguish,d. celebrity whlch England and America have spent imm,nse sums t, attain nevertheless both England and America will ungrudgingly congratulate Norway upon the remarkable success. We trust the reported success will prove absolute, and in this event Worsley's Famous Furnishing Establishment will endeavour to have nailed to the North Pole the well-known advertisement :</p>
        <p>FURNISH AT WORSLEY'S,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>"THE PANTHEON." GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>"THE PANTHEON." GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>NVEMILY CHAT :- Worsley's despatch goods to all parts of the world, and believe in advertising as one of the essentials of success. No firm in Manchester has shown more spirit and enterprise In advertising, the turnover has increased enormously, the output at the present time is the largest in Manchester. The main secret of success is small profits, combined with the largest stock in England to select from. A visit to" The Pantheon" is both instructive and interesting; it contains over a million square feet of showrooms, is fitted throughout with the electric light, a new showroom, storerooms, carpet factory, and an entirely new pianoforte saloon forming the latest additions to the premises. The Manchester Electric Company have all but completed the new electric installation with powerful engines and dynamo. Pay a visit to 'The Pantheon " before selecting your furniture, it will cost you nothing, but may profit you muc'i ; you are at perfect liberty to inspect without being expected to purchase.</p>
        <p>ALL the goods advertised are for cash, but may be had on the deferred payment system if required, with- out any addition to the cost. Insp,ction invited.</p>
        <p>READ ON OF BARGAINS GREAT AND WONDERFUL. EPITOME OF BARGAINS IN HOUSES OF FURNITURE. For £l5; usual price, £2O. A smart House of Furniture, con- sisting of the whole of the Furniture for 3 Rooms. the Sitting-room having Solid Mahogany or Walnut Furni- ture; the Bedroom containing a Bedroom Suite corn plete ; also Solid Brass and Enamel Bedstead, complete with all the Bedding. &amp;c. The Kitchen is also fur- nished complete, besides Hall, Stairs, Landing, and Scullery. Everything is as good as new, and cost over £4O not six months ago. A full detailed list will be</p>
        <p>sent post free on application. For £25; usual price, £35. A magnificent House of Furni- ture, removed from Sale, cost £7O. tor 4 Rooms. Par-</p>
        <p>ticulare on application. For £4O, cost £95. A grand House of Furniture, mostly made by Ogden, in splendid zondition, equal to new; one of the gems; the best bargain ever offered by Worsley's. Contains furniture for dining-room, draw- ing-room, and three bedrooms, besides kitchen; the sundries are worth £lO. Suites t every room. The I furniture can be guaranteed throughout, being all solid and well made. Send for full detailed list. For £6O. Complete House of Furniture. This lot is a defaulter's house of furniture, which we supplied on th deferred payment system, but which contract was never completed. It was a bargain in the first instance, but is doubly so now. The dining-ro,m is furnished en- tirely in solid brown oak, the dr twing-room is Chip- pendale mahogany, the best bedroom in solid walnut -and burr, the visitors' bedroom in satinwood. The kitchen servants' bedrooms are nicely furnished, and besides there are the pictures, curtains, blinds, sun- dries, &amp;c., also the best carpets and oilcloths through out. The whole lot for 1160 ; cheap at £l2O. Send a once for a complete detailed list of this lot to Worsley's For 100 Guineas. The Best Complete House of Furniture i Manchester for this price, or even double this price. Worsley's in this lot offer all the heavy, substantial furniture, removed from Brooklands, for the 100 guineas, and will undertake to supply new carpets and • linoleum throughout without making any additional charge for this lot, The furniture is worth double the amount, still, whoever buys this lot may have perfect freedom in selection of carpets and linoleum. The furniture is for dining, drawing, sitting, and three bedrooms. Send for the complete list, when you can better judge of the most extraordinary bargain eve offered.</p>
        <p>The above form the Ist Division of the Flying Squadron 01 wonderful bargains mobilise a at "The Pantheon,"</p>
        <p>GREAT 33/ RGAINS IN DINING - MOAN.</p>
        <p>FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>For £2O. The Best Solid Oak Dining-room Suite ever offered for this money, containing suite of nine pieces—couch' two easy chairs, and six easy chairs, in morocco. A magnificent s'deboard, dining-table, and overmantle, all in solid brown oak, exquis,tely cp.rved in bas relief. The lot is worth £5O. Inwspection inyited. Will be sent on approval anywhere. For £lO 10. Al Complete Dining-room Suite, Sideboard' Dining Table. and Overmantel in Walnut, similar t the one £2O. The whole lot for £lO 10s„ not 10 guineas each, but the lot for that price. Call and see this lot for yourself, it will almost knock you down with surprise, being simply given away. Five years warranty. Money returned if not approved. • For £45. Very Handsome Dining-rocm Furniture in Pollard oak and morocco. Six feet Sideb”ard with fine hand- some massive back, beautifully fitted with exquisitely shaped bevelled crystal mirrors; magnificent Couch, Two fine Basy Chairs, and Six standard padded back Single Chairs. splendid Pollard oak Dining Table, with five leaves and leaf case; and last a capital Overmantel, with d üble bevels. The sideboard cost more money than we want for the lot. Inspect at WORSLNICS Hundreds Of lots at all sorts of prices, from £lO to £250 Dining-room Suites from £3 10s. to £75. OnView.</p>
        <p>DRAWING-ROOM FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>For £lO 10s. Avery pretty Drawing-room Suite of seven pieces in silk tapestry. and Cahinet complete. very nicelymade, second-hand, not much soiled, presents a very nice appearance, and a wonderful bargain. For £2O. A good substantial Drawing-room Suite, rather old-fashioned, but delightfully comfortable, upholstered In old Gobelin Tapestry, wonderfully clean for its age. Upholstered in the best possible manner for its age. All the framework is as good as the day it was made, in fact, furniture is not made now as good ar when this suite was manufactured. Consists of Settee, Two Sutherland Easy Chairs, and Six Single Chairs, Cabinet, and Overmantel. A rare bargain. For £35. A rich recherche Drawing-room Suite, in carved Chippendale, covered in exquisite Genoa velvet, with raised flowers ; the framework is exquisitely carved. Suite of seven pieces, also Two extra Occasional Chairs and a tine Cabinet of original des,gn, Overmantel, Two Wine Tables, and Coal Cabinet. A rare Bargain at £35; Hundreds of Suites from £4 to £BO. Cabinets from 50s. to £65. Overmantels from 15s. to £25. Thousands of Knic- knace.</p>
        <p>BEDROOM FURNITURE BARGANS.</p>
        <p>For £7 10s. A Capital 31t. 61n. American Walnut Bedroom Suite.withenclosed stands, pedestal chairs,and rail com- plete. A downright good, substantial bedroom suite, quite as good as any new one,and in siilendid condition. A Bargain. For £lO. A very similar Suite,but 41 t. wide,in Satinwood.for £lO, cost £25 when new ; has been entirely repolished and renovated ; equal to new; guaranteed for ten years ; well worth now. For £l5. A Massive Solid Brown Oak Bedroom Suite, 51t. wide, the wardrobe being in two parts, one long cen- tre door fitted with plate glass, the panels of the end doors are heavily carved in bas relief; both washstand and dressing table are fully enclosed ; three chairs, pedestal, and towel horse complete. A fine, massive, handsome suite Inspect early, as it is bound to go quickly. Worsley's. Eor £2l. A Magnificent flit. Bedroom Suite, solid through- out, in exquisitely figured wood, cost £6O when new ; 41t. pedestal dressing table and washstand,table. de nuit,and three chairs. The wardrobe is very hand- some, being a fine, gold design, projecting centre door. The suite is cheap at £5O to-day, and is a great bargain.</p>
        <p>Worsley'e. Hundreds of Bedroom Suites from 355. to £l5O. Hendreds of Wardrob,s from 355. to £25. Hundreds of Pairs of Stands from 10s. 6d. to £25.</p>
        <p>BEDSTEAD BARG AIN S.</p>
        <p>For £5. A Magnificent Solid Brass Bedstead, cost £2O, with spring mattress and wool overlay complete. A won3er- ful bargain. For £2 10s. A Brass Bedstead nnd Spring Mattress, together with wool overlay and bolster and two pil.ows, cost £l2. For Li 15s. A Good Brass and Enamelled Bedstead, Spring Mattress, and Wool Overlay.</p>
        <p>A Capital Lot of BEDSTEAD slightly soiled. being cleared during our Big Sale at ss. to 10s. each; 5,000 Bedsteads in stock from 4s. to £2B e telt. All stoe{ Spring Mattresses and Bedding being cleared out at any price.</p>
        <p>SI TT I N G-R 0 1 FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>In this department an immense clearance must be.effected. We have an enormous lot of odd lots here, such a. odd Couches, Sos, Chairs, Cheffoniers. Bookeases, Tables, Dressers, which we propose to mark down, and offer at startling prices. We briefly enumerate a few : —Hair Cloth Couch, Ilk mAiloganv. 21i., cost Li ; Splendid Sofa, 253. A fine lot of chairs, all mahogany, at ss. each, some worth 205., each. Mahogany Cheff mier, 355. ; with plate glass back,4sB. several old-fashioned Dining Fables from 105. to 20s. Loo Table, in mahogany, cost £lO, for 20s. Several Spanish l‘fahogany Carved Back Dressers, worth £lO to £l2 each, for £4. £5 and £6. Pull-sizel Painted Dress-rs, with sycamore tops, 7 drawers and c,•pbo trds, for 203. to 353. Kitchen Chairs Is. each. Bedroom Chairs. Is. 34. each. Chest of Drawers, 10a. to 205. each, Thousands of odd articles to clear at any</p>
        <p>OFFICE FURNITURE</p>
        <p>We have several job lots of Office Furniture to elem., also several Second-hand American De.iks, Cylinders, Fiat-tops, Registered. &amp;c. Flat-tops 105. to 50s. etch. Cylinder Desks 24 t.) £8 105. American DeSks, £6 to £l2 each. Chairs: Pigeon-holes, Bookc tees, and Cuphotrds (-rally cheap: A r tre opportunity for picking up something cheap and good.</p>
        <p>WORSLEY, TI E PANTHEON,</p>
        <p>"W. H.</p>
        <p>GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER, CLOSE TO CENTRAL STATION. HOURS OF BUSINESS, 9 TO 7 BVEItY DAY.</p>
        <p>Ketttro to Mt Obitor.</p>
        <p>MR. PEERS ON CHRISTIAN "CONTRACTING OUT."</p>
        <p>Bs Ist nichts schrecklicher als.eine thatlge Unwissenheit</p>
        <p>—Goethe.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l hope Mr. Peers will pardon my adoption of his ree.ent practice of putting a text at the head of his effu- sions. If imitation be indeed the sincerest form of flattery then Mr. Peers is paying a high compliment to the clergy by his new custom ; it would be well if he had the moral courage to admit that his letters resemble, in everything but candour,a famous sermon of Rowland Hill which began :—" My brethren, we will first go through the text, we will then go round the text,and we will then go away from the text altogether." May I be allowed to congratulate him on his consist- ency ? He is convinced that a Christian is either a fool or a hypocrite. One week he writes to you pointing out that we believe certain ancient errors, therefore we are fools ; we politely explain that he is mistaken as to our beliefs and that we, in common with the rest of the civilized part of mankind, have cast aside some ancient theories which we have found to be erroneous. He ac- cepts our disclaimer and informs us that we are a set of hypocrites. I will not comment'on the studiedly offensive style of his letter headed "Christian contracting out," but would remark that the argument is invalid and shows absurd prejudice. A modern astronomer who shared all the be- liefs of Ptolemy or a modern doctor who shared all the beliefs of Galen would justly be deemed a fool ; but surely ne sane corson would call. either the astronomer or the doctor a hypocrite because he had kept himself abreast of modern knowledge. Yet, this is the treat- ment accorded by Mr. Peers to his Christian contem- poraries. His Resurrection proved the Divinity of Jesus; for Christians His teachings are Divinely authoritative. But His Apostles were Jews; the early church comprised both Jews and Greeks; and so, even in the New Testa- ment times, the pure teaching of the Son of God began to be confused with current superstitions and many old Jewish ideas became current amongst Christians. Those old Jewish errors and the later Greek speculations which are combined with them to form the popular Christian theology of the second and later centuries, have nothing to do with the Christianity of Christ, which alone is Divinely authoritative. The main features of that teaching stand out clearly enough, but about minor points there is room for difference of opinion because the teaching of our Lord has been somewhat coloured in passing through the minds of His Apostles to the pages of the New Testament. Surely it is as much our duty to cling to the truth as it is to reject the error which has surrounded it.</p>
        <p>The importance of the subject emboldens me to tres- pass on your space. for I recognise with Goethe that much mischief may be wrought by the energetic ignor- ance of a sincere but shallow sceptic.—Yours truly, LAYMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MR. JOHN PEERS AND THE PROTECTION OF</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN PEERS AND THE PROTECTION OF</p>
        <p>MISSIONARIES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am diverted from taking up the thread of my subject by a short passage in Mr. Peers' last letter, in which, with kis usual audacity he accuses me of sug- gesting the false, or suppressing the true. Let the facts be stated. In the year 1853 we were at variance with China, the upshot of which resulted in what is known as the Treaty of Tientsin, which came into general operation towards the close of 1860. By the terms of this Treaty diplomatic agents might reside at Pekin; the Christian religion was to be protected by the Chinese authorities ; and the British subjects were to be allowed to travel for pleasure, or business in all parts of the Empire. Such were among the privileges granted by the Chinese Government. Traders, travellers, scientific ex- plorers, and missionaries were all alike to be protected. Now Mr. Peers, were the religious societies the diplomats who brought the Chinese Government to that arrange- ment? And do you think that there would have been ' either justice or common sense—seeing a treaty had to be arranged—to provide for the safety of traders and other classes of the British subjects, and to single out for exclusion for its benefits, the men, who, under any circumstances of danger, not only would " dare," but who had for nearly 50 years previously " dared," to carry the gospel of Christ into that dark region of the earth ? For, are you so ignorant, Mr. Peers, not to have known that Morrison entered China in the year 1807 and initiated a work, which is destined to be the downfall of paganism in that land ? Not, however, to digress—it appears that your righteous soul is vexed at the thought of British bayonets beinc, requisi- tioned to maintain the rights of a British subject if he happens to be a Missionary. You would perhaps allow that it is quite the proper thing where it is some mer- cenary affair of Commerce and where the pocket gains of the British tax-payer have been unfavourably affected. The treaty in question was made to protect British sub- jects, whoever they may be, unless guilty of violating the laws of the country ; and I repeat it, that it is utterly false to charge religious socieLies with "forcing" the gospel of Christ upon the Chinese, whom you call an unwilling people,to receive it. Their part is to offer and persuade the people to accept their message, and not to force it ' • and as Chinese law does not forbid them to do so much they have a right to claim its protection. But an unlawful and brutal mob happens to attack, to plunder and destroy the property, and lives of Christian Missionaries and the Chinese government is called upon to compensate for loss and to punish the cruel and law- less ruffians—nor does the government deny its duty to do so—or refuse to carry out its engagements—and this, Mr. Peers, according to your enlightened dictum, is to use" force" in thrustiug down the throes of the " Yellows "the Christian religion. Be it so ; but must folk will judge that such force was never more right- eously employed. But the whole phase of this question becomes changed when the real truth of it is know. Mr. Peers has either very little knowledge of Chinese character and policy, or, much as" he abominates lying" he evidently sup- presses what is well known to be the truth touching the hostility of the "Yellows "to missionary efforts,l affirm that the subject of religion has little or nothing to do in the case; and I have unimpeachable evidence at hand which will confirm this Statement to the utmost.</p>
        <p>I must close however for the present but not without assuring Mr. Peers that I abominate lying as much as he can possibly do (even for " the greater glory of God —if that is conceivable)—and none the less, the profane babble of the class he represents, the whole trend of which can only make for the greater glory of the devil. —Yours truly,</p>
        <p>ZA.MINER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>13th February, 1896. _ LOOK UPON THIS PICTURE —AND UPON THAT." " Comparialons are odorous."—Mrs. Malaprop.</head>
        <p>13th February, 1896. _ LOOK UPON THIS PICTURE —AND UPON THAT." " Comparialons are odorous."—Mrs. Malaprop.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Despite the dictum of the above quoted excel- lent lady, I wish to make a few comparisions. And if my" unco gold " critics—between whom I find myself sandwiched in last issue, as a very black diamond in a precious setting, a sinner in a saintly sandwich—if they should find my comparisions a little •' odorous "to have an " ancient and fish like smell "—they have only themselves to thank, as having raised this "stink in the nostrils "by their own persistent stirring. But first of all let me compliment" mine adversaries" upon their modesty. Tbey are determined the world shall not know to whose brilliant efforts my converoion when effected is due! With noble self-sacrifice they anonym- ously try to pluck me as a brand from the burning, in spite of the danger of getting hold of the wrong end of stick, and burning their fingers! Talking of modesty, see how " Zaminer " does not openly claim to have all the logic and right reasoning on his side,but leaves the judicious reader to infer as much. " Your logic Mr. Peers is equally bad and worthless." Yes ! It is always the "Other fellow's " logic that is "bad and worthless." Any logic—good, bad or indiffer- ent—will pass muster if it be on our side. When " Zaminer " deigns to leave rant and give a specimen of his logic we may be able to criticise it ; at present it is only conspicuous by its abs:nce. The older of your readers will remember a half-cracked blacking-hawker— " Sammy Snig "—who made the lanes and alleys about Warrington echo by his appeals to the unregenerate to "Come to Jesus." Now " Sammy" would have reeled aff reams of rant such as " Zaminer's " last, and with as little reasoning or logic in it. And,to such as "Sammy," Christianity—the religion par-excellence of the poor, in pocket and in intellect—is preeminently adapted. No nation or people above the rank of ignorance and barbarism ever did or ever will embrace it. To the cultured it is, and ever has been, as it was to the cultured Greeks' " foolishness." No man of mature mind and in possession of present day knowledge—not previously steeped in the lethe of "Christian" training—would'accept it if placed before him in its biblical form, uneviscerated and " un-con- tracted." For what, to such a man, would its accept- ance involve? The exchange of secularly acquired knowledge for the crudities:of a fantastic belief. Call poor " Zaminer's " ill-selected witnesses—Plato and Seneca—endow them with present day knowledge and put them to the inquisition. Ask them if they can accept as a revelation from Heaven, the childish cosmo;ony of Moses with its heathen fable of Adam and the talking serpent ; if they would believe the earth was " made" in six days by two differ- ent makers, that man was created. specially, and if so why so many traces of the transition stages of his evolution—evidences of his animal origin—are still there to deny this fable? Ask them if they believe any God worthy of the name and not the barbarous conception of a barbarous age, would submit this inexperienced "creation" to a cruel and useless experiment—the con- sequences and results of which he must have foreknown? Would not this be "foolishness to the Greeks" indeed And yet all this is the formation of Christianity, and to believe one you must swallow the other. Could these philosophers be induced to accept as a revealed reli- gion that which, like their own bodies, had so many contradictory evidences in it, traces of transition through and deviation from still oldn creeds? It would be an insult to the intelli4ence even of ordinary people to ask such a thing. Both our wit- nesses would reject the stultifying presentment. Try to imagine Plato or Seneca, with his £250,000, invested in Britain alone, listening with patience to irresponsible</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22, 1396.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 22, 1396.</p>
        <p>babble,expatiating upon the virtues of taking us thought for the morrow ? Fancy the "bad half-hour" Chris- tianity and its professors would have if interrogated by Seneca and Plato !" Do you Christians take no thought for the morrow "? No ! Then how far have you got in the process of" contracting out"? And how can you have the effrontery to ask us to accept what you reject yourselves. Case dismissed. No, No ! Mr. Zaminer your witnesses, like Balaam, called up to curse would remain to bless scepticism; they would set the Scientific Trinity —observation, investigation, and verification—to test the Trinity of theology ; and if they could solve arithmetic and the complicated relationships involved therein their salvation would be assured.</p>
        <p>As to the other half of my " sandwich " "Vivian Grey," he raises a question often put to Agnostics— " What would you suostitute if you removed Christian- ity ?" The best " answer" ta this query is applied in another question, " What does the housemaid substitute for the dust and cobwebs she removes ? It is merely the dust and cobwebs of traditional superstition which besmirches the fair face of true religion and too effect tually hides from the eyes of men the vital principles of righteousness that we wish to remove. There is one question I wish to put before all my critics for a candid reply—ls the " God "of the Old Testament in whom is no snadow of turning the same God who figures in the New as " the Father ?" History tells us that as men change so does :their conception of the Deity. This is not in accord with Revelation and $a is an "-odorous " comparison. To your readers Mr. Editor, peace, con- tentment and prosperity. —Yours truly, JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BIRDS OF CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>THE BIRDS OF CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the present day, when a great and increasing interest is taken by many people in natural history, our British wild birds claim a large share of the attention of all lovers of nature. Their charming song,varied plum- age, and habits—admirably adapted to meet the condi- tions of life under which each species exists—as well as the direct and indirect economic value of many, and the facility with which they may be observed at all seasons of the year must evar make objects of interest to even the least observant.</p>
        <p>The natural teatines of Cheshire make it an excellent field for the study of bird-life. The wild birds of the east, the broad central plain with its wooded valleys and secluded meres, and the seacoast of the western border supply the conditions necessary to the existence and well-being of many different kinds of birds. In spite, however, of these natural advantages. Cheshire is still behind the neighbouring counties of York, Lancashire and Derby, inasmuch as no w.n.k dealing with its native birds has hitherto been published. The reclamation and drainage of waste lands, and the increase of population, with a consequent restriction of the haunts of many birds, coupled with the growing scarcity of several species—due to their wanton destruc- tion by unscrupulous collectors and so-called naturalists —are, perhaps, reason enough why the compilation of a county avi-fauna should be no longer delayed, and, we hope, in the near future, to supply the existing defici- ency. If, however, full justice is to be done to our sub- ject, we must be dependent upon the co-operation and assistance of bird lovers throughout the county. It is obvious that we can have no personal knowledge of many rare birds which are liidden either in private collections or in cases of stuffed birds in farmhouses and gamekeepers' cottages, and for information about such isolated specimens we must rely upon the courtesy of local ornithologists, whose correspondence we heartily invite. Of even greater importance than records of rare species are lists of birds observed in different parts of the county, together with details of local variation and habits or mode of life, and the dates of the arrival and departure of the migratory species. We trust that Cheshire can boast of many bird lovers who will be at sufficient trouble to furnish us with such lists. In conclusion, we may say that any communication with which we may be favoured will be promptly and gratefully acknowledged.—Yours, &amp;c., T. A. COWARD.</p>
        <p>Higher Downs, Bowdon. CHARLES OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Romiley, near Stockport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>IV ESTON.</head>
        <p>IV ESTON.</p>
        <p>MEETINGIOF THE PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met on Friday evening, in the Church Schoolroom. There were present—the Rev. D. S. Bowen, Messrs. Sill, Savage, Tudor, Basnett, Vase, Guest, Ball, Sweetman, and James Collier (clerk). In the absence of the Chairman (the Rey. Canon Wood), it 'was resolved. on the motion of Mr. Basnett, seconded by Mr. Vose,that Mr. Sill preside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SOUTH-ROAD, WHSTON POINT.</head>
        <p>SOUTH-ROAD, WHSTON POINT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk intimated that the overseers had received a communication from the Local Government Board, stat- ing that powers had been given to the District Council of the Private Street Act under the Public Health Act, so that they could widen South-road, Weston Point. This would come into operation on March 2nd.—The Chair- man considered the statement very satisfactory, as it would settle the road question at Weston Point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DANGEROUS ROADS.</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS ROADS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Vose, in accordance with notice of motion, pro. posed "that the attention of the District Council be called to the bad and dangerous condition of Cheshire's lane." He expressed the opinion that perhaps it would be wise to amend the motion to the effect that a com- mittee be appointed from the Parish Council to take the subject into consideration, and advise what should be done. He said the lane led from the Village Inn, into Weston Point, or Bankes'-lane. Anyone travelling from Weston Point into Weston-lane, if the road under notice was made fit for pedestrians or vehicular traffic, would find the distance shortened considerably, and would save 15 minutes in walking to Weston Point from Weston or tice versa. But under existing circumstances it was unfit to be used. Ho could not exactly say whose duty it was to repair the road, but he could affirm that it was in a shameful state and a disgrace to any community. If it had to be made right from the rates,he was sure that the people would be ready to incurs the expense, for it would be a great convenience to the people of Weston.—The Chair- man suggested, as that meeting was the last of the Council, it would be well to stick to the motion as it stood on the agenda.—Mr. Basnett seconded the propo- sition.—The Rev. D. S. Bowen said unfortunately, they had no road tram Weston Point to Runcorn, without coming round by Weston, and by and by they ex- pected to be surrounded by railways, which would make travelling more difficult. If Sandy Waggon-road were put into repair, it would shorten the distance for Weston Point people to Runcorn considerably, as well as to one part of Weston. He moved on amendment that Sandy-lane be improved.—Mr. Savage seconded the amendment. A conversation followed, and ultimately Mr. Ball moved, and Mr. Tudor seconded, "That the attention of the owners of the land adjoining Cheshire's- lane be called to its dangerous condition to pedestrians, and that they be requested to eive the subject their im- mediate consideration."—Mr. Vase withdrawing his pro- position in favour of this, it was unanimously adopted, and the Clerk was instructed to communiate with the gentlemen owning the land.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. The Chairman said as that was the last meeting of the present Council.he desired to move that a vote of thanks be accorded to the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood for the able way in which he had condurted the business dur- ing the past 15 months,and that the Clerk convey this to him.—Mr. Ball seconded, saying he was snre they were all exceedingly sorry for the cause which kept Canon Wood away from the Council. The business under his guidance had been efficiently conducted.—The motion was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GXRDEN.</head>
        <p>THE GXRDEN.</p>
        <p>The following information which will be of use to those interested in gardening has been supplied us for publication by the Secr,tary of the Warrliagton Floral and Horticultural Society. FEBRUARY. TABLE FOR SOWING.</p>
        <p>SOW FROM FOR COMPETITION IN *Beans Ist to 15th July. *Beans 15th to 28th August. *Brocoli Ist to 28-,h November. *Cauliflower Ist to 7th August and Sept. __ . ._. . .</p>
        <p>*E—ar-ly—Cabl;age -Ist- to 20'h Sep-tember. - Celery 7th to 20th August and Sept. *Leeks Ist to 7th Aug., Sept., and Oct. *Onions Ist to 7th Aug., Sept., and Oct. Parsley Ist to 7th Aug., Sept., and Oct. *Parsnips Ist to 10th Aug., Sept., and Oct. *Peas 15th to 30th July. Tomatoes Ist to 10th July and August. Antirrhinums, uentstemons, begonias, cockscombs, gloxinias, pansies. violas, dahlias, scabious, flower seeds which should be sown under glass this month. Those marked with an asterisk (*) should also be sown for general crop this month. The dates given are aver- age ones. In very late districts or in very early ones, growers will require to depart a little from the times stated above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TABLE FOR PLANTING.</head>
        <p>TABLE FOR PLANTING.</p>
        <p>Auriculas, any time weather is suitable. Climb!ng plants, any time weather is suitable. Gladioli, in pots in frames early in the month to start them. Herbaceous, towards the end of the month and in March. Liliums, pot for early display. Phloxes, towards the end of the month and in March. Violas, Picotees, and carnations, towards the end of the month and in March. Polyanthus, any time in suitable weather. Roses, any time in suitable weather. Cabbage, greens, savoys, *shallots, can be planted this month and in March for general use or exhibition purposes.</p>
        <p>THE NEW HIGH SHERIFF FOR LA.NCASHIRE. Sir Peter Walker, of Liverpool, and Osmaston Manor, Derbyshire, has been appointed high sheriff of the County Palatine of Lancaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>COLLISION BETWEEN CYCLISTS AT EARLESTOWN. On Monday at noon Dr. Harold Watkins, when riding his bicy2le up Queen-st., collided with Mr.T. Bowes, who wers also cycling, at the jnnction of Market-st. with Queen-st. Neither of the riders were injured, but the machine Mr. Bowes was rid- ing was slightly damaged. POLICE CASES AT EARLESTOWN. At the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday morning before Messrs. Gillespie and Borron, Samuel Webster was fined ss. and costs, for being drunk and disorderly. Margaret McLaughlin and Sarah Pooley were sent to goal for seven days for loiter- ing.—On Monday at Earlestown Police Station, be- fore Mr. J. W. Emmett, Patrick Corcoran, J. Mar- shall, and Polly Bowers, and Patrick Tulley, were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk. Mar- shall and Tulley went to goal. SAD END OF AN OLD WOMAN AT NEWTON. On Wednesday night about six o'clock an old woman named Elizaoeth Jackson, 76 years of age, wife of James Jackson of 10 Gibraltar Cottages, Newton, came to a sad end by falling downstairs. She was going upstairs to straighten the beds, and being rather feeble,itwas supposed she overbalanced herself, and fell to the bottom. Her husband tried to lift her, but being unable to do so, got the assis- tance of his son, and together they placed her on a couch and sent for Dr. Heywood. She however never regained consciousness and died half an hour later.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY (EBENEZER LoDGE•) The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening, in the Co-operative Hall. Bro. A. Morris C.T., presided, and there was a good attendance of members and visitors. Two candidates were proposed for membership. Bro. A. Morris gave report of District Council held at Liver- pool on Saturday. The Secretary was requested to convey the congratulations of the members to Bro. and Sister T. Wharton, late Miss Haden, on the oc- casion of their marriage. Songs, recitations and readings were given by Sisters Taylor and Wright, and Bros. Brown, Morris, W. Wharton and Hatton, rater which the meeting closed. ACCIDENT TO A FOOTBALL PLAYER. On Saturday afternoon during a match between Earlestown and Hindley on the Earlestown Foot- Club ground, A. Limond, the centre half back for Earlestown, was charged by one of the opposing players, receiving an injury to his left shoulder. lie retired to the pavilion, but as nothing could be found to be broken, after a rest he resumed play and continued on the field until the end of the game. During the evening he went to see Dr. Valentine, who stated that he had received a frac- ture of the collar bone and be was treated accord- ingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke, CT., presided, and there was a large at- tendance of members and visitors. One candidate was admitted to membership. The digest of business for the annual district lodge meeting at Liverpool was submitted. Arrangements were made for a visit to the Ashton lodge on Thursday next, A vote of sympathy was passed with Bro. Rev. J. A. Lee, D.C.T., who is seriously ill. The lodge was afterwards declared open, and a large number of friends were admitted, the room being well filled. Bro. J. Hatton, P.C.T., was responsible for the pro- gramme which consisted of a number of vocal and instrumental items which were submitted by Bros. A. Howarth, W. Willis, C. Drew, J. R. Muscroft, Antrobus (Sc. Helens). CI. W. Dennis, and W. Brown, Sisters A. Newman, and F. Holliday. EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The 141st quarterly meeting of the Earlestown Co-operative Society was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening. Mr. John Moss presided, and there was an abnormally large attendance of members.the hall being well filled. The report and statement of accounts,sh owed that the cash receipts for the quarter amounted to £4,064 4s. 2d.,being an increase ov3r last quarter of £124 103., and of the corresponding period last year a decrease of £45 14s. 10d. The net profits available for dividend on purchases were £566 135., from which it was pro- posed to pay a dividend of 23. 9d. in the which would require £552 4s.,and add £lO to the dividend reserve fund, leaving a balance to the contingent fund of £4 93., thus making the reserve fund £451 10s. 'lid. During the quarter 13 new members had been admitted, and 33 struck off, leaving 943 on the books, 643 of whom had brought in checks. In the election of president, Messrs. Moss and Forrester were proposed, the result of the voting showing that Mr. Forrester was elected by 95 votes against Mr. Moss 67. Mr. S. Barrett was elected on the committee in:plaee of Mr. Forrester. The auditors' fee was increased to £2 per quarter each. Mr. P. Parr introduced and explained the working of a new check system, which it is proposed to institute in connection with the society. An acrimonious discussion followed as to the status of the secretary and manager in relation to the committee, and it was ultimately decided that the committee needed no further guide or authority than that conveyed by the rules. CONGREGATIONAL CItURCII, NEWTON. The annual social gathering of the members of the Congregational Church,Newton, was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. There were about 130 present to tea, the ladies of the congrega- tion giving the tea, thelproceeds of which were for the purpose of purchasing tea urns. After tea, the Rev. J. Allatt presided. The.tables were arranged in various parts of the room, nicely decked with flowers and fairy lamps. Different kinds of games such as chess, draughts, &amp;c., were provided and the company sitting in groups amused themselves as they felt inclined. The Rev. J. Allatt, addressing those present, reviewed the work of the church for the past 33 years, during which time he had been pastor. He referred to the structural changes which had taken place in the church,and also in the music and hymns, making. a kind reference to Mr. W. Thompson, the organist. He briefly touched upon the work of the past year, and said they had lost four members, two from death, and two by removal to other places,but these had been counterbalanced by a slight increase on the church membership.— Mr. John Owen presented a statement of accounts, showing the finances to be in a satisfactory condi- tion.—Mr. Syme (auditor) testified to the excellent manner in which the accounts were kept, and pro- posed a vote of thanks to Mr. Owen for his services. Mr. Cooper seconded. and it was carried with acclamation.—Mr. T. Thompson submitted a state- ment of the penny bank, which was of interest to those present. Songs, violin, and whistling solos were given during the evening by Messrs. W. H. Thompson, J. Nichols, F. Mather, J. Howard and Miss Mather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EARLESTOWN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The second annual eoncert given by the Earlestown Orchestral' Society took place on Thursday evening in the Town Rall,Earlestown,before a large audience. This Society under the leadership of Mr. A. Ashley. of War- rington, has made rapid strides in the direction of effi- ciency, and the bill of fare they placed before their pat- rons on Thursday night proved that their intention was to raise the standard of the concerts usually held in Earlestown. Last year their effort was a good one, abd we then predicted that if they kept up their secured re- putation and placed before the public only first-class, healthy music, they need have no fear as to the result. The programme on Thursday night was in every sense excellent, the sclections were good, and the artistes were in every way equal to their tasks. The band,under the conductorship of Mr. Ashley,had some very difficult tasks to perform, and they came out of the ordeal most creditably. Their selections were an overture, " Tan- credi," gavotte, " Immer Wieder," selection, " Reminis- cences of England." This was perhaps the best item,as it gave plenty of scope for solos on euphonium, clarionet, and cornets, the other selection being comic polka "Sneezing." A clarionet solo, " Cavatina and finale," was played in a masterly manner by Mr. A. Ashley, and he received a hearty recognition. As a violinist Mr. John Lawson in a couple of items placed to his name,showed he was not by any means a novice, and his execution left nothing to be desired. Of the vocalists Miss Marie Burnett, Madame Annie Parry,Mr.Fred Owens, and Mr. A. F. Thornborough, we can only speak in the highest terms of praise, their singing being thoroughly enjoy- able. Mr. Loui Parry :(Chester) supplied the humour, and he of course, received the usual double recognition. Mr. F. A. Dibb proved himself a clever soloist and ac- companist. Altogether the concert was a rare musical treat, and was thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Baxendale, who was murderously attacked with a hatchet a month ago at Birkenshaw. near Bradford, by a man named Cromwell, died on Tuesday afternoon. Lord Stalbridge, the chairman of the London and North-Western Railway Company, informed the share- holders of the half-yearly meeting on Tuesday that the company's Manchester traffic was "growing enor- mously." Among other interesting statistics quoted by his Lordship was an increase of 1,640,138 in the number of third-class passengers carried in the last half- year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>AGED PERSON'S TREAT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>AGED PERSON'S TREAT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Perhar s the most interesting gathering which assem- bles in the Public Hall, Runcorn, throughout the year is on the occasion of what has now become known as the aged persons' treat. On Tuesday, the event again took place when nearly 500 persons over the age of 60 years responded to the invitations which had been issued. A number of committees had been appointed to make adequate arrangements, and it is gratifying to state that the event passed off without the slightest hitch. For this,credit is due to the following gentleman: Collecting, Messrs. Imison, F. J. Norman, Firth, Stafford,Coventry, Wood, Woodland, Reynolds, Bellamy, Posnett, Poole, Wiswall, Lake, W. Crosby and Meredith. Tea and pro- visions :Messrs. Bowyer, Coventry, Shaw, Crosby,Wood, Collier, Robinson and Reynolds; Hall Committee: Messrs. Woodland, Poole, T. Riley, D. Littlemore, G. Hitchmough, Williams, Shaw, Byfield, T. Leach and G. A. Hayes; Entertainment Committee: Messrs. James Littler, J. Holford, J. Leathwood, C. C. Le Couteur, W. Cooke and W. Crosby ; Stores Committee: Messrs. Sweetman and H. Hughes; Scrutiny Committee: Messrs. Sutton, Bowyer, Howard, Williams, P. Leath- wood, T. Riley, Taylor, Langshaw, Sweetman and Moulton ; Cabs' Committee : Messrs. Williams, Littler. A. Riley, Parry, J. Ellison and D. Littlemore. An excellent bill of fare was provided. It consisted of bread and butter, beef and ham sandwiches, seed and currant cake, and before rising from the tea tables, the women were the recipients of a packet of tea, and the men of loz. of tobacco, the gift of Messrs. Thomas Ogden, Liverpool. and R. Moore, Church-st., Runcorn. The following ladies presided at the tea tables :—Mes• dames J. R. Frith, T. Wood, T. Cooke, J. D. Jones, J. E. Meredith, R. H. Posnett, Dr. McKenzie, Coventry, Wiswall, F. J. Norman, Crosby, J. M. Frith, E. Mar- shall, J. Wilson, Beck, Percival, Littlemore, Rogers, Richardson, W. Shaw, sen., W. Shaw, jun., T. Bowyer, W. Collier, G. Howard, P. Leathwood, T. Williams, G. H. Kirkham, W. Hughes, A. Riley, Arthur Riley, Tur- ner, R. Moore,and Nurse Buckenham, and Misses Wood- land, Pierpoint, Millington, C. Littler, Smith, and Hea- therington. A grand concert was afterwards provided, and at this there was a good attendance of the public. Mr. E. Imison, J.P. (chairman of the Urban Council) presided, and he was supported on the platform by the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., F. J. Norman, J.P., M. Sweetman, G. H. Kirkham, E. Marshall, J. Wilding, J. E. Meredith, T. Bowyer, Thomas Reynolds, H. Hughes, and others. The opening item was a choral march by a glee party con- ducted by Mr. Holford, in which the solo part was taken by Miss K. Edwards. A brief address by the Chairman followed. He thought be was in a position to congratulate them and himself upon the improved condition of things as com- cared with that time twelve months ago when they had a similar gathering. An institution that survived such distressing times as those experienced last year was likely to continue amongst them. There was no great merit attaching to the Chairman of the District Council in sustaining it. There were many gentlemen outside the Council who supported it, and if evet a chairman was inclined to give it up, he (Mr. Imison) was of the opinion that he would have a hot time of it. (Laughter.) For himself, he felt bound to do his utmost to make that annual gathering a decided success. (Applause.) The programme was then continued. Mr. Wesley Cooke very feelingly sang "Daddy," and Miss Pope suc- ceeded him with a song entitled " The Flight of Ages." Several further items were contributed by the glee party, and the humorous songs by Mr. T. Cooke and Mr. C. Atherton were both encored. Miss Heatherington was very successful in her song, and was deservedly en- cored. Responding, she gave a pleasing rendition of "Home, sweet Home." An interesting item was an in- strumental trio in which Miss Milne, of Frodsham, per- formed on the mandoline, Mr. C. C. Le Couteur, auto- harp, and Mr. William Cooke, piano, took part. In res- ponse to continued applause, the performers played a second selection. Songs were also well rendered by Mrs. Temperlev, Messrs. C. Leathwood,Worrall,and Shingler. Mr. Philip Speakman also delivered an address. A pleasant evening's entertainment concluded with the singing of "God save the Queen." The accompanists were Messrs. C. C. Le COuteur and William Cooke. A vote of thanks was passed to those who had in any way assisted at the treat,on the proposition of Mr.R.Peacock, one of the recipients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH,</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual tea party and enter- tainment in connection with the above place of worship was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-st. Tea was served in the hall and heartily enjoyed by some 300 people who were attended to by Mesdames Carleton, Hatton, Rawson, Welsby, Fairclough, B. L. Pierpoint, McGregor, Reynolds, Pye, W. M. Monks, Byering, Fer- guson, Wallhead,Mainwaring,Spinks, West,Mr. William Monks, and the Misses Rimmer, Harding, Leigh, and Reid. After the eatables had been disposed of the room was cleared and seats arranged for the entertainment which followed and at which there was an excellent at- tendance. The Mayon (Councillor James Fairclough) presided, and congratulated those assembled on having an excel- lent vicar with a good wife. He bad known the Rev.E. C. E. Carleton ever since he came to Warrington, and he had been his (the Mayor's) private chaplain for two years, the work of which office he performed efficiently and well. - - - • - • -</p>
        <p>_ The following programme of pleasing and varied items was then gone through in a very creditable manner; glee," Where are thou, beam of light," Choir; pianoforte solo, Miss M. Faircleugh ; song, "True till death," Mr. Ealing; piano and violin duet, "Blue bells ,f Scotland." Miss Dunbabin and Master Fred Randall; - citation, Quarrel scene from "The School for Scandal," Miss Spinks song, " Mary of Argyle," Mr. C. Hoult ; song. " Your story and mine," Miss L. White (With Violin Obligato by Master Randall), pianoforte solo, Miss Byerley ; song, "The Storm Fiend." Mr. W. J. Forshaw ; song, "Whisper and I shall hear," Miss Wenham ; song, "Anchored," Mr. Westbrook ; instrumental quintette (three violins, mandoline and piano), "Waltz," Misses Lea, Rowson, Pierpoint, N. and L. Tunstall ; song, Jack's Yarn," Mr. Booth ; humorous sketch, Mr. A. Grounds; song, "The Village Blacksmith," Mr. McGregor; piano and violin duet, " Tarentelle," Misses Lea and Pierpoint ; ventriloquial entertainment, "Mirth and Mimicry," Mr. A. Grounds; glee,"England," Choir; humorous sketch. "The Village Concert," Mr. W. J. Bell. Mr. Percy Randall, accompanied on the piano. During an interval in the programme the Rev. E.C.E. CARLETON said that the past 12 months had been a very prosperous period for the church and he expressed his thanlrfulness for the ready co-operation he bad received from the body of lay-workers. He thought their work had been carried on on very sound principles and that strict economy had been exercised. In conn2ction with that Mr. Carleton mentioned the name of Mr. Welsby, who held a very responsible post associated with the finance of the church, and be was sure they would agree with him in saying his (Mr. Welsby's) efficient and eco- nomical management, entitled him to the thanks of everyone, ard he believed that everyone fully recognised that fact. (Applause.) He need not say that Mr. Dun- babin had been equally as efficient and economical, as treasurer of the Sunday School in administering the funds at his disposal, which were not very large, but which, he was glad to say, were sufficient for the pur- pose. Owing to the smallness of their funds they were unable to embark on any ambitious scheme, but if their richer neighbours came forward, and offered to provide in the future for the work they had to do in the district he was sure they would be very grateful for the assist- ance. It was a large and growing parish,with two large churches, and with two clergy to supervise the whole work. He did not for a moment underrate the very valuable assistance they got from the staff of workers, and on Sundays from Mr. Radford, but what he did say was that Mr. Wild and himself were practically alone, in the way of parochial visitation ,and the religious instruc- tion by the clergy in the day schools, and if they had not the assistance of his wife, and he was bound to say it, affairs would not go on as satisfactorily as they did. Fie was afraid they would not get extra clerical aid, for that meant something like an additional £l2O or £l5O per annum, and that was entirely out of the question. If they had not to support their day schools, perhaps some of the money given to that object would go a long way to supplying what they required, and he reminded them that the church day schools throughout the coun- try were auprorted in a way that involved considerable sacrifice. He was hoping that the Government would see their way to giving some increased grants to the voluntary schools ; such assistance that would enable them to give the collections at the harvest festival ser- vices to the curate' fund,which he was sorry to say had lately suffered some diminution. He expressed his thanks to all, who had supported that fund, and he had, likewise, to remember that similar assistance had been rendered by Mr. Owen. To the Mayor and Mayoress they were deeply indebted, and to Mrs. Fairclough in particular, as she came weekly into the parish, and worked hard at one of their most important branches. (Loud applause.) Afterwards a hearty vete of thanks was passed to the Chairman for presiding.</p>
        <p>On the completion of the programme the room was again cleared and dancing indulged in until 1 a.m., the whole of the proceedings having proved most enjoyable.</p>
        <p>Letters received from Yokohama state that the Em- press of Japan has contributed a thousand yen (£108) to the fund that has been raised on behalf of the families of the men of H.M.S. Edgar, who lost their lives in the boating accident off Chemulpo a short time since. It has now been definitely arranged that the Duke of Connaught shall represent the Queen at the coronation of the Czar and Czarina. A young woman was shot in a railway carriage near Copenhagen on Sunday night by a man who was travel- ing with her. Both died during the evening. The girl as been identified as a seamstress, and her murderer as lecturer at the University of Lund,named Morgan,who Eaid to be a Cambridge graduate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CONCERT AT WARGRAVE.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CONCERT AT WARGRAVE.</p>
        <p>The annual juvenile entertainment, promoted by Mr. Chandley,head master at the Wargrave National Schools, took place on Monday evening in the schoolroom. There was a crowded and appreciative audience, who by their applause gave evidence of their admiration, both of the efforts of Mr. Chandley, and the excellence of the per- formance of the children. The entertainment was pleas- ingly varied, and by the ever changing items, there was no fear that it would become monotonous. Not only was this so, but every opportunity was given to show the qualities of the children' which otherwise would be dormant. Mr. Claandley had plenty of material to work upon' and the result showed that he had chosen the best. Of course it must be understood that an en- tertainment of this kind cannot be put before the pub- lic without a vast amount of preparatory labour, and in this task he has been ably assisted by Miss Ratcilffe,Miss Dane, Miss Sweetlove, and Miss Holt, who have given most valuable help. The programme opened with a piano solo, " Jenny Jones," which was admirably played by Master A. Purnell. Following this came a chorus by the senior boys and girls The Queen's Navee." In this as in all the other choruses the children showed they had been carefully trained. The voices were well balanced, and excellent time was kept. A song, " Eight little mothers," was given by that number of mites, each of whom carried a doll, and their actions created the greatest merriment. The senior boys and girls sang "The angels are watching," with excellent taste. Much amuse- ment was caused by Master Harry Naylor in the recita- tion" Jack-in-box." Masters Wrigley, Sutton and Tay- lor, made capital representatives of the Jack tar in the song," The powder monkey," and they were loudly ap- plauded. The action song, "Sunshine in school," by junior girls.was followed by a recitation, "The unhappy schoolboy," in which Master P. Laithwaite pourtrayed the imaginary hard lot of the boy who does not care for school. The bar-bell, drill by senior boys and girls was capitally gone through, and "The sentry's song," was sung by Masrer A. Tarbuck in such an excellent manner that it was redemanded,but the demand was not complied with. The closing item of the first part, the Castanet drill dance, was perhaps the best item of that part,the 12 boys and girls going through with a precision that deserved the vociferous encore they received. It was too much, however' to expect them to repeat it. Miss M. Sweetlove opened the second part with a piano solo, "The Swiss herdsman," and the junior boys and girls followed with the song, " Sailor's life," eight boys afterwards indulging in a hornpipe. Die part song, " Sweet evening bells," by the senior boys and girls,was followed by the recitation," Women of Mumble's Head," which was effectively given by Miss Selina Molyneux, who, for one so young, was no mean elocutionist. The song, " The Fairy Jane," was well given by Masters Wrigley, Sutton and Taylor, was followed by a tableaux of nursey rhymes, in which a number if infants naively introduced such characters as" Rock a bye baby,"" The cat and the fiddle," "The old woman and her broom," (which deserves special mention), " Old Mother Hub- bard,"" Jack and Jill," "The Man in the Moon," "Little Boy Blue," "Little Jack Horner," " Mother Goose." "The Colonel's Song" was admirably given by Master Wrigley, in character. The next item, vocal quadrilles, by eight juniors, was exceedingly well done, there not being a single hitch, and the youngsters deserved all the applause they got. Master H. Bradshaw gave a capital interpretation of the song," The Lori Chancellor's song." The next item was an exhibition of real moving wax- work figures, over which Master H. Bradshaw presided as showman. The figures were well got up,and the des- cription was splendidly given. The representations were "W. G. Grace," " American Sambo," " Queen Bess," "Mrs. Sqeers and her school,"" Joan of Arc," "William Tell," " School Master and the late scholar," " Sims Reeves," " Paderewski," "Boadicea," and "Football player." The concluding chorus was "Sweet good- night." The Rev. J. Carson presided and offered a few con- gratulatory remarks at the close. As a large number could net obtain admittance, the entertainment was repeated on Tuesday evening, when there was again a fair audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>LECTURE ON POULTRY KEEPING AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON POULTRY KEEPING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A lecture was given at the Town Hall, Newton, on Wednesday evening, by Mr. Thomas Carr, lecturer, engaged by the Lancashire County Council, under the auspices of the Newton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council Technical Instruction Committee, on "Poultry Keeping." Councillor J. Simpson presided, in .the un- avoidable absence of Councillor F. J. Wood,and besides the lecturer, he was supported by Councillor T. Ormand. IR the course of his lecture Mr. Carr said there was nothing that would pay like keeping poultry if they would properly study the subject, especially relating to egg producing. The outlay of capital was comparatively small, and the returns were very sure and very quick. It was a surprise to him how little people knew about fowls ; in fact many of them knew absolutely nothing, yet where was there a place in England that offered such a demand for the production of eggs as South Lan- cashire? There was nothing wanted so much personal attention as fowls and the better they were attended to the better they would pay. He spoke against in-and-in breeding and advised that chickens should be hatched in the months of March, April, and May. He considered that the best layiog hens were Leghorns, Minorcas, Hamburgs, Andalusions, and Hoodans. In answer to questions Mr. Carr said he gave his fowls nothing but ground cockle,a kind of screening from flour mills,and he only fed them once a day. A vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer on the motion of Mr. Liptrott secomded by Mr. Sankey, and a similar compliment was paid the Chairman.—Mr.Carr lectured on the same subject in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Thursday night,there being again a large audience. Councillor J. W. Emmett presided, and the lecturer dealt with the feeding of poultry and their diseases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>This Board met in the Board-room at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday afternoon, when the chair was occupied by Mr. J. Knowles. There were also present in addition to the members of the Rural Council, the Rev. J. S. Simpson, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. A. R.Norman, Mr. S. Yarwood and Mr. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk). and Mr. Cunnah (workhouse master).</p>
        <p>REFUSING TO BE APPRENTICED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Jeffs, superintendent of the Great Grimsby Ice Company Limited Apprentices Home, Victoria-street, Grimsby, wrote stating that the boy Howard sent by the Runcorn Union refused to be apprenticed, and they therefore had no option but to return him.—The Master reported that the boy had returned to the House, stating that the Ice Company's boats were being sold. Tbe boy had gone to his father's house at Weston.—The Chair- man : Do you expect he will come back here ?—The Master : I should not be at all surprised.—The matter then dropped.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF A NORSE. Nurse Hughes wrote resigning her position of nurse in the house.—Mr. Heywood: This is the old one.—Mr. Mclntyre: It is the one we retained.—The Chairman: Why is she leaving P—The Deputy Clerk: She has got another appointment as companion so the Master in- forms me.—Mr. Brandreth moved, and Mr. Mclntyre seconded, that the resignation be accepted.—The Master was instructed to obtain a successor from the Northern Workhouse Nurses Association. ASSISTANT OVERSEER FOR ACTON GRANGE. The Acton Grange Parish Council nominated Isaac Lawton, of Moore, as assistant overseer of the parish. FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood submitted the report of the Finance Committee whioh recommended the payment of accounts amounting to £3,860 16s. 6d. This included a cheque for £3,680 13s. 10d. for county rate, and the usual amount of out-relief. After the payment of those accounts there would be a deficit of £1,711 17s. 11d., but the Board would receive a cheque from the County Council that would cover the deficiency. He moved the adoption of the report.—Mr. Woodward seconded, and the report was adopted. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A GIRL.—On Wednesday afternoon, ar a girl named Annie Haddock was trying,with some othe children, to work the automatic machine which stands in front of the Castle Hotel, it fell over on her legs, frac turing one, and crushing the other. P.C. Juett, of the St. John Ambulance, rendered first aid, and the girl was conveyed home in a trap belonging to the hotel. She is doing as well as can be expected. WESLEYAN CRICKET CLUB SOIREE.—The annual soiree was held on Wednesday evening,and was of a most suc- cessful character. The attendance was large, and the proceedings were presided over by Mr. McCullock. An excellent musical programme was gone through, and in- cluded songs by the following friends from Runcorn : Miss F. Eden; Messrs. C. Leathwood, A. Clare, and Sanders. Messrs. William Hulse and Robert Eden re- cited, and Mr. H. Holt contributed an instrumental solo. .Light refreshments were handed round. Games were participated in, and greatly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>OILS.—GARLAND, Importer, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>SEwma MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Ireadle £4 4s. witr hindsorne walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON 16 and 18, dankey-st,, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FRODSHAM-.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM-.</p>
        <p>Otr WORKING MEN'S GUILD.—In connection with the a- h institution a lantern entertainment was given 10 Victoria Hall, Frodsham, in aid of the funds of the gn The lantern was manipulated by Mr. George SuttollirvA Manchester, and a considerable number of slides s/7, shown. Recitations were given by Mr. G. StephenON of Manchester, entitled the "Women of Mumbles Hesti., and the Bridgekeeper's Story, with others which vmf, equally interesting ; songs and solos were contributed 26 Messrs. W. Youd, John Jones, and G. Chatwoedt accompaniments being rendered by Messrs. Youd fl Fogg. The guild so far has been of great service in P: viding games and readings for the winter months. A LampeionTEn's SAD END.—Mr. H. Churton,corollg`' held an inquest at the Red Lion Inn, F sham on the body of James Kinsey, aged 39 yt lamplighter and tailor, of Ship-st., on Tuesday ar noon.—Mary Ann Kinsey, wife of the deceased, depo: that her husband left home on Saturday night at 10. to perform his duty of extinguishing the lamps. He Wal perfectly sober. There was no illfeeling existing bet Wee them, and they lived comfortably together. Owing,tl, ' her husband failing to return home at midnight, sw gave information to the police. Questioned by the ja; as to whether her husband had not just cause for jell' ousy through her conduct, she denied that he had. St) stated that only a woman visited her after deceased' departure, but afterwards admitted that a man calls at the house—Sergeant Clays stated that owing to tr previous witness's statement of the non-return of by husband, he and P.C. Price searched for him sevel hours without success. They went out again next mat' ing,and at 10.45 discovered his body in the river Wea, opposite Weston Soapery, on the Frodsham Marsh. was about 20 yards from where he had apparently entered the water. His rod, used for extinguishing do lamps, was found on the side of the river, A verdict of "Found drowned" was returned. GAS AND WATER COMPANY'S ANNUAL MEETING.—TIO meeting of the shareholders was held on Thursday after noon.when monz those present were Messrs. E. Imison• J.P. (in the chair), J. W. Rotlawell, J. R. Frith, W' Whittaker, W. Cunningham, James Handley, J.P., William Handley, and G. Frith (secretary). The Chair man moved the adoption of the report and balance sht, which showed the revenue account with a balance o £B2B 103. 2d. After payment of a dividend of 10 , cent. and 7 per cent. on the respective shares, there Ws.' an individed balance of £613 84.10 d., and also a balanq of £2OO to the reserve fund. Oa April Ist the price a gas was reduced 31 per thousand cubic feet, and the it crease in the consumption continued satisfactory.—The Chairman stated that he thought they could congratir late themselves on the result of the year's working,whieS had been satisfactory to the shareholders and the pub' lic. They were anxious to supply gas to consumers the lowest possible rate, and to consider the interests 0 , the district in every conceivable way. The reduction o; 3d. per thousand cubic feet had led to an increased consumption, and as soon as their position justified, •further reduction would be made. They were lookirg forward to an extension of the works being requ'red• , The directors were thoroughly alive to the necessity t° sell gas as cheaply as possible, and so long as it maitr tamed anything like its present figure, they had nothiof to fear.—The report was adopted.—The retiring dir' tors and auditor were thanked for their services, and 1, elected. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. G. Frith the secretary, and also the Chairman. THE RELIGIOUS TRACT SOCIETY.—The annual meetia of the Frodsham branch of this society was held ia Town Hall, Frodsham, on Tuesday evening, when fbel Rev. F. F. Edmunds, of Overton, presided. The depc:, tation to the meeting was the Rev. J. Wilkins, and 01; gentleman gave a most interesting account, illustrateo by limelight views, of the work in Japan in particular, At the close the usual votes of thanks were accorded t(!, the lecturer, the Chairman, Mr. Turton (who manage) the lantern) and to the secretary of the Frr branch, Mr. Ormrod.</p>
