        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN. that the following ORDER, made by the Town Council. under the Town's Police Clauses Act. 1847, will be put in force on and after.the Ist day of March next. No Cart, Timber Carriage, Float, Lurry, Dray, Waggon, Hearse or Mourning Coach (except as to such hearse or mourning coach in case of a funeral from a house in the streets hereinafter mentioned) Locomotive, Traction Engine, or Threshing Machine, shall stand in or pass along that part of Bridge-st,lying between Friars'-gate and Market-gate,or in or along that part of Horsemarket-street, lying between Lyme- street, and Market-gate. or in or along that part of Sankey- street. lying between Bold-street, and Market-gate or in or along that partiof Buttermarket-streetlying between Scotland road 4 and Market-gate, between the hours of Ten in ths fore- noon and Five in the afternoon, except for the purpose of receipt or delivery of goods in any such street. An person in charge of any cartbr other vehicle above des- cribed who shall wilfully commit a breach of the above order or who shall not forthwith depart from any of the streets named in the Order when required by any constable of the borough, will be liable to a penalty not exceeding 40s. J. LYON WHITTLE. Town C ouncil</p>
        <p>COUNTY OF CHESTER. WILD BIRDS PROTECTION ACTS. 1880 AND 1894. NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN that the Right Honor- able Sir Matthew White Ridley, Baronet, Her Majesty's Principal Secretary cf State for the Home Department, has in pursuance of the powers conferred on him by the above Acts, and upon the application of the County Council of this County, made an Order ap- plicable to the whole of the County of Cheater, provid- ing (inter alia) as follows: 1. The time during which the killing and taking of wild birds or any of them is prohibited by the Act of 1880 shall be extended in the County ' of Chester so as to be from the Ist day of March to the 12th day of August in each year. 2. The Act of 1880 as extended by Section 1 of this Order shall apply within the whole of the County of Chester to the Kestrel and Heron as if those species were included in the Schedule of that Act. 3. The taking or destroying of the eggs of the Owl, the Bittern, the Kingfisher, the Kestrel, the Heron. the Shelldrake, the Dunlin, the Black- headed Gull, the Common Tern, and the Oyster Cateher shall be prohibited in the whole of the County of Chester. PENALTDIS : The Wild Birds Protection Act of 1880 provides that any person who between the Ist day of March and the Ist day of August (EXTENDED BY THE ABOVE ORDER to 12th AUGUS r) in any year shall knowingly and wilfully shoot, or attempt to shoot, or shall use any boat for the purpose of shooting or causing to be shot, any Wild Bird named in the Schedule to the Act, includ- ing the Kestrel and Heron, or shall use any lime, trap, snare, net, or other instrument for the purpose of taking any such Wild Bird, or shall expose or offer for sale or shall have in his control or possession after the 15th day of March, any such Wild Bird recently killed or taken.shall on conviction of any such offence before any two Justices of the Peace forfeit and pay for every such Bird in respect of which an offence has been committed, a sum not exceeding One Pound. The Wild Birds Protection Act of 1894 provides that any person who takes or destroys, or incites any other person to take or destroy the eggs of any Wild Birds named in paragraph 3of the before-mentioned Order, shall on conviction before any two Justices of the Peace forfeit and pay for every egg so taken or destroyed a sum not exceeding One Pound. Dated this 3rd day of February, 1896. REGINALD POTTS, Clerk of the Cheshire County Council. Chester.</p>
